"performed_via_github_app":{"id":56611,"client_id":"Iv1.4a97e692dda8878b","slug":"codacy-production","node_id":"MDM6QXBwNTY2MTE=","owner":{"login":"codacy","id":1834093,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjE4MzQwOTM=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1834093?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy","html_url":"https://github.com/codacy","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codacy/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"Codacy Production","description":"We need these permissions to access your repositories in order to provide you with the best experience possible. We will be setting up commit hooks to analyze every commit and pull request faster. Don’t worry, we don’t modify any of your code!\r\n\r\n[Learn more about the required permissions](https://docs.codacy.com/getting-started/which-permissions-does-codacy-need-from-my-account/#github-cloud)","external_url":"https://www.codacy.com","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/codacy-production","created_at":"2020-03-06T11:23:10Z","updated_at":"2026-04-16T09:41:21Z","permissions":{"checks":"write","contents":"read","emails":"read","issues":"write","members":"read","merge_queues":"read","metadata":"read","organization_custom_properties":"read","organization_hooks":"write","pull_requests":"write","repository_custom_properties":"read","repository_hooks":"write","statuses":"write"},"events":["custom_property","custom_property_values","merge_group","organization","repository"]}}],"source_review_comments":[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3136789044","pull_request_review_id":4169449285,"id":3136789044,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s6695o0","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,744 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.0** (current): Initial PRD. Establishes a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Defers manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (writes bronze data from an external source), `silver` (normalizes bronze into cross-source semantic tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system and writes it to a per-install bronze scope in the data store. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the plugin is a container that produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that transforms bronze tables (possibly from multiple connectors) into silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference, but any container that reads declared inputs and writes declared outputs is allowed. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. Widgets declare their data contract as a table schema; the host application fetches data and passes it in via a stable interface. |\n+| Bronze data | Raw source data preserved verbatim from the external system. Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Controls which plugins the instance's tenants may install (through a mirror of the central catalog, an allowlist, or both), approves dependency resolutions, and intervenes when a plugin misbehaves. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block, force-uninstall, or pin versions; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the admin for approval before any change is applied. The admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on the admin's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Emits Bronze to a Per-Install Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** emit its output into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze data is the durable record of what the source system returned. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Transforms Bronze Into Unified Outputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** transform one or more bronze inputs (from connector plugins it depends on) into silver output tables with declared schemas. The plugin **MUST** declare its input bronze tables (as `(plugin_id, plugin_version, table_name)` references) and its output silver tables (schema + columns) in its manifest.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `bronze* → silver*`. Declaring both sides explicitly is what allows the dependency resolver to verify compatibility and the runtime to detect contract violations.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing RLS, or holding long-lived DB credentials. The widget becomes a pure rendering function over (data, config), which is the minimum-privilege model.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A plugin with `silver` capability that depends on a connector plugin cannot be installed if its declared dependency is unmet; the admin sees the unmet dependency in the resolver output\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","original_commit_id":"48ee78ba54c3dddeba01a8f6217e783c70637ae8","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Acceptance criterion conflicts with the FR priority/strength.**\n\nLine 691 makes dependency-graph UI effectively mandatory, but `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` is currently `p2` and `SHOULD` (Lines 482-485). Align one side so release gating is unambiguous.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md` at line 691, The acceptance criterion\ncurrently makes the dependency-graph UI mandatory (checked box) but the feature\nrequirement cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui is defined as p2/SHOULD; resolve the\nconflict by either (A) changing the acceptance item \"- [ ] An instance admin can\nview the dependency graph...\" to remain optional/SHOULD (remove implication of\nrelease gating) or (B) elevating cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui to p1/MUST so the\nacceptance becomes a hard requirement; update the wording and the\npriority/strength value for cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui to match whichever option\nyou choose so both the acceptance criterion and the FR are aligned.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:hawk:a879b324-9f38-4ba0-8572-9bb4e7787f67 -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit b6a1639","created_at":"2026-04-24T09:35:23Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:45Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3136789044","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3136789044"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3136789044"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3136789044/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":691,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":691,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3136789048","pull_request_review_id":4169449285,"id":3136789048,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s6695o4","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,744 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.0** (current): Initial PRD. Establishes a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Defers manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (writes bronze data from an external source), `silver` (normalizes bronze into cross-source semantic tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system and writes it to a per-install bronze scope in the data store. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the plugin is a container that produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that transforms bronze tables (possibly from multiple connectors) into silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference, but any container that reads declared inputs and writes declared outputs is allowed. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. Widgets declare their data contract as a table schema; the host application fetches data and passes it in via a stable interface. |\n+| Bronze data | Raw source data preserved verbatim from the external system. Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Controls which plugins the instance's tenants may install (through a mirror of the central catalog, an allowlist, or both), approves dependency resolutions, and intervenes when a plugin misbehaves. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block, force-uninstall, or pin versions; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the admin for approval before any change is applied. The admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on the admin's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Emits Bronze to a Per-Install Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** emit its output into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze data is the durable record of what the source system returned. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Transforms Bronze Into Unified Outputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** transform one or more bronze inputs (from connector plugins it depends on) into silver output tables with declared schemas. The plugin **MUST** declare its input bronze tables (as `(plugin_id, plugin_version, table_name)` references) and its output silver tables (schema + columns) in its manifest.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `bronze* → silver*`. Declaring both sides explicitly is what allows the dependency resolver to verify compatibility and the runtime to detect contract violations.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing RLS, or holding long-lived DB credentials. The widget becomes a pure rendering function over (data, config), which is the minimum-privilege model.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A plugin with `silver` capability that depends on a connector plugin cannot be installed if its declared dependency is unmet; the admin sees the unmet dependency in the resolver output\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables carry `tenant_id` and cross-tenant reads are rejected by the data-store enforcement layer\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin artifacts. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs | p1 |\n+| Instance database | Stores the installed-plugin inventory, config, and audit log | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, frontend widget registration) | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for widget capability; `widget` FRs ship when this is available | p2 |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| External secret manager (optional) | Provides plugin credentials when configured; manual-entry fallback otherwise | p2 |\n+| Central catalog service | Curates and serves public plugin metadata | p2 (URL install works without it) |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Central-catalog entries run compatibility checks before listing; URL-install carries an explicit \"untrusted source\" warning in the admin UI; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Dependency graph explodes into conflict states admins cannot untangle | Plugins become effectively impossible to upgrade | No-legacy-mode plus explicit resolution forces the conflict to the surface; dependency-graph UI gives admins visibility; unused-dependency reporting keeps the graph from accumulating cruft |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Silver consumers ship their own data-quality tests against declared input contracts; plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin runtime crash leaves an install in a half-applied state | Manual recovery required | Every install/upgrade plan is persisted before application; runtime is reconciliation-based, so restart resumes from persisted state |\n+| Widget plugin escapes its sandbox and accesses tenant data it should not | Tenant data leakage | Widget does not query the data store directly; host app resolves the query and passes only the narrowed result; Kubernetes network policies restrict plugin pods to their declared egress |\n+| Third-party plugin becomes unmaintained and no one upgrades it past a breaking platform version | Tenants stuck, cannot upgrade platform | Central catalog marks maintenance status; instance admins can block unmaintained plugins; customers relying on third-party plugins are recommended to fork-on-need |\n+| Cost / resource explosion from tenants installing many heavy connectors | Cluster resource exhaustion | Plugin installs inherit per-tenant resource quotas enforced at the Kubernetes level; instance admins can cap plugins per tenant |\n+| Bronze data retention and delete flows conflict with GDPR / compliance requirements | Regulatory exposure | Bronze-is-sacred is a plugin-system guarantee; GDPR erasure is handled by a platform-level data-lifecycle tool that can drop a tenant's bronze scope on lawful request — not part of this PRD |\n+\n+## 13. Open Questions\n+\n+| Question | Owner | Target Resolution |\n+|----------|-------|-------------------|\n+| Plugin manifest schema — exact YAML shape, required vs optional fields, how capabilities are expressed, extension points for future capability kinds | Backend tech lead + Plugin Author lead | DESIGN / first ADR |\n+| Native Insight Connector Protocol — if and when we define a protocol beyond Airbyte wrapping; stdin/stdout JSONL vs gRPC vs OpenAPI endpoints; is the protocol necessary in v1 or is \"container writes to its declared scope\" enough | Ingestion tech lead | DESIGN / second ADR, post-v1 if possible |\n+| Per-tenant isolation mechanism — single table with `tenant_id` column and RLS policies, vs schema-per-install — which better suits on-prem distribution; current platform uses the former at the table level | Data tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Installed-inventory data model — exact tables, status state machine, audit log shape, relationship between plugin install and its per-install bronze scope | Backend tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Integration testing strategy — how does a plugin author test their plugin against a real customer data shape when the customer's source system is behind a firewall that Insight cannot reach; options include recorded/anonymized request traces shipped to the author, an opt-in customer-side trace-collection service operated by the Insight team, mock servers per connector type, or a combination | Plugin Author lead + Customer Success | DESIGN + customer-engagement policy; track separately from the data-model DESIGN |\n+| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish` — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"48ee78ba54c3dddeba01a8f6217e783c70637ae8","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Broken markdown table row due to unescaped pipe characters.**\n\nThe inline CLI example uses `|` and splits the row into extra cells, breaking table rendering.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish` — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |\n+| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init\\|validate\\|test\\|package\\|publish` — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |\n```\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_start -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Committable suggestion</summary>\n\n> ‼️ **IMPORTANT**\n> Carefully review the code before committing. Ensure that it accurately replaces the highlighted code, contains no missing lines, and has no issues with indentation. Thoroughly test & benchmark the code to ensure it meets the requirements.\n\n```suggestion\n| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init\\|validate\\|test\\|package\\|publish` — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_end -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.22.1)</summary>\n\n[warning] 739-739: Table column count\nExpected: 3; Actual: 7; Too many cells, extra data will be missing\n\n(MD056, table-column-count)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md` at line 739, The table row containing the\nCLI example \"insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish\" is breaking the\nMarkdown table because the pipe characters are unescaped; update that cell by\nescaping each pipe (e.g., replace | with \\|) or by using an HTML entity (&#124;)\nor wrapping the entire CLI string in an inline <code> tag so the pipes are\ntreated as literal characters; target the table row starting with\n\"Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI\" and the CLI string \"insight-plugin\ninit|validate|test|package|publish\" when making the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:hawk:a879b324-9f38-4ba0-8572-9bb4e7787f67 -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit b6a1639","created_at":"2026-04-24T09:35:23Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:46Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3136789048","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3136789048"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3136789048"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3136789048/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":739,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":739,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3137184676","pull_request_review_id":4169878048,"id":3137184676,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s66_aOk","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🔴 Critical_\n\n**Contradiction with FR `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.**\n\nLine 708 states the silver plugin \"declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version,\" but this directly contradicts FR `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract` (lines 387-395), which explicitly requires that silver plugins \"**MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins\" and instead match on schema-based input contracts.\n\nThis precondition should describe the relationship as schema-based discovery, not plugin-level dependency.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md` at line 708, Update the Preconditions\nsentence that currently says the silver plugin \"declares a dependency on the\nconnector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version\" to describe\nschema-based discovery instead: remove any wording about plugin-level\ndependencies and state that the silver plugin discovers connector outputs by\nmatching against the connector's published bronze-table input contract (schema +\nmajor-version compatibility) as required by FR\ncpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract; ensure the revised text explicitly says\nsilver plugins match on schema-based input contracts (not specific connector\nplugins) and, if needed, reference major-version compatibility of the schema\nrather than a plugin-level dependency.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma:f7c9076d-7531-4f05-a5db-32ad9c0b5fc2 -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit b6a1639","created_at":"2026-04-24T10:49:21Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:47Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3137184676","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3137184676"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3137184676"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3137184676/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":708,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":708,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3137997717","pull_request_review_id":4170790320,"id":3137997717,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67CguV","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Rollback is not defined anywhere in this document.","created_at":"2026-04-24T13:28:53Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T13:28:53Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3137997717","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3137997717"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3137997717"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3137997717/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":700,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":700,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138609810","pull_request_review_id":4171532813,"id":3138609810,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67E2KS","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Good catch — rollback was genuinely undefined. Addressed in v1.2 (just pushed: b6a1639), but not by adding a rollback operation. Instead:\n\n**Shadow-deploy replaces rollback.** New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`: an upgrade installs the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependencies) *alongside* the existing install, not in place. Both stacks run against the same inputs for a tenant-configurable trial window (default 7 days). The live stack continues to feed widgets / analytics-api — users see no change until promotion. The admin UI exposes a comparison view (row counts, schema diffs, per-column stats) so the tenant admin inspects the new version on real data before committing.\n\nAt the end of the trial, the tenant admin either:\n- **PROMOTES** → runtime uninstalls the old version and any transitive dependencies that became unused; the shadow stack becomes live; old bronze is retained (sacred per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n- **REJECTS** → runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream relied on it; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack.\n\nTrial expiry defaults to REJECT — nothing silently promotes itself.\n\n**There's no \"rollback\" API at all.** Reverting a previously-promoted upgrade is itself a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior version (still in the catalog): same trial, same compare, same promote/reject flow. One primitive for both directions.\n\nWhy this over in-place + rollback: the rollback path of an in-place upgrade requires transactional snapshot/restore across connector + silver + widget, which is expensive to implement and almost never exercised in practice until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk — the new version is proven on real data before anything flips for users — and composes naturally with `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version` (two versions already allowed to coexist) and the bronze-sacred guarantee (both stacks write to their own scopes).\n\nUC-003 was rewritten to this model; the \"retry or rollback\" phrasing you reviewed is gone. Acceptance criteria now gate on shadow-deploy behavior including the default-to-reject on trial expiry and the no-rollback-API invariant. Changelog v1.2 calls out your review as the trigger.","created_at":"2026-04-24T15:12:20Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T15:12:20Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138609810","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138609810"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138609810"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138609810/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":700,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3137997717,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":700,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138618917","pull_request_review_id":4171543024,"id":3138618917,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67E4Yl","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"With multi-version, that's potentially hundreds of distinct bronze + silver scopes in ClickHouse. There's no analysis of whether ClickHouse handles this efficiently, no storage cost model, no discussion of scope cleanup.\n","created_at":"2026-04-24T15:13:58Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T15:13:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138618917","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138618917"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138618917"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138618917/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":729,"original_line":561,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":561,"position":729,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138627633","pull_request_review_id":4171552482,"id":3138627633,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67E6gx","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,834 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.2** (current): Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Upgrade with Trial Period\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`\n+\n+When a tenant admin approves an upgrade plan, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependency versions) as a SHADOW STACK alongside the existing install rather than replacing it in place. Both stacks **MUST** run concurrently for a tenant-configurable TRIAL PERIOD (default: 7 days; minimum: one natural sync period or 24 hours, whichever is shorter). During the trial the plugin system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Keep the existing live stack as the authoritative data producer for user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api) — no user observes the upgrade until it is promoted.\n+- Run the shadow stack against the same inputs, writing to its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<shadow_install_id>`, `silver_<shadow_install_id>`, and so on for any capability the plugin introduces).\n+- Expose a comparison view in the admin UI with row counts, schema diffs, and — where shapes match between old and new — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change before committing to it.\n+\n+At the end of the trial the tenant admin either PROMOTES the shadow stack (the runtime uninstalls the old version plus any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and the old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) or REJECTS it (the runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded because no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). If the trial period expires without explicit action, the runtime **MUST** default to REJECT — plugins **MUST NOT** silently promote themselves.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a live plugin to a prior version is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older version (still published in the catalog under `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`): the same trial, comparison, and promote/reject loop applies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In-place upgrades that roll back on failure require the system to snapshot live state and restore it transactionally across connector + silver + widget — expensive to implement, hard to test, and the rollback path is almost never exercised until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk: the new version proves itself on real data before anything flips for users. It naturally composes with multi-version coexistence (`cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`) and the bronze-is-sacred guarantee (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) — during the trial both stacks write to their own scopes without interfering. It also replaces the vague \"rollback\" concept with a symmetric, well-understood primitive.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**PRD currently includes DESIGN-level implementation and schema detail.**\n\nThis PRD repeatedly specifies how-level mechanics (field-level schema constructs, dbt project structure, runtime `sources.yml` injection, engine-specific cross-project restrictions). These should be moved to DESIGN/ADR docs and referenced from the PRD to keep this artifact at product-intent level. Based on learnings: ARCH-PRD-NO-001 / DATA-PRD-NO-001 require PRDs to avoid technical implementation details and data schema definitions.  \n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 414-415, 454-457, 478-478\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md` around lines 362 - 363, The PRD includes\nimplementation-level schema and runtime mechanics (e.g., the paragraph starting\n\"A plugin that declares the `connector` capability MUST declare its output as a\nschema...\" and similar text at the other flagged locations) which should be\nremoved from the PRD and relocated to DESIGN/ADR docs; update the PRD to state\nonly the product intent (e.g., \"connectors must declare their output schema and\nguarantee conformity\") and replace the detailed how-to (field-level schema, dbt\nstructure, sources.yml injection, engine cross-project rules) with a short\nreference pointer to the new DESIGN/ADR documents (create or link ADRs named\nlike \"connector-schema-design\" and \"connector-runtime-behavior\") so the PRD\nremains high-level and implementation details live in design docs.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:hawk:4e52def0-914c-4a76-854b-e8d6913e1f4c -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commits e7dcd82 to e2f1cd7","created_at":"2026-04-24T15:15:38Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138627633","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138627633"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138627633"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138627633/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":387,"original_start_line":362,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":388,"original_line":363,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":363,"position":388,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138627642","pull_request_review_id":4171552482,"id":3138627642,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67E6g6","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,834 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.2** (current): Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Upgrade with Trial Period\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`\n+\n+When a tenant admin approves an upgrade plan, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependency versions) as a SHADOW STACK alongside the existing install rather than replacing it in place. Both stacks **MUST** run concurrently for a tenant-configurable TRIAL PERIOD (default: 7 days; minimum: one natural sync period or 24 hours, whichever is shorter). During the trial the plugin system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Keep the existing live stack as the authoritative data producer for user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api) — no user observes the upgrade until it is promoted.\n+- Run the shadow stack against the same inputs, writing to its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<shadow_install_id>`, `silver_<shadow_install_id>`, and so on for any capability the plugin introduces).\n+- Expose a comparison view in the admin UI with row counts, schema diffs, and — where shapes match between old and new — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change before committing to it.\n+\n+At the end of the trial the tenant admin either PROMOTES the shadow stack (the runtime uninstalls the old version plus any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and the old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) or REJECTS it (the runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded because no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). If the trial period expires without explicit action, the runtime **MUST** default to REJECT — plugins **MUST NOT** silently promote themselves.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a live plugin to a prior version is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older version (still published in the catalog under `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`): the same trial, comparison, and promote/reject loop applies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In-place upgrades that roll back on failure require the system to snapshot live state and restore it transactionally across connector + silver + widget — expensive to implement, hard to test, and the rollback path is almost never exercised until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk: the new version proves itself on real data before anything flips for users. It naturally composes with multi-version coexistence (`cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`) and the bronze-is-sacred guarantee (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) — during the trial both stacks write to their own scopes without interfering. It also replaces the vague \"rollback\" concept with a symmetric, well-understood primitive.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **MUST** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **MUST** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\". For tenant admins during a shadow-deploy trial, seeing the live stack and the shadow stack side-by-side is part of the core upgrade workflow (`cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`), not a nice-to-have.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive **shadow-deploy** plan (this plugin + dependencies) — the new versions are installed ALONGSIDE the existing ones, not in place of them (per `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`)\n+3. UI shows the plan — which plugins will install as shadow versions, the tenant-configurable trial window (default 7 days), and the side-by-side comparison surfaces that will become available once data starts flowing\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime installs the shadow stack in parallel with the existing live stack; both stacks write to their own per-install scopes and run on their own schedules\n+5. During the trial window, the live stack continues to feed user-facing surfaces; the shadow stack processes the same inputs into its own scopes; the admin UI exposes a comparison view (row counts, schema diffs, per-column statistics where shapes match)\n+6. The admin PROMOTES the shadow stack (explicitly, or automatically after the trial window IF the tenant opted in to auto-promote) — the runtime uninstalls the old version and any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+7. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the promoted stack","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","original_commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Inconsistent trial-expiry behavior between UC and FR/acceptance.**\n\nLine 729 allows auto-promote on expiry (if opted in), but Line 319 and Line 775 require expiry to default to **REJECT** with no auto-promotion. Please align UC-003 with the normative FR/acceptance behavior so release intent is unambiguous.  \n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 318-319, 775-775\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md` around lines 726 - 730, UC-003's\ntrial-expiry handling is inconsistent with the FR/acceptance text: change the\nbehavior described at the UI/flow steps (currently allowing auto-promote on\nexpiry at the step mentioning auto-promote) to match the normative behaviour\nthat expiry defaults to REJECT with no auto-promotion unless explicitly\nopted-in; update the three places referenced (the UC-003 section and the lines\naround 318-319 and 775) so they all state \"expiry defaults to REJECT;\nauto-promote only if tenant explicitly opted-in and recorded\" and ensure the\npromotion/uninstall wording (including the cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved\nretention note) reflects this unified policy.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:hawk:4e52def0-914c-4a76-854b-e8d6913e1f4c -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit e7dcd82","created_at":"2026-04-24T15:15:38Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:50Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138627642","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138627642"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138627642"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138627642/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":726,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":730,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":730,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138697214","pull_request_review_id":4171628319,"id":3138697214,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67FLf-","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the tenant is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; installing a second connector that produces the expected shape makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf through the admin API; force-uninstall is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables carry `tenant_id` and cross-tenant reads are rejected by the data-store enforcement layer\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin artifacts. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs | p1 |\n+| Instance database | Stores the installed-plugin inventory, config, and audit log | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, frontend widget registration) | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for widget capability; `widget` FRs ship when this is available | p2 |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| External secret manager (optional) | Provides plugin credentials when configured; manual-entry fallback otherwise | p2 |\n+| Central catalog service | Curates and serves public plugin metadata | p2 (URL install works without it) |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Central-catalog entries run compatibility checks before listing; URL-install carries an explicit \"untrusted source\" warning in the admin UI; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Dependency graph explodes into conflict states admins cannot untangle | Plugins become effectively impossible to upgrade | No-legacy-mode plus explicit resolution forces the conflict to the surface; dependency-graph UI gives admins visibility; unused-dependency reporting keeps the graph from accumulating cruft |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"With 50 plugins per tenant, this becomes version-hell. When the resolver says \"plugin A v2 needs plugin B >= 3.0 but B is pinned at 2.x,\" the admin's only options are: cascade-upgrade B (potentially triggering more conflicts) or abandon the install.\n\nAnd you respond with 'visibility', it is not solution.","created_at":"2026-04-24T15:28:44Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T15:28:44Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138697214","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138697214"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138697214"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138697214/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":767,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":767,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138710603","pull_request_review_id":4171643048,"id":3138710603,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67FOxL","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"20+ FRs are marked p1. When everything is p1, nothing is :)","created_at":"2026-04-24T15:31:26Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T15:31:26Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138710603","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138710603"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138710603"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138710603/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":517,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":517,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138908137","pull_request_review_id":4171860134,"id":3138908137,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67F-_p","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Example: I have 3 bronze connectors: gitlab, github, bitbucket. And a plugin for gathering all of them into silver layer.\n\nI add one more connector: gitea. \n\nHow the silver layer will be changed? Who and how will fix data in the silver layer? Who and how tell the silver DBT plugin about gitea bronze level?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:04:09Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:04:09Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138908137","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138908137"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138908137"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138908137/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":105,"original_line":91,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":91,"position":105,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138914697","pull_request_review_id":4171867529,"id":3138914697,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GAmJ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I think you need a reference in Insight catalog here. It is not clear what is it.","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:05:17Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:05:17Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138914697","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138914697"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138914697"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138914697/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":100,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":100,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138919537","pull_request_review_id":4171872928,"id":3138919537,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GBxx","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Plugin report or something check it? Or this should be in DESIGN... ","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:06:10Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:06:10Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138919537","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138919537"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138919537"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138919537/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":105,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":105,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138937325","pull_request_review_id":4171893257,"id":3138937325,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GGHt","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Execution protocol can not be Airbyte AND future native Insight protocol OR any other protocol the author wants. You need to define it here somehow, because you need to understand is it stdout messages, raw DB access or writing to kafka. \n\n\"Running container produces declared bronze output\" -> how it can do it without any specified protocol? Magic?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:09:41Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:09:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138937325","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138937325"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138937325"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138937325/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":134,"original_line":119,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":119,"position":134,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138946863","pull_request_review_id":4171904140,"id":3138946863,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GIcv","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Magic again. \n\nYou have com.cyberfabric.gitlab-0.0.1 and com.cyberfabric.gitlab-0.0.2, both active (because second should be tested before actual switch). Who and how decide which table to use?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:11:01Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:11:01Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138946863","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138946863"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138946863"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138946863/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":135,"original_line":120,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":120,"position":135,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138957737","pull_request_review_id":4171917129,"id":3138957737,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GLGp","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"tables? Which airbyte protocol mentioned in line 119 produce tables?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:12:59Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:12:59Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138957737","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138957737"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3138957737"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3138957737/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":142,"original_line":124,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":124,"position":142,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139027880","pull_request_review_id":4171997032,"id":3139027880,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GcOo","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"k8s ?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:26:46Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:26:46Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139027880","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139027880"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139027880"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139027880/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":178,"original_line":160,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":160,"position":178,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139060603","pull_request_review_id":4172031535,"id":3139060603,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GkN7","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"What about tenant isolation? How the plugin will not write to other tenant silver layer or access another tenant data?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:33:21Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:33:21Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139060603","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139060603"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139060603"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139060603/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":193,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":193,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139070820","pull_request_review_id":4172042082,"id":3139070820,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67Gmtk","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"What happens if i set plugin OCI artifact to `nginx` from dockerhub?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:35:14Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:35:14Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139070820","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139070820"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139070820"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139070820/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":244,"original_line":225,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":225,"position":244,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139078019","pull_request_review_id":4172050206,"id":3139078019,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GoeD","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Who can guarantee it? I can publish artifact `com.cyberfabric.gitea` in CF OCI and somebody can do the same publishing with same version and name in dockerhub and install it. We will receive reject for our plugin.","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:36:39Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:37:23Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139078019","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139078019"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139078019"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139078019/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":254,"original_line":235,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":235,"position":254,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139084515","pull_request_review_id":4172057397,"id":3139084515,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GqDj","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"How to guarantee that author will increase version number in the way you want?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:38:05Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:38:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139084515","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139084515"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139084515"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139084515/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":256,"original_line":237,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":237,"position":256,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139091577","pull_request_review_id":4172065068,"id":3139091577,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67Grx5","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I think it is BIG security hole. You run plugin in your own cluster, but you do not control what is inside. It can be scanners, miners, some vpn's to allow ssh access inside your cluster.... ","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:39:39Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:39:39Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139091577","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139091577"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139091577"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139091577/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":264,"original_line":245,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":245,"position":264,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139102045","pull_request_review_id":4172076365,"id":3139102045,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GuVd","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The PRD defers \"catalog governance\" to a policy doc (line 814) and stronger sandboxing to a \"follow-up PRD.\" But this isn't a follow-up concern — it's a prerequisite for the stated goal of third-party plugins. You don't open a plugin marketplace and then figure out sandboxing later.","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:41:58Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:41:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139102045","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139102045"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139102045"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139102045/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":264,"original_line":245,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139091577,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":245,"position":264,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139121955","pull_request_review_id":4172096743,"id":3139121955,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67GzMj","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"How you can control third-party OCI? You have plugin \"com.my.data\" v0.0.1 docker tag \"sometag\".\nSomebody change the image in that container storage and set same version and tag. \n\nAfter a while you reload this image. So?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:46:06Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:46:06Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139121955","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139121955"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139121955"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139121955/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":297,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":297,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139130997","pull_request_review_id":4172106458,"id":3139130997,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67G1Z1","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"What happens if the scheme is not conforms by error or with malicious intent? ","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:48:09Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:48:09Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139130997","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139130997"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139130997"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139130997/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":387,"original_line":339,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":339,"position":387,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139136045","pull_request_review_id":4172111508,"id":3139136045,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67G2ot","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"It will have DB connection? Who will control that it do `INSERT` with proper parameters but not `SHOW DATABASES` or `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db` ?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:49:11Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:49:12Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139136045","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139136045"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139136045"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139136045/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":397,"original_line":349,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":349,"position":397,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139146310","pull_request_review_id":4172122692,"id":3139146310,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67G5JG","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Migration mech is not clear. It should be not `in-place` as we discussed, so how? `INSERT FROM SELECT`? What about speed of such data copy?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:51:08Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:51:08Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139146310","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139146310"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139146310"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139146310/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":407,"original_line":359,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":359,"position":407,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139157190","pull_request_review_id":4172134000,"id":3139157190,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67G7zG","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"You have no protocol now. You install connector 'com.my.connector' supporting Airbyte protocol.\nNext you publish protocol. Who and how will update 'com.my.connector' ? Author do not want to do it, he quit programming and went to the goose farm.","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:53:23Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:53:23Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139157190","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139157190"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139157190"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139157190/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":417,"original_line":369,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":369,"position":417,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139171216","pull_request_review_id":4172148450,"id":3139171216,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67G_OQ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Again, plugin has access to DB internals and see all tables. \n\n1. It can grab anything it want and send outside. \n2. It need to detect bronze tables... how? you have predefined gitlab_table, bitbucket_table and third-party plugin with LOOK_THIS_IS_GITEA_NOW_HOPE_WORKS table. Any ideas how to locate such source table? :)\n","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:56:05Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:56:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139171216","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139171216"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139171216"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139171216/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":439,"original_line":391,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":391,"position":439,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139177390","pull_request_review_id":4172154719,"id":3139177390,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67HAuu","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Hm, how you provide clickhouse connection to those languages? You will pass credentials to db?","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:56:59Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:56:59Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139177390","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139177390"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139177390"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139177390/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":459,"original_line":411,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":411,"position":459,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139183682","pull_request_review_id":4172161560,"id":3139183682,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67HCRC","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"And also this plugin can send JWT tokens of user somewhere :)","created_at":"2026-04-24T16:58:10Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T16:58:10Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139183682","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139183682"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139183682"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139183682/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":510,"original_line":433,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":433,"position":510,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139194588","pull_request_review_id":4172173556,"id":3139194588,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67HE7c","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Again, how to control it? I can send the data with my own tenant_id instead of real one and will get an ability to view other tenants.","created_at":"2026-04-24T17:00:34Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T17:00:34Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139194588","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139194588"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139194588"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139194588/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":552,"original_line":475,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":475,"position":552,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139202004","pull_request_review_id":4172181484,"id":3139202004,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67HGvU","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Is there description of this manager?","created_at":"2026-04-24T17:02:18Z","updated_at":"2026-04-24T17:02:18Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139202004","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139202004"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3139202004"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3139202004/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":507,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":507,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564170","pull_request_review_id":4176120627,"id":3142564170,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7lK","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (just pushed: e7dcd82). The storage concern was real and is closed by two complementary FRs:\n\n1. **`cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`** — plugin authors ship migration scripts that can be expressed as `CREATE VIEW` over the prior snapshot's bronze (read-only, via the cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT in `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), as `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or as full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. View-based migrations make the storage cost during a shadow trial `~1× + delta` instead of `2×`. The author picks the cost-vs-richness tradeoff per plugin.\n\n2. **`cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`** — non-live snapshots older than a configurable threshold (default 30d) surface to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. Cleanup is explicitly admin-driven (no auto-retire).\n\nSection 12 (Risks) now includes a dedicated \"Storage cost balloons if many shadow snapshots accumulate\" entry pointing at these mitigations. Section 13 (Open Questions) tracks \"snapshot retirement policy\" as a DESIGN-level decision (per-tenant cap on concurrent snapshots? hard expiry? storage-cap-driven retire?).\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:00Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:00Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564170","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564170"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564170"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564170/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":729,"original_line":561,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138618917,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":561,"position":729,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564215","pull_request_review_id":4176120655,"id":3142564215,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7l3","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the tenant is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; installing a second connector that produces the expected shape makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf through the admin API; force-uninstall is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables carry `tenant_id` and cross-tenant reads are rejected by the data-store enforcement layer\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin artifacts. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs | p1 |\n+| Instance database | Stores the installed-plugin inventory, config, and audit log | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, frontend widget registration) | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for widget capability; `widget` FRs ship when this is available | p2 |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| External secret manager (optional) | Provides plugin credentials when configured; manual-entry fallback otherwise | p2 |\n+| Central catalog service | Curates and serves public plugin metadata | p2 (URL install works without it) |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Central-catalog entries run compatibility checks before listing; URL-install carries an explicit \"untrusted source\" warning in the admin UI; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Dependency graph explodes into conflict states admins cannot untangle | Plugins become effectively impossible to upgrade | No-legacy-mode plus explicit resolution forces the conflict to the surface; dependency-graph UI gives admins visibility; unused-dependency reporting keeps the graph from accumulating cruft |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"You're right that \"visibility\" alone is not a solution, and v1.3 (e7dcd82) addresses this fundamentally — there is no longer runtime dependency resolution to get stuck in.\n\nSection 5.2 was rewritten under a **snapshot-based versioning model** (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`). A snapshot is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot **authoring time** by the tenant curator, not at runtime install. The \"plugin A v2 needs B>=3.0 but B is pinned at 2.x\" situation is impossible at install time, because by the time the snapshot is published, every version has been chosen explicitly and tested together (in a shadow snapshot, on real data, against the live one — `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`).\n\nIf two desired versions can't coexist because of a deeper conflict, the curator publishes two separate snapshots, and the tenant picks one. There is no \"cascade-upgrade\" surprise — the tenant always sees the full pinned set in the snapshot before deploying it.\n\nUC-003 was rewritten under this flow (\"Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\"); UC-005 is new and covers tenant snapshot authoring directly.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:01Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:01Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564215","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564215"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564215"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564215/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":767,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138697214,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":767,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564247","pull_request_review_id":4176120674,"id":3142564247,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7mX","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fair point, addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82). Priority rebalance was one of the six themes called out in the changelog.\n\nWas 32 `p1`. Now **18 `p1`**, all genuinely ship-blocking foundations:\n\n- multi-tenancy: `tenant-scoped-installs`, `tenant-data-isolation`, `tenant-sovereignty`\n- versioning: `snapshot-as-state`, `snapshot-shadow-deploy`, `inventory-storage`\n- capabilities: `connector-output-contract`, `connector-bronze-scope`, `bronze-preserved`, `silver-input-contract`, `silver-transform`, `isolated-transform-project`\n- security: `per-install-db-user`, `image-signing-required`, `trust-tiers`, `baseline-isolation`\n- UX: `config-schema-ui`, `secret-provisioning`\n\nEverything else — `widget-*` capabilities, `silver-engine-flexibility`, `silver-consumer-validates`, `connector-protocol-flexibility`, `dep-graph-ui`, `status-reporting`, `migration-via-views`, all interface and contract sections, `oci-artifact`, `manifest`, `naming-versioning`, `semver-contracts` — moved to `p2` (functional features and UX polish).\n\n`p3` reserved for future improvements (`retired-snapshot-reporting` is one).\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:03Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:03Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564247","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564247"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564247"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564247/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":517,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138710603,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":517,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564271","pull_request_review_id":4176120695,"id":3142564271,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7mv","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) — this is exactly the case the schema-based discovery model is designed for.\n\n`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract` (added in v1.1, clarified in v1.3) makes silver plugins **decoupled from specific connectors by identity**. A silver plugin declares its input contract as: (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) and (b) discovery rules — typically a `tag` declared by upstream connectors in their `output.tag`.\n\nSo in your example: `silver-git` declares `input: { tag: git-activity, expected_schema: ... }`. The three connectors (gitlab/github/bitbucket) each declare `output: { tag: git-activity, schema: ... }`. When you add `gitea` to the same snapshot with the same tag, the plugin runtime's discovery refresh picks it up automatically on the next run. Silver-git does not change. The `cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT` mechanism extends this to migrations across snapshots.\n\nUC-004 alternative flow now spells this out explicitly: *\"A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot: silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag.\"*\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:04Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:04Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564271","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564271"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564271"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564271/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":105,"original_line":91,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138908137,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":91,"position":105,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564294","pull_request_review_id":4176120716,"id":3142564294,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7nG","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Clarified in v1.3 (e7dcd82). The catalog actor is `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog` — described in Section 2.2 as an Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata and reference snapshots. In v1.3 it is explicitly **optional convenience**, not governance: tenants author their own snapshots; the central catalog is a starting-point library, not a gatekeeper.\n\nIf a specific reference is still unclear after v1.3, please point to the exact line — happy to tighten the wording.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:05Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564294","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564294"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564294"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564294/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":100,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138914697,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":100,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564311","pull_request_review_id":4176120731,"id":3142564311,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7nX","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Yes, intentionally deferred to DESIGN — explicit in Section 13 (Open Questions). v1.3 added several specific DESIGN-targeted entries: snapshot manifest format, snapshot inheritance, signing-key management, image-scan policy, egress-proxy implementation. Each names the owner and the resolution target. The PRD intentionally stops at the contract-and-invariant layer; *how* invariants are enforced (e.g., schema-conformance check via proxy vs ClickHouse table-DDL mirror) is DESIGN territory.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:06Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:06Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564311","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564311"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564311"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564311/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":105,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138919537,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":105,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564326","pull_request_review_id":4176120743,"id":3142564326,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7nm","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82). The split is now explicit:\n\n- **Source-side protocol** (how the connector container *gets* data from the upstream system) is the **author's choice** — Airbyte stdout, Singer tap, gRPC, raw HTTP, scheduled CSV pulls, whatever. v1 ships with Airbyte support but the system does not require any specific source protocol (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`, now `p2` as a relaxation statement).\n\n- **Sink-side protocol** (how the connector container *writes* its bronze) is **universal and non-negotiable**: the runtime provisions a per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to the install's bronze scope (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`, `p1`); the plugin writes via that user's credentials. There is no \"raw DB access,\" no \"Kafka writeback\" — the only legitimate write target is the install's own bronze scope.\n\nSo \"container produces bronze\" cashes out as: container has `INSERT` on `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` and writes there. Schema conformance is enforced at write (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`).\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:08Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:08Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564326","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564326"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564326"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564326/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":134,"original_line":119,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138937325,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":119,"position":134,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564349","pull_request_review_id":4176120761,"id":3142564349,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7n9","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Resolved by the snapshot model in v1.3 (e7dcd82). The \"two versions of `com.cyberfabric.gitlab` running concurrently\" case is no longer a per-plugin choice — it's a snapshot-level reality.\n\nAt any moment, the tenant has exactly **one `live` snapshot** plus zero or more `shadow` snapshots (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`). Each snapshot has its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`), its own per-install ClickHouse credentials, and its own pods — they are fully independent data planes.\n\nWidgets and analytics-api read from **only** the live snapshot. The shadow snapshot is admin-only, exposed through a side-by-side comparison view. There is no ambiguity about \"which version's data\" — by definition the live snapshot's data is the data the user sees. When the admin promotes the shadow, the *live* pointer atomically switches; the previous snapshot keeps running but no longer feeds users until either retired or itself promoted-back via a new shadow-deploy.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:09Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:09Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564349","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564349"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564349"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564349/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":135,"original_line":120,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138946863,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":120,"position":135,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564389","pull_request_review_id":4176120786,"id":3142564389,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7ol","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"See reply on L134 — bronze is ClickHouse tables in the install's per-install scope. The Airbyte source protocol is the input side (extracting from the source system); the output side is universally ClickHouse, written via the connector's per-install ClickHouse user with `INSERT` scoped to its own bronze scope. v1.3 (e7dcd82) makes this contract explicit in `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user` and `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:10Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:10Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564389","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564389"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564389"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564389/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":142,"original_line":124,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138957737,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":124,"position":142,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564427","pull_request_review_id":4176120808,"id":3142564427,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7pL","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Yes — Kubernetes is required. Section 3.1 (Operational Concept) lists it as a hard prerequisite, and v1.3 reinforces this in Section 5.9 (Security): plugin pods rely on `NetworkPolicy`, `ResourceQuota`, ServiceAccount-with-no-bindings, runtime classes (`runc` / `gVisor` / `Kata`) — none of which are available outside K8s. Section 11 (Assumptions) reiterates: \"Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\"\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:12Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:12Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564427","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564427"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564427"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564427/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":178,"original_line":160,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139027880,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":160,"position":178,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564463","pull_request_review_id":4176120828,"id":3142564463,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7pv","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user` (`p1`). Every plugin install gets a generated ClickHouse user with `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER` scoped **only** to its own bronze/silver scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`). Silver plugins additionally get `SELECT` on the discovered upstream bronze scopes — by name, never wildcards.\n\nCross-tenant or cross-install writes are physically impossible at the database layer because the GRANTs do not cover those scopes. This is database-level enforcement, not application-level filtering. The default ClickHouse user is forbidden for plugins.\n\nCombined with `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation` (NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access via default ServiceAccount) and `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`, this closes the data-plane access model.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:13Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:13Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564463","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564463"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564463"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564463/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":193,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139060603,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":193,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564495","pull_request_review_id":4176120844,"id":3142564495,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7qP","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by Section 5.9 — Security.\n\n`cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required` (`p1`): every plugin install **MUST** reference its image by `@sha256:...` digest, and the digest **MUST** be accompanied by a cryptographic signature (cosign / Sigstore). Pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature are rejected at admission.\n\n`cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers` (`p1`): if you point a plugin manifest at `nginx` and there is no signature against any configured trust root, the plugin lands in the **unsigned tier**. Unsigned-tier plugins are restricted to: Insight-curated registry allowlist only, gVisor/Kata runtime, dbt-only silver, cross-origin iframe widget render, manual tenant-admin approval for cross-snapshot SELECT, and the standard `baseline-isolation` (NetworkPolicy / ResourceQuota / no K8s API). Even if `nginx` somehow lands on the allowlist, baseline-isolation ensures it cannot exfiltrate or mutate tenant data — it simply runs as a useless container that produces no bronze.\n\nFor vendor-signed and instance-signed tiers, `nginx` cannot land at all because it lacks the trust-rooted signature.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:15Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:15Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564495","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564495"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564495"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564495/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":244,"original_line":225,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139070820,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":225,"position":244,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564519","pull_request_review_id":4176120872,"id":3142564519,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7qn","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82). Plugin identity in the trusted tiers is `(image_digest, signature)`, **not** `(name, version)`.\n\n`cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`: a plugin is in *vendor-signed* tier only if the image digest is signed by Insight's signing key. A `com.cyberfabric.gitea` artifact published to dockerhub by an unrelated party will not match Insight's signature, so it cannot enter the vendor-signed tier — it lands in the *unsigned* tier with reduced capabilities (curated registry allowlist, gVisor runtime, etc.). Same name doesn't matter; the signature does.\n\nFor instance-signed tier, the customer's own engineering team signs with the key the instance admin has registered as a trust root, so the same logic applies inside the customer's organization. Naming collisions are reduced to a UI-level disambiguation problem (\"two plugins called `com.cyberfabric.gitea` are visible to your tenant — pick one\"), not a security problem.\n\nSigstore transparency log + per-version signatures + revocation list (also in `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`) close the remaining edge cases.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:16Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:16Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564519","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564519"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564519"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564519/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":254,"original_line":235,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139078019,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":235,"position":254,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564538","pull_request_review_id":4176120891,"id":3142564538,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7q6","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Partially mitigated in v1.3 (e7dcd82); SemVer is no longer load-bearing.\n\n`cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts` is still a hygiene rule for plugin authors, but it has been demoted to `p2` because v1.3's safety doesn't depend on authors getting SemVer right:\n\n1. **Snapshot-shadow-deploy** (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`): the tenant admin tests every upgrade in a shadow snapshot on real data, against the live snapshot side-by-side, before promoting. A breaking change shipped as a minor version is caught here, regardless of what the version number claims.\n2. **`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`**: silver plugins' data-quality tests run against bronze before each transform. A connector that broke its output schema is caught at the receiver side.\n3. **`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`**: the runtime enforces the connector's declared output schema at write — non-conforming rows are rejected at the producer side.\n\nSection 12 (Risks) updated accordingly: \"SemVer hygiene is helpful but no longer load-bearing; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense.\"\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:18Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:18Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564538","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564538"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564538"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564538/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":256,"original_line":237,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139084515,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":237,"position":256,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564572","pull_request_review_id":4176120920,"id":3142564572,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7rc","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Strongly agree, and addressed comprehensively in v1.3 (e7dcd82). **New Section 5.9 — Security**, with three foundation FRs:\n\n1. **`cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`** (`p1`): cosign + Sigstore, digest pinning, transparency log, revocation list. Closes the supply-chain side.\n\n2. **`cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`** (`p1`): vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with a fixed capability matrix. Modulates what's allowed *above* the baseline, never replaces it.\n\n3. **`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`** (`p1`) — applies to **every plugin regardless of tier**:\n   - Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs (no `default` user).\n   - `NetworkPolicy` enforcing manifest-declared egress allowlist (no scanners/miners/VPN to anywhere not whitelisted).\n   - `ResourceQuota` from manifest, capped by instance-admin defaults (no resource exhaustion).\n   - Default `ServiceAccount` with **zero** K8s API permissions (no SSH-via-K8s-Exec, no listing pods, no reading secrets).\n   - Read-only root filesystem; only declared volume mounts writable.\n   - For unsigned tier specifically: `gVisor` or `Kata` runtime class for kernel-level isolation.\n\nSection 12 (Risks) now has explicit entries for \"scanners/miners/reverse VPN inside cluster\" and \"widget plugin steals user JWT\" with these mitigations cited.\n\nThe reply below to this comment is also addressed: see next thread.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:19Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:19Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564572","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564572"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564572"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564572/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":264,"original_line":245,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139091577,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":245,"position":264,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564596","pull_request_review_id":4176120938,"id":3142564596,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7r0","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Agreed — and v1.3 (e7dcd82) treats it that way. Section 5.9 is **part of the v1 PRD**, with `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`, `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`, and `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation` all marked `p1` (ship-blocking foundations, not deferred).\n\n**Catalog governance** (the policy / who-approves-what part) is still in Section 13 (Open Questions) because it's a process question, not a technical one — but the technical sandboxing has been promoted to first-class FRs. The runtime can enforce trust tiers and baseline isolation independently of who is approving plugins for a curated catalog.\n\nSo: technical sandbox → `p1` in PRD; catalog governance policy → DESIGN-level Open Question, expected to land before any third-party plugin is published.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:20Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:20Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564596","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564596"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564596"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564596/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":264,"original_line":245,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139091577,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":245,"position":264,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564612","pull_request_review_id":4176120949,"id":3142564612,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7sE","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82). `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required` (`p1`) requires images to be referenced by **digest** (`@sha256:...`), never by mutable tag. Manifests with a tag-only reference are rejected at install.\n\nOnce a plugin install records a digest, the third-party can republish *anything* under the same name and tag — the digest will not match, the signature will not verify, the runtime will refuse to run it. The install is pinned to the digest the author intended at sign time.\n\nSigstore transparency log + revocation list (same FR) close the residual case where the signing key itself is compromised: revoked digests cannot start new pods even if the install was created before revocation.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:22Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:22Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564612","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564612"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564612"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564612/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":297,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139121955,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":297,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564635","pull_request_review_id":4176120965,"id":3142564635,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7sb","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by **two FRs** working at the producer and the consumer side:\n\n1. **Producer side**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance` (new in v1.3, Section 5.9) — the runtime enforces the connector's declared output schema at write; rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types are rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. Implementation options (validating proxy vs ClickHouse DDL mirroring) deferred to DESIGN.\n\n2. **Consumer side**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` (preserved from v1.0) — silver plugins ship data-quality tests against their declared input contract; the runtime runs them before each transform and halts on failure.\n\nTogether: a connector that ships malformed rows by accident or malice fails at write rather than corrupting bronze. If somehow it gets through, the silver consumer catches it before contaminating downstream silver / gold.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:23Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:23Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564635","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564635"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564635"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564635/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":387,"original_line":339,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139130997,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":339,"position":387,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564672","pull_request_review_id":4176120985,"id":3142564672,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7tA","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user` (`p1`).\n\nEvery plugin install gets a generated ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to its capability:\n- Connector install: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` — its own bronze scope only.\n- Silver install: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered>.*` for resolved upstream bronze, plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver.\n\n`SHOW DATABASES` returns only databases the user has SELECT on. `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db` fails with `Not enough privileges`. The default user is forbidden for plugins. Quotas (max queries/min, max memory) are also configurable per user against runaway plugins.\n\nThis is enforcement at the database engine level, not at any application proxy. Section 12 (Risks) cites this explicitly under \"Plugin attempts cross-tenant data access through DB.\"\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:24Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:24Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564672","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564672"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564672"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564672/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":397,"original_line":349,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139136045,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":349,"position":397,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564691","pull_request_review_id":4176121009,"id":3142564691,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7tT","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views` — and the speed concern is the reason it offers three options:\n\n| Form | Storage cost | Latency | When |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `CREATE VIEW` over prior bronze | ~0 (no copy) | view-evaluation overhead per read | minor renames / casts / computed columns |\n| `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW` (background populated) | grows to historical size | cheap reads after materialization | needs indexes / projections |\n| `INSERT FROM SELECT` (full physical copy) | full historical | one-time copy cost; cheap after | full reshape |\n| No migration | 0 | n/a | connector re-fetches from upstream on schedule |\n\nThe plugin author picks per plugin based on their data shape. The runtime grants the new install `SELECT` on the prior install's bronze for the duration of the shadow trial only (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), then revokes on promote / reject / retire. So speed is a function of author choice, not a system-imposed worst case. View-based keeps a long-running shadow at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×`.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:25Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:25Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564691","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564691"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564691"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564691/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":407,"original_line":359,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139146310,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":359,"position":407,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564708","pull_request_review_id":4176121020,"id":3142564708,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7tk","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) primarily by digest pinning + snapshot immutability.\n\nWhen tenant admin's snapshot pins `com.my.connector@sha256:abcd...` (with a specific signature), that exact image runs forever — the author retiring to the goose farm changes nothing. There is no \"auto-update to new protocol.\" The plugin keeps working at the pinned digest until the tenant admin chooses to upgrade by initiating a new shadow-deploy.\n\nIf a future native Insight protocol replaces Airbyte source protocol, both protocols would coexist for a deprecation window (Section 13 Open Question: \"Transition off Airbyte\"). Tenants on the old protocol keep running on their pinned snapshots. Migrating off requires either (a) the original author publishing a new version, (b) a fork by another party with their own signature, or (c) accepting the plugin frozen at the prior protocol forever.\n\nSection 12 (Risks) captures this as \"Third-party plugin becomes unmaintained and no one upgrades it past a breaking platform version\" with the snapshot-pinning mitigation cited.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:27Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:27Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564708","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564708"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564708"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564708/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":417,"original_line":369,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139157190,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":369,"position":417,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564730","pull_request_review_id":4176121039,"id":3142564730,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7t6","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`. ClickHouse's `system.tables` (and `system.databases`, `system.columns`, etc.) return only entries the calling user has GRANTs on. With the per-install user scoped to `bronze_<own_install>` and (for silver) the resolved upstream bronze, the plugin literally cannot enumerate other plugins' tables — they appear empty in `system.*`.\n\nFor your discovery concern (plugin naming its table `LOOK_THIS_IS_GITEA_NOW_HOPE_WORKS`): silver discovery is **not done by the plugin scanning `system.tables`**. The plugin runtime resolves discovery rules from the silver plugin's manifest (tag/pattern/parameters) **on the system side**, then materializes a per-install `sources.yml` that lists only the resolved upstream scopes. The silver plugin's dbt project reads from `source(...)` references — concrete, runtime-injected, scoped. There is no path for a plugin to \"find\" a table that doesn't match the discovery contract its silver consumer accepts.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:28Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:28Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564730","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564730"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564730"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564730/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":439,"original_line":391,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139171216,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":391,"position":439,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564749","pull_request_review_id":4176121055,"id":3142564749,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7uN","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Yes — credentials passed via env vars, identical to how dbt is wired. v1.3 (e7dcd82) `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`: the plugin runtime provisions the per-install ClickHouse user, stores the password in a K8s `Secret`, and injects `CH_HOST` / `CH_USER` / `CH_PASSWORD` (or similar — exact names is DESIGN) into the plugin pod via env. dbt-clickhouse, Rust `clickhouse-rs`, Python `clickhouse-driver`, anything — they all read the same env. Standard 12-factor pattern.\n\nBecause the credentials are scoped (per-install GRANT only), passing them to the plugin is safe: even if the plugin were to leak the password, it would leak access only to its own scope. Rotation is part of the upgrade lifecycle — at install (and at any explicit rotation event), the runtime issues a new password, updates the Secret, and the plugin pod picks it up on next sync.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:30Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564749","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564749"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564749"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564749/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":459,"original_line":411,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139177390,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":411,"position":459,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564784","pull_request_review_id":4176121086,"id":3142564784,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7uw","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers` — widget render mode is one of the columns in the capability matrix:\n\n| Tier | Widget render | JWT access |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vendor-signed | native React component (full DOM) | through host SDK proxy (which exposes data only, not JWT directly) |\n| Instance-signed | native React component | through host SDK proxy |\n| Unsigned | **cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute** | **never** — JWT does not cross the iframe boundary |\n\nUnsigned widgets communicate with the host via `postMessage` carrying only data, not authentication context. The user JWT is held by the host and never serialized into messages.\n\nFor vendor- and instance-signed tiers, the host SDK is the data path — it queries on behalf of the widget and passes only the result; the widget code never gets a token of any kind, even though it has DOM access.\n\nSection 12 (Risks) updated with \"Widget plugin escapes its sandbox and accesses user JWT or tenant data\" → \"for unsigned plugins the widget renders in a cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` and the user JWT is never exposed across the iframe boundary.\"\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:31Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:31Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564784","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564784"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564784"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564784/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":510,"original_line":433,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139183682,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":433,"position":510,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564814","pull_request_review_id":4176121104,"id":3142564814,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7vO","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Addressed in v1.3 (e7dcd82) by `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user` (`p1`). The argument that \"plugin can lie about `tenant_id`\" assumes the plugin has the credentials to write to a table where `tenant_id` is the security boundary. In v1.3, **`tenant_id` is not the security boundary — schema/scope is**.\n\nEach plugin install has GRANTs scoped to `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` only. The install_id maps 1:1 to the tenant in inventory metadata. The plugin user has *no* credentials to write to any other install's scope, regardless of what `tenant_id` value it puts in any payload. Cross-tenant writes are impossible at the database engine.\n\n`tenant_id` may still appear in plugin-produced rows for application-level filtering ergonomics, but the trust boundary is the GRANT, not the column.\n\nThis composes with `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation` (still `p1`), which explicitly reads \"Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table MUST be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\"\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:32Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564814","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564814"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564814"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564814/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":552,"original_line":475,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139194588,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":475,"position":552,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564840","pull_request_review_id":4176121131,"id":3142564840,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T7vo","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Yes — `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning` extended in v1.3 (e7dcd82) with concrete options.\n\nSupported integrations (named explicitly in the FR text):\n- **HashiCorp Vault**\n- **External Secrets Operator** (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.)\n- **SealedSecrets** for GitOps-managed deployments\n- **Manual entry** in the admin UI for instances without an external store wired up; submitted values encrypted at rest in K8s Secret.\n\nExact integration list is a DESIGN decision (Section 13 Open Question: \"Signing-key management\" is the closest related entry, but secret-store choice is its own DESIGN call). The PRD-level contract: the manifest names a secret by reference, never by value; secrets are not logged, not in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, not in the catalog. Rotation produces a new secret version that the runtime injects on next sync.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:34Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:02:34Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564840","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564840"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142564840"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142564840/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":507,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3139202004,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":507,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577436","pull_request_review_id":4176131716,"id":3142577436,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-0c","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,744 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.0** (current): Initial PRD. Establishes a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Defers manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (writes bronze data from an external source), `silver` (normalizes bronze into cross-source semantic tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system and writes it to a per-install bronze scope in the data store. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the plugin is a container that produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that transforms bronze tables (possibly from multiple connectors) into silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference, but any container that reads declared inputs and writes declared outputs is allowed. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. Widgets declare their data contract as a table schema; the host application fetches data and passes it in via a stable interface. |\n+| Bronze data | Raw source data preserved verbatim from the external system. Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Controls which plugins the instance's tenants may install (through a mirror of the central catalog, an allowlist, or both), approves dependency resolutions, and intervenes when a plugin misbehaves. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block, force-uninstall, or pin versions; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the admin for approval before any change is applied. The admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on the admin's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Emits Bronze to a Per-Install Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** emit its output into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze data is the durable record of what the source system returned. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Transforms Bronze Into Unified Outputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** transform one or more bronze inputs (from connector plugins it depends on) into silver output tables with declared schemas. The plugin **MUST** declare its input bronze tables (as `(plugin_id, plugin_version, table_name)` references) and its output silver tables (schema + columns) in its manifest.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `bronze* → silver*`. Declaring both sides explicitly is what allows the dependency resolver to verify compatibility and the runtime to detect contract violations.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing RLS, or holding long-lived DB credentials. The widget becomes a pure rendering function over (data, config), which is the minimum-privilege model.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A plugin with `silver` capability that depends on a connector plugin cannot be installed if its declared dependency is unmet; the admin sees the unmet dependency in the resolver output\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","original_commit_id":"48ee78ba54c3dddeba01a8f6217e783c70637ae8","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Resolved in `e2f1cd7`. Two-step path: in v1.2 the FR was elevated to `p1`/`MUST` matching the original acceptance criterion; in v1.3 priority rebalance demoted it to `p2` but I left the `MUST` wording in the FR text — your concern was correct.\n\n`e2f1cd7` aligns the surface:\n\n- FR text: **MUST** → **SHOULD** (matches `p2` / functional feature, not release-gating).\n- The release-gating piece — the comparison view during a shadow-deploy trial — is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`. The dep-graph FR is the broader cross-plugin graph where a flat list view satisfies v1 operability.\n- Acceptance criterion rephrased so a list view satisfies it; graph visualization is `SHOULD`-only.\n\nBoth sides now align unambiguously.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:41Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577436","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577436"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577436"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577436/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":691,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3136789044,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":691,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577459","pull_request_review_id":4176131743,"id":3142577459,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-0z","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,744 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.0** (current): Initial PRD. Establishes a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Defers manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (writes bronze data from an external source), `silver` (normalizes bronze into cross-source semantic tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system and writes it to a per-install bronze scope in the data store. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the plugin is a container that produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that transforms bronze tables (possibly from multiple connectors) into silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference, but any container that reads declared inputs and writes declared outputs is allowed. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. Widgets declare their data contract as a table schema; the host application fetches data and passes it in via a stable interface. |\n+| Bronze data | Raw source data preserved verbatim from the external system. Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Controls which plugins the instance's tenants may install (through a mirror of the central catalog, an allowlist, or both), approves dependency resolutions, and intervenes when a plugin misbehaves. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block, force-uninstall, or pin versions; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the admin for approval before any change is applied. The admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on the admin's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Emits Bronze to a Per-Install Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** emit its output into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze data is the durable record of what the source system returned. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Transforms Bronze Into Unified Outputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** transform one or more bronze inputs (from connector plugins it depends on) into silver output tables with declared schemas. The plugin **MUST** declare its input bronze tables (as `(plugin_id, plugin_version, table_name)` references) and its output silver tables (schema + columns) in its manifest.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `bronze* → silver*`. Declaring both sides explicitly is what allows the dependency resolver to verify compatibility and the runtime to detect contract violations.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing RLS, or holding long-lived DB credentials. The widget becomes a pure rendering function over (data, config), which is the minimum-privilege model.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A plugin with `silver` capability that depends on a connector plugin cannot be installed if its declared dependency is unmet; the admin sees the unmet dependency in the resolver output\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables carry `tenant_id` and cross-tenant reads are rejected by the data-store enforcement layer\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin artifacts. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs | p1 |\n+| Instance database | Stores the installed-plugin inventory, config, and audit log | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, frontend widget registration) | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for widget capability; `widget` FRs ship when this is available | p2 |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| External secret manager (optional) | Provides plugin credentials when configured; manual-entry fallback otherwise | p2 |\n+| Central catalog service | Curates and serves public plugin metadata | p2 (URL install works without it) |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Central-catalog entries run compatibility checks before listing; URL-install carries an explicit \"untrusted source\" warning in the admin UI; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Dependency graph explodes into conflict states admins cannot untangle | Plugins become effectively impossible to upgrade | No-legacy-mode plus explicit resolution forces the conflict to the surface; dependency-graph UI gives admins visibility; unused-dependency reporting keeps the graph from accumulating cruft |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Silver consumers ship their own data-quality tests against declared input contracts; plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin runtime crash leaves an install in a half-applied state | Manual recovery required | Every install/upgrade plan is persisted before application; runtime is reconciliation-based, so restart resumes from persisted state |\n+| Widget plugin escapes its sandbox and accesses tenant data it should not | Tenant data leakage | Widget does not query the data store directly; host app resolves the query and passes only the narrowed result; Kubernetes network policies restrict plugin pods to their declared egress |\n+| Third-party plugin becomes unmaintained and no one upgrades it past a breaking platform version | Tenants stuck, cannot upgrade platform | Central catalog marks maintenance status; instance admins can block unmaintained plugins; customers relying on third-party plugins are recommended to fork-on-need |\n+| Cost / resource explosion from tenants installing many heavy connectors | Cluster resource exhaustion | Plugin installs inherit per-tenant resource quotas enforced at the Kubernetes level; instance admins can cap plugins per tenant |\n+| Bronze data retention and delete flows conflict with GDPR / compliance requirements | Regulatory exposure | Bronze-is-sacred is a plugin-system guarantee; GDPR erasure is handled by a platform-level data-lifecycle tool that can drop a tenant's bronze scope on lawful request — not part of this PRD |\n+\n+## 13. Open Questions\n+\n+| Question | Owner | Target Resolution |\n+|----------|-------|-------------------|\n+| Plugin manifest schema — exact YAML shape, required vs optional fields, how capabilities are expressed, extension points for future capability kinds | Backend tech lead + Plugin Author lead | DESIGN / first ADR |\n+| Native Insight Connector Protocol — if and when we define a protocol beyond Airbyte wrapping; stdin/stdout JSONL vs gRPC vs OpenAPI endpoints; is the protocol necessary in v1 or is \"container writes to its declared scope\" enough | Ingestion tech lead | DESIGN / second ADR, post-v1 if possible |\n+| Per-tenant isolation mechanism — single table with `tenant_id` column and RLS policies, vs schema-per-install — which better suits on-prem distribution; current platform uses the former at the table level | Data tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Installed-inventory data model — exact tables, status state machine, audit log shape, relationship between plugin install and its per-install bronze scope | Backend tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Integration testing strategy — how does a plugin author test their plugin against a real customer data shape when the customer's source system is behind a firewall that Insight cannot reach; options include recorded/anonymized request traces shipped to the author, an opt-in customer-side trace-collection service operated by the Insight team, mock servers per connector type, or a combination | Plugin Author lead + Customer Success | DESIGN + customer-engagement policy; track separately from the data-model DESIGN |\n+| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish` — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"48ee78ba54c3dddeba01a8f6217e783c70637ae8","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed in v1.2 (`8ba2c1d`) — pipes inside the inline CLI example escaped to `\\|`. The Open Questions table renders correctly now. Thanks for the catch.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:42Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:42Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577459","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577459"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577459"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577459/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":739,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3136789048,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":739,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577503","pull_request_review_id":4176131770,"id":3142577503,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-1f","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed in v1.2 (`8ba2c1d`). UC-004 preconditions rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`:\n\n> A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based.\n\nThanks for catching this — it was a leftover from v1.1 that I missed when refactoring silver to schema-based contracts.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:44Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:44Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577503","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577503"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577503"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577503/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":708,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3137184676,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":708,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577555","pull_request_review_id":4176131795,"id":3142577555,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-2T","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,834 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.2** (current): Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Upgrade with Trial Period\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`\n+\n+When a tenant admin approves an upgrade plan, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependency versions) as a SHADOW STACK alongside the existing install rather than replacing it in place. Both stacks **MUST** run concurrently for a tenant-configurable TRIAL PERIOD (default: 7 days; minimum: one natural sync period or 24 hours, whichever is shorter). During the trial the plugin system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Keep the existing live stack as the authoritative data producer for user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api) — no user observes the upgrade until it is promoted.\n+- Run the shadow stack against the same inputs, writing to its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<shadow_install_id>`, `silver_<shadow_install_id>`, and so on for any capability the plugin introduces).\n+- Expose a comparison view in the admin UI with row counts, schema diffs, and — where shapes match between old and new — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change before committing to it.\n+\n+At the end of the trial the tenant admin either PROMOTES the shadow stack (the runtime uninstalls the old version plus any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and the old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) or REJECTS it (the runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded because no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). If the trial period expires without explicit action, the runtime **MUST** default to REJECT — plugins **MUST NOT** silently promote themselves.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a live plugin to a prior version is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older version (still published in the catalog under `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`): the same trial, comparison, and promote/reject loop applies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In-place upgrades that roll back on failure require the system to snapshot live state and restore it transactionally across connector + silver + widget — expensive to implement, hard to test, and the rollback path is almost never exercised until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk: the new version proves itself on real data before anything flips for users. It naturally composes with multi-version coexistence (`cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`) and the bronze-is-sacred guarantee (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) — during the trial both stacks write to their own scopes without interfering. It also replaces the vague \"rollback\" concept with a symmetric, well-understood primitive.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Acknowledged. The PRD intentionally retains some implementation-specific language in the security and isolation FRs because the contract surface there *is* the implementation choice — saying \"plugins must be isolated\" without specifying how (NetworkPolicy, ResourceQuota, ServiceAccount) leaves the contract too soft for review. Same logic applies to `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project` (the dbt-project / `sources.yml` injection language is the contract for plugin authors, not a DESIGN choice).\n\nWhere mechanics genuinely belong in DESIGN, v1.3 explicitly defers — Section 13 (Open Questions) was extended in v1.3 with: snapshot manifest format, signing-key management, image-scan policy, egress-proxy implementation, snapshot retirement policy. Each names the owner and the resolution target.\n\nIf specific lines still feel over-specified after that scoping pass, please point them out — happy to relocate concrete items to ADRs and reference them from the PRD.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:45Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:45Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577555","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577555"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577555"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577555/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":387,"original_start_line":362,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":388,"original_line":363,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138627633,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":363,"position":388,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577585","pull_request_review_id":4176131817,"id":3142577585,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-2x","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,834 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.2** (current): Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Upgrade with Trial Period\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`\n+\n+When a tenant admin approves an upgrade plan, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependency versions) as a SHADOW STACK alongside the existing install rather than replacing it in place. Both stacks **MUST** run concurrently for a tenant-configurable TRIAL PERIOD (default: 7 days; minimum: one natural sync period or 24 hours, whichever is shorter). During the trial the plugin system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Keep the existing live stack as the authoritative data producer for user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api) — no user observes the upgrade until it is promoted.\n+- Run the shadow stack against the same inputs, writing to its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<shadow_install_id>`, `silver_<shadow_install_id>`, and so on for any capability the plugin introduces).\n+- Expose a comparison view in the admin UI with row counts, schema diffs, and — where shapes match between old and new — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change before committing to it.\n+\n+At the end of the trial the tenant admin either PROMOTES the shadow stack (the runtime uninstalls the old version plus any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and the old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) or REJECTS it (the runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded because no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). If the trial period expires without explicit action, the runtime **MUST** default to REJECT — plugins **MUST NOT** silently promote themselves.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a live plugin to a prior version is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older version (still published in the catalog under `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`): the same trial, comparison, and promote/reject loop applies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In-place upgrades that roll back on failure require the system to snapshot live state and restore it transactionally across connector + silver + widget — expensive to implement, hard to test, and the rollback path is almost never exercised until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk: the new version proves itself on real data before anything flips for users. It naturally composes with multi-version coexistence (`cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`) and the bronze-is-sacred guarantee (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) — during the trial both stacks write to their own scopes without interfering. It also replaces the vague \"rollback\" concept with a symmetric, well-understood primitive.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **MUST** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **MUST** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\". For tenant admins during a shadow-deploy trial, seeing the live stack and the shadow stack side-by-side is part of the core upgrade workflow (`cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`), not a nice-to-have.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive **shadow-deploy** plan (this plugin + dependencies) — the new versions are installed ALONGSIDE the existing ones, not in place of them (per `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`)\n+3. UI shows the plan — which plugins will install as shadow versions, the tenant-configurable trial window (default 7 days), and the side-by-side comparison surfaces that will become available once data starts flowing\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime installs the shadow stack in parallel with the existing live stack; both stacks write to their own per-install scopes and run on their own schedules\n+5. During the trial window, the live stack continues to feed user-facing surfaces; the shadow stack processes the same inputs into its own scopes; the admin UI exposes a comparison view (row counts, schema diffs, per-column statistics where shapes match)\n+6. The admin PROMOTES the shadow stack (explicitly, or automatically after the trial window IF the tenant opted in to auto-promote) — the runtime uninstalls the old version and any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+7. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the promoted stack","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","original_commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","user":{"login":"mitasovr","id":370538,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MDUzOA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/370538?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr","html_url":"https://github.com/mitasovr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mitasovr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed in v1.3 (`e7dcd82`). UC-003 fully rewritten under the snapshot-shadow-deploy model:\n\n- Trial expiry no longer allows `auto-promote` even as opt-in. The reminder is purely UX: **no auto-promote, no auto-revert**.\n- UC-003 step 6: *\"The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot.\"* No timer-driven action.\n- UC-003 alt flow: *\"Trial reminder expires without action: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts.\"*\n- Glossary entry for *Trial period*: \"Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert.\"\n\nThis aligns with `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy` (the FR formerly known as `shadow-deploy-upgrade`) and the corresponding acceptance criterion. v1.3 changelog calls this out as one of the six changes.\n","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:46Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:13:46Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577585","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577585"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577585"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577585/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":726,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":730,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138627642,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":730,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577953","pull_request_review_id":4176132267,"id":3142577953,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-8h","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.1** (current): Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@mitasovr`, the fix looks great — the revised precondition is now fully consistent with `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`, and the explicit \"no plugin-level dependency declared between them\" wording is a nice touch that makes the schema-based coupling unmistakable to any future reader. Thanks for the quick turnaround!\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n(\\_/)\n(^‿^)\n/>🎉\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: dzarlax\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 38\nFile: docs/components/connectors/task-tracking/jira/specs/PRD.md:1-601\nTimestamp: 2026-03-25T21:38:30.793Z\nLearning: In the cyberfabric/insight repository, Cypilot SDLC kit rules ARCH-PRD-NO-001 (No Technical Implementation Details) and DATA-PRD-NO-001 (No Data Schema Definitions) prohibit including field-level Bronze table schemas, column types, API field mappings, or Bronze-to-Silver transformation details inside PRD documents. PRDs capture *what* the system must do, not *how*. Schema definitions belong in DESIGN artifacts (e.g., connector-specific `.md` files like `jira.md` for Bronze table definitions). PRDs should reference these design artifacts via cross-links rather than duplicating schema content.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 0\nFile: cypilot/config/kits/sdlc/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-03-17T19:56:47.282Z\nLearning: Create PRD artifacts to document product intent: actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Fixed%3A%20silver%20plugins%20now%20discover%20connector%20outputs%20via%20schema-based%20input%20contracts%20(schema%20%2B%20major-version)%2C%20not%20plugin-level%20deps%20%E2%80%94%20prevents%20FR%20contradiction.%20Try%20%40coderabbitai%20for%20smarter%20reviews%20%E2%9C%85%F0%9F%94%8D&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Finsight%2Fpull%2F230%23discussion_r3137184676)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:05Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577953","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577953"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577953"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577953/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":708,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3137184676,"author_association":"NONE","original_position":708,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577960","pull_request_review_id":4176132272,"id":3142577960,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-8o","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,744 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.0** (current): Initial PRD. Establishes a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Defers manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (writes bronze data from an external source), `silver` (normalizes bronze into cross-source semantic tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system and writes it to a per-install bronze scope in the data store. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the plugin is a container that produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that transforms bronze tables (possibly from multiple connectors) into silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference, but any container that reads declared inputs and writes declared outputs is allowed. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. Widgets declare their data contract as a table schema; the host application fetches data and passes it in via a stable interface. |\n+| Bronze data | Raw source data preserved verbatim from the external system. Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Controls which plugins the instance's tenants may install (through a mirror of the central catalog, an allowlist, or both), approves dependency resolutions, and intervenes when a plugin misbehaves. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block, force-uninstall, or pin versions; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the admin for approval before any change is applied. The admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on the admin's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Emits Bronze to a Per-Install Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** emit its output into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze data is the durable record of what the source system returned. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Transforms Bronze Into Unified Outputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** transform one or more bronze inputs (from connector plugins it depends on) into silver output tables with declared schemas. The plugin **MUST** declare its input bronze tables (as `(plugin_id, plugin_version, table_name)` references) and its output silver tables (schema + columns) in its manifest.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `bronze* → silver*`. Declaring both sides explicitly is what allows the dependency resolver to verify compatibility and the runtime to detect contract violations.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing RLS, or holding long-lived DB credentials. The widget becomes a pure rendering function over (data, config), which is the minimum-privilege model.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A plugin with `silver` capability that depends on a connector plugin cannot be installed if its declared dependency is unmet; the admin sees the unmet dependency in the resolver output\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables carry `tenant_id` and cross-tenant reads are rejected by the data-store enforcement layer\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin artifacts. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs | p1 |\n+| Instance database | Stores the installed-plugin inventory, config, and audit log | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, frontend widget registration) | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for widget capability; `widget` FRs ship when this is available | p2 |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| External secret manager (optional) | Provides plugin credentials when configured; manual-entry fallback otherwise | p2 |\n+| Central catalog service | Curates and serves public plugin metadata | p2 (URL install works without it) |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Central-catalog entries run compatibility checks before listing; URL-install carries an explicit \"untrusted source\" warning in the admin UI; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Dependency graph explodes into conflict states admins cannot untangle | Plugins become effectively impossible to upgrade | No-legacy-mode plus explicit resolution forces the conflict to the surface; dependency-graph UI gives admins visibility; unused-dependency reporting keeps the graph from accumulating cruft |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Silver consumers ship their own data-quality tests against declared input contracts; plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin runtime crash leaves an install in a half-applied state | Manual recovery required | Every install/upgrade plan is persisted before application; runtime is reconciliation-based, so restart resumes from persisted state |\n+| Widget plugin escapes its sandbox and accesses tenant data it should not | Tenant data leakage | Widget does not query the data store directly; host app resolves the query and passes only the narrowed result; Kubernetes network policies restrict plugin pods to their declared egress |\n+| Third-party plugin becomes unmaintained and no one upgrades it past a breaking platform version | Tenants stuck, cannot upgrade platform | Central catalog marks maintenance status; instance admins can block unmaintained plugins; customers relying on third-party plugins are recommended to fork-on-need |\n+| Cost / resource explosion from tenants installing many heavy connectors | Cluster resource exhaustion | Plugin installs inherit per-tenant resource quotas enforced at the Kubernetes level; instance admins can cap plugins per tenant |\n+| Bronze data retention and delete flows conflict with GDPR / compliance requirements | Regulatory exposure | Bronze-is-sacred is a plugin-system guarantee; GDPR erasure is handled by a platform-level data-lifecycle tool that can drop a tenant's bronze scope on lawful request — not part of this PRD |\n+\n+## 13. Open Questions\n+\n+| Question | Owner | Target Resolution |\n+|----------|-------|-------------------|\n+| Plugin manifest schema — exact YAML shape, required vs optional fields, how capabilities are expressed, extension points for future capability kinds | Backend tech lead + Plugin Author lead | DESIGN / first ADR |\n+| Native Insight Connector Protocol — if and when we define a protocol beyond Airbyte wrapping; stdin/stdout JSONL vs gRPC vs OpenAPI endpoints; is the protocol necessary in v1 or is \"container writes to its declared scope\" enough | Ingestion tech lead | DESIGN / second ADR, post-v1 if possible |\n+| Per-tenant isolation mechanism — single table with `tenant_id` column and RLS policies, vs schema-per-install — which better suits on-prem distribution; current platform uses the former at the table level | Data tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Installed-inventory data model — exact tables, status state machine, audit log shape, relationship between plugin install and its per-install bronze scope | Backend tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Integration testing strategy — how does a plugin author test their plugin against a real customer data shape when the customer's source system is behind a firewall that Insight cannot reach; options include recorded/anonymized request traces shipped to the author, an opt-in customer-side trace-collection service operated by the Insight team, mock servers per connector type, or a combination | Plugin Author lead + Customer Success | DESIGN + customer-engagement policy; track separately from the data-model DESIGN |\n+| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish` — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"8ba2c1db31bb9fa988f6a21f1dc5a3de4bf00207","original_commit_id":"48ee78ba54c3dddeba01a8f6217e783c70637ae8","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@mitasovr`, thanks for the confirmation — glad the table renders correctly now! 🎉\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: dzarlax\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 38\nFile: docs/components/connectors/task-tracking/jira/specs/PRD.md:1-601\nTimestamp: 2026-03-25T21:38:30.793Z\nLearning: In the cyberfabric/insight repository, Cypilot SDLC kit rules ARCH-PRD-NO-001 (No Technical Implementation Details) and DATA-PRD-NO-001 (No Data Schema Definitions) prohibit including field-level Bronze table schemas, column types, API field mappings, or Bronze-to-Silver transformation details inside PRD documents. PRDs capture *what* the system must do, not *how*. Schema definitions belong in DESIGN artifacts (e.g., connector-specific `.md` files like `jira.md` for Bronze table definitions). PRDs should reference these design artifacts via cross-links rather than duplicating schema content.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 0\nFile: cypilot/config/kits/sdlc/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-03-17T19:56:47.282Z\nLearning: Create PRD artifacts to document product intent: actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Found%20and%20fixed%20broken%20Markdown%20table%20caused%20by%20unescaped%20'%7C'%20in%20an%20inline%20CLI%20example%20%E2%80%94%20escaped%20pipes%20to%20%5C%7C%20so%20the%20table%20renders%20correctly.%20Saved%20docs%20integrity%20%26%20cut%20CI%20noise.%20Try%20%40coderabbitai%20%F0%9F%9A%80%F0%9F%94%A7&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Finsight%2Fpull%2F230%23discussion_r3136789048)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:05Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577960","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577960"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577960"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577960/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":739,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3136789048,"author_association":"NONE","original_position":739,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577972","pull_request_review_id":4176132299,"id":3142577972,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-80","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,744 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.0** (current): Initial PRD. Establishes a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Defers manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (writes bronze data from an external source), `silver` (normalizes bronze into cross-source semantic tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system and writes it to a per-install bronze scope in the data store. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the plugin is a container that produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that transforms bronze tables (possibly from multiple connectors) into silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference, but any container that reads declared inputs and writes declared outputs is allowed. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. Widgets declare their data contract as a table schema; the host application fetches data and passes it in via a stable interface. |\n+| Bronze data | Raw source data preserved verbatim from the external system. Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Controls which plugins the instance's tenants may install (through a mirror of the central catalog, an allowlist, or both), approves dependency resolutions, and intervenes when a plugin misbehaves. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block, force-uninstall, or pin versions; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the admin for approval before any change is applied. The admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on the admin's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Emits Bronze to a Per-Install Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** emit its output into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze data is the durable record of what the source system returned. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Transforms Bronze Into Unified Outputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** transform one or more bronze inputs (from connector plugins it depends on) into silver output tables with declared schemas. The plugin **MUST** declare its input bronze tables (as `(plugin_id, plugin_version, table_name)` references) and its output silver tables (schema + columns) in its manifest.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `bronze* → silver*`. Declaring both sides explicitly is what allows the dependency resolver to verify compatibility and the runtime to detect contract violations.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing RLS, or holding long-lived DB credentials. The widget becomes a pure rendering function over (data, config), which is the minimum-privilege model.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\" when something fails.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive upgrade plan (this plugin + dependencies)\n+3. UI shows the plan; admin sees exactly which other plugins will upgrade and to what versions\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime performs each upgrade in dependency order, preserving bronze data and re-deriving silver and widget state\n+5. Inventory is updated; admin sees all participating plugins at their new versions and status reports green\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Upgraded plugins are running; bronze data is preserved across the transition.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects plan**: No changes applied. Plugin remains at the prior version. The \"upgrade available\" indicator persists.\n+- **One plugin in the chain fails mid-upgrade**: The runtime pauses the chain, reports the failure, and requires admin intervention (retry or rollback).\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed; the silver plugin declares a dependency on the connector plugin's output bronze tables at a specific major version.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to ghcr.io and installed in an Insight instance through the marketplace UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A plugin with `silver` capability that depends on a connector plugin cannot be installed if its declared dependency is unmet; the admin sees the unmet dependency in the resolver output\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two versions of the same plugin can coexist in one tenant; uninstalling the older version does not affect the newer version's bronze data\n+- [ ] Uninstalling a plugin preserves its bronze data unless the admin explicitly opts in to deletion\n+- [ ] Upgrading a plugin to a new major version requires explicit admin approval of the full transitive upgrade plan\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph of all installed plugins across all tenants, with per-node health state","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","original_commit_id":"48ee78ba54c3dddeba01a8f6217e783c70637ae8","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@mitasovr`, thanks for the detailed walkthrough — the two-step version history explains the drift perfectly. The split makes sense: `SHOULD` + list view for the broad dependency graph at `p2`, and the shadow-deploy comparison view carrying the hard `MUST` under its own FR. Both sides are now coherent.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n(\\_/)\n(^‿^)\n/>🎉\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: dzarlax\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 38\nFile: docs/components/connectors/task-tracking/jira/specs/PRD.md:1-601\nTimestamp: 2026-03-25T21:38:30.793Z\nLearning: In the cyberfabric/insight repository, Cypilot SDLC kit rules ARCH-PRD-NO-001 (No Technical Implementation Details) and DATA-PRD-NO-001 (No Data Schema Definitions) prohibit including field-level Bronze table schemas, column types, API field mappings, or Bronze-to-Silver transformation details inside PRD documents. PRDs capture *what* the system must do, not *how*. Schema definitions belong in DESIGN artifacts (e.g., connector-specific `.md` files like `jira.md` for Bronze table definitions). PRDs should reference these design artifacts via cross-links rather than duplicating schema content.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 0\nFile: cypilot/config/kits/sdlc/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-03-17T19:56:47.282Z\nLearning: Create PRD artifacts to document product intent: actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Review%20flagged%20mismatch%20between%20acceptance%20criterion%20and%20FR%20(MUST%20vs%20SHOULD).%20Fixed%3A%20dep-graph%20set%20to%20SHOULD%20(p2)%2C%20acceptance%20accepts%20list%20view%3B%20release-gating%20kept%20separate.%20Try%20%40coderabbitai%20%E2%9C%85%F0%9F%94%A7&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Finsight%2Fpull%2F230%23discussion_r3136789044)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:06Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:06Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577972","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577972"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142577972"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142577972/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":691,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3136789044,"author_association":"NONE","original_position":691,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142578041","pull_request_review_id":4176132412,"id":3142578041,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T-95","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,834 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.2** (current): Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Upgrade with Trial Period\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`\n+\n+When a tenant admin approves an upgrade plan, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependency versions) as a SHADOW STACK alongside the existing install rather than replacing it in place. Both stacks **MUST** run concurrently for a tenant-configurable TRIAL PERIOD (default: 7 days; minimum: one natural sync period or 24 hours, whichever is shorter). During the trial the plugin system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Keep the existing live stack as the authoritative data producer for user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api) — no user observes the upgrade until it is promoted.\n+- Run the shadow stack against the same inputs, writing to its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<shadow_install_id>`, `silver_<shadow_install_id>`, and so on for any capability the plugin introduces).\n+- Expose a comparison view in the admin UI with row counts, schema diffs, and — where shapes match between old and new — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change before committing to it.\n+\n+At the end of the trial the tenant admin either PROMOTES the shadow stack (the runtime uninstalls the old version plus any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and the old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) or REJECTS it (the runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded because no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). If the trial period expires without explicit action, the runtime **MUST** default to REJECT — plugins **MUST NOT** silently promote themselves.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a live plugin to a prior version is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older version (still published in the catalog under `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`): the same trial, comparison, and promote/reject loop applies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In-place upgrades that roll back on failure require the system to snapshot live state and restore it transactionally across connector + silver + widget — expensive to implement, hard to test, and the rollback path is almost never exercised until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk: the new version proves itself on real data before anything flips for users. It naturally composes with multi-version coexistence (`cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`) and the bronze-is-sacred guarantee (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) — during the trial both stacks write to their own scopes without interfering. It also replaces the vague \"rollback\" concept with a symmetric, well-understood primitive.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through (a) an external secret manager (configured at the instance level) and (b) a manual entry path in the admin UI for cases where no external store is wired up. Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, or stored in the catalog.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect secrets to flow through Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or an equivalent. Small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **MUST** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **MUST** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is the only scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block\". For tenant admins during a shadow-deploy trial, seeing the live stack and the shadow stack side-by-side is part of the core upgrade workflow (`cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`), not a nice-to-have.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-upgrade-with-deps`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A plugin install exists; a newer version is available that requires a new version of another plugin.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects Upgrade on the plugin's tile\n+2. Resolver computes the transitive **shadow-deploy** plan (this plugin + dependencies) — the new versions are installed ALONGSIDE the existing ones, not in place of them (per `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`)\n+3. UI shows the plan — which plugins will install as shadow versions, the tenant-configurable trial window (default 7 days), and the side-by-side comparison surfaces that will become available once data starts flowing\n+4. Admin approves; plugin runtime installs the shadow stack in parallel with the existing live stack; both stacks write to their own per-install scopes and run on their own schedules\n+5. During the trial window, the live stack continues to feed user-facing surfaces; the shadow stack processes the same inputs into its own scopes; the admin UI exposes a comparison view (row counts, schema diffs, per-column statistics where shapes match)\n+6. The admin PROMOTES the shadow stack (explicitly, or automatically after the trial window IF the tenant opted in to auto-promote) — the runtime uninstalls the old version and any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+7. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the promoted stack","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","original_commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@mitasovr`, thank you for the thorough summary. The changes in v1.3 fully resolve the inconsistency — removing the auto-promote opt-in, anchoring trial expiry to a UX reminder only, and carrying the \"no auto-promote / no auto-revert\" guarantee through the FR, UC-003 alt flow, and the new Glossary entry creates a single, unambiguous policy across the document. \n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: dzarlax\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 38\nFile: docs/components/connectors/task-tracking/jira/specs/PRD.md:1-601\nTimestamp: 2026-03-25T21:38:30.793Z\nLearning: In the cyberfabric/insight repository, Cypilot SDLC kit rules ARCH-PRD-NO-001 (No Technical Implementation Details) and DATA-PRD-NO-001 (No Data Schema Definitions) prohibit including field-level Bronze table schemas, column types, API field mappings, or Bronze-to-Silver transformation details inside PRD documents. PRDs capture *what* the system must do, not *how*. Schema definitions belong in DESIGN artifacts (e.g., connector-specific `.md` files like `jira.md` for Bronze table definitions). PRDs should reference these design artifacts via cross-links rather than duplicating schema content.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 0\nFile: cypilot/config/kits/sdlc/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-03-17T19:56:47.282Z\nLearning: Create PRD artifacts to document product intent: actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Inconsistent%20trial-expiry%20bug%20fixed%3A%20UC-003%20now%20matches%20FR%20%E2%80%94%20expiry%20defaults%20to%20REJECT%3B%20no%20auto-promote%2Frevert%2C%20admin%20must%20promote.%20Safer%20snapshot-shadow-deploy%20%F0%9F%9A%80%E2%9C%85%20Try%20%40coderabbitai&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Finsight%2Fpull%2F230%23discussion_r3138627642)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:12Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:12Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142578041","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142578041"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142578041"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142578041/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":726,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":730,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138627642,"author_association":"NONE","original_position":730,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142578293","pull_request_review_id":4176132765,"id":3142578293,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67T_B1","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,834 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution](#52-installation-and-dependency-resolution)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Admin Upgrades a Plugin and Resolves Dependencies](#uc-003-admin-upgrades-a-plugin-and-resolves-dependencies)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.2** (current): Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry)\n+- Discover available plugins through a central Insight catalog and through ad-hoc URL install\n+- Install, upgrade, and uninstall plugins per-tenant with explicit admin-driven dependency resolution\n+- Carry multiple versions of the same plugin within a single tenant when needed for migration\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Report per-plugin health, sync status, and dependency-graph state to instance admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The admin-API-driven process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. Resolution is explicit and admin-approved; there is no auto-upgrade, auto-resolve, or \"legacy compatibility\" mode. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Installs, configures, and removes plugins for their tenant. Picks from the instance-approved catalog or supplies a URL for an ad-hoc plugin. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description and compatibility; a configuration form generated from the plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — central Insight catalog + ad-hoc URL install; any OCI-compatible registry\n+- Installation lifecycle — install, upgrade, uninstall, disable, enable at tenant scope\n+- Dependency resolution — explicit, admin-driven, per-tenant, supporting multiple versions of the same plugin simultaneously\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Installation and Dependency Resolution\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance installing the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or through catalog filtering.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing / isolation boundary; shared installs would break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Multiple Versions of the Same Plugin Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support installing two or more versions of the same plugin within a single tenant concurrently. This is the mechanism for migration across breaking changes — the old version keeps producing bronze while the new version is validated, then the old version is uninstalled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without multi-version support, every breaking upgrade is a cutover that risks data loss or downtime. With it, migrations become incremental.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Explicit, Admin-Driven Dependency Resolution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-explicit-resolution`\n+\n+When a tenant admin requests an install or upgrade, the admin API **MUST** compute the full set of plugins (and versions) that would need to be added or upgraded to satisfy the request and **MUST** present that set to the tenant admin for approval before any change is applied. The tenant admin **MUST** be able to reject the resolution. The plugin system **MUST NOT** automatically upgrade, downgrade, or uninstall plugins on anyone's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugins may touch tenant-critical data. Surprise upgrades are unacceptable. Explicit resolution gives the tenant admin a decision point and an audit trail; the default behavior of doing nothing unless approved is the safe default.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, or enable a plugin on behalf of a tenant. Instance admins retain oversight powers — catalog allowlists, force-uninstall, and blocking specific plugins — but every change that adds to or advances a tenant's plugin set **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-uninstall by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data — otherwise accountability breaks down. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages); it is not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Upgrade with Trial Period\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-shadow-deploy-upgrade`\n+\n+When a tenant admin approves an upgrade plan, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new plugin version (and any newly-required dependency versions) as a SHADOW STACK alongside the existing install rather than replacing it in place. Both stacks **MUST** run concurrently for a tenant-configurable TRIAL PERIOD (default: 7 days; minimum: one natural sync period or 24 hours, whichever is shorter). During the trial the plugin system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Keep the existing live stack as the authoritative data producer for user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api) — no user observes the upgrade until it is promoted.\n+- Run the shadow stack against the same inputs, writing to its own per-install scopes (`bronze_<shadow_install_id>`, `silver_<shadow_install_id>`, and so on for any capability the plugin introduces).\n+- Expose a comparison view in the admin UI with row counts, schema diffs, and — where shapes match between old and new — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change before committing to it.\n+\n+At the end of the trial the tenant admin either PROMOTES the shadow stack (the runtime uninstalls the old version plus any transitive dependencies that become unused, the shadow stack becomes live, and the old bronze is retained per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) or REJECTS it (the runtime uninstalls the shadow stack; shadow bronze is discarded because no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). If the trial period expires without explicit action, the runtime **MUST** default to REJECT — plugins **MUST NOT** silently promote themselves.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a live plugin to a prior version is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older version (still published in the catalog under `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`): the same trial, comparison, and promote/reject loop applies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In-place upgrades that roll back on failure require the system to snapshot live state and restore it transactionally across connector + silver + widget — expensive to implement, hard to test, and the rollback path is almost never exercised until an incident. Shadow-deploy inverts the risk: the new version proves itself on real data before anything flips for users. It naturally composes with multi-version coexistence (`cpt-plugin-fr-multi-version`) and the bronze-is-sacred guarantee (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`) — during the trial both stacks write to their own scopes without interfering. It also replaces the vague \"rollback\" concept with a symmetric, well-understood primitive.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### No Legacy Compatibility Mode\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-no-legacy-mode`\n+\n+When a dependency cannot be satisfied (for example, plugin A at v2 requires plugin B at ≥3.0.0 but plugin B is pinned at 2.x for another plugin), the plugin system **MUST** refuse the install and surface the conflict to the admin. The plugin system **MUST NOT** attempt to run plugin A in a fallback mode against an older plugin B.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Implicit \"best-effort\" compatibility layers compound indefinitely and create dependency-hell states that are impossible to reason about. Forcing an explicit choice keeps the dependency graph honest.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant installed-plugin inventory (which plugin IDs, at which versions, enabled or disabled, when installed, by whom) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Unused-Dependency Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-unused-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify plugin installs that were pulled in as transitive dependencies and are no longer referenced by any user-installed plugin, and **SHOULD** present them to the admin for optional uninstall. The system **MUST NOT** uninstall them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents dependency cruft from accumulating silently as plugins come and go. Keeps the surface area the admin has to reason about aligned with current reality.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"b6a1639fbd430afaaa66411fcbb942e71b102d11","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@mitasovr`, that distinction is well-reasoned and I accept it. The original flag was pattern-matched against the learning derived from a Jira connector PRD context, where the issue was *internal* field-level Bronze schema definitions and API field mappings leaking into a PRD. Your situation is different: the plugin system PRD *is itself* defining the extensibility contract for external plugin authors. When a requirement says \"plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries\" or \"connectors MUST declare output schema in the manifest,\" the mechanism is the contract — removing or abstracting it would make the FR unverifiable for any plugin author trying to implement against this spec.\n\nThe scoping approach you've described (v1.3 deferring non-author-facing mechanics — manifest format, signing-key management, image-scan policy, egress-proxy — to Section 13 with named owners and resolution targets) addresses the spirit of ARCH-PRD-NO-001 correctly: the what-for-authors stays in the PRD; the how-the-platform-implements-it goes to DESIGN/ADR.\n\nNo further changes needed on this thread. The framing is sound.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 0\nFile: cypilot/config/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-03-17T17:37:41.947Z\nLearning: Applies to cypilot/config/**/*{*connector*,*schema*,*bronze*,*silver*,*gold*}*.{md,yaml,yml,json} : ALWAYS open and follow `docs/CONNECTORS_REFERENCE.md` WHEN writing or reviewing connector specifications, data schemas, or Bronze/Silver/Gold pipeline tables\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: dzarlax\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 38\nFile: docs/components/connectors/task-tracking/jira/specs/PRD.md:1-601\nTimestamp: 2026-03-25T21:38:30.793Z\nLearning: In the cyberfabric/insight repository, Cypilot SDLC kit rules ARCH-PRD-NO-001 (No Technical Implementation Details) and DATA-PRD-NO-001 (No Data Schema Definitions) prohibit including field-level Bronze table schemas, column types, API field mappings, or Bronze-to-Silver transformation details inside PRD documents. PRDs capture *what* the system must do, not *how*. Schema definitions belong in DESIGN artifacts (e.g., connector-specific `.md` files like `jira.md` for Bronze table definitions). PRDs should reference these design artifacts via cross-links rather than duplicating schema content.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: mozhaev-dev\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 210\nFile: src/ingestion/connectors/ai/claude-admin/dbt/claude_admin__ai_dev_usage.sql:36-37\nTimestamp: 2026-04-21T10:18:48.160Z\nLearning: In the cyberfabric/insight repository, dbt incremental staging models (e.g., `claude_admin__ai_dev_usage.sql`, `claude_admin__ai_api_usage.sql`, and counterparts across other AI connectors) intentionally use a global `max(report_date)` watermark pattern (`date > (SELECT max(report_date) FROM {{ this }})`). This is a known, accepted inconsistency inherited from original staging models. The fix — scoping the watermark by `tenant_id` and `insight_source_id` — is deliberately deferred to a future sweep that will be done alongside the Silver routing work when `silver:class_*` tags are added. Do not flag the global watermark as a blocking issue on individual connector PRs.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/insight PR: 0\nFile: cypilot/config/kits/sdlc/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-03-17T19:56:47.282Z\nLearning: Create PRD artifacts to document product intent: actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:28Z","updated_at":"2026-04-25T21:14:28Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142578293","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142578293"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3142578293"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3142578293/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":387,"original_start_line":362,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":388,"original_line":363,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":3138627633,"author_association":"NONE","original_position":363,"position":388,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146703291","pull_request_review_id":4180237579,"id":3146703291,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67juG7","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[H-1] L4-only egress enforcement; L7 deferred — CDN-fronted services share IP space with attackers**\n\n`NetworkPolicy` is L4 (IP + port). The egress allowlist names hosts (`gitlab.com`) but L4 enforcement means whitelisting *IP ranges*. CDN-fronted services share IP space with attacker-controlled subdomains — once you allow GitHub's IP range, any GitHub-hosted attacker page is reachable. Same for `gitlab.com` (CDN), AWS-hosted services (massive shared IP space), Cloudflare-fronted endpoints.\n\nFor a system that runs third-party code with ClickHouse credentials and tenant data, L4 egress is not a security boundary — it's a soft hint. A connector that legitimately needs `gitlab.com` egress can exfiltrate tenant data to *any* gitlab.com webhook (anyone can register one), to GitHub Pages, to Cloudflare Workers, etc., all through the same approved allowlist.\n\nThe L7 proxy that would actually solve this is in Open Questions (line 1009) without a v1 commitment:\n\n> \"Egress proxy implementation — declarative `NetworkPolicy` is enough for L4 allowlist, but L7 (e.g., HTTP host-based) needs an explicit proxy (Squid, Envoy with allowlist, or sidecar); pick one for v1\"\n\nThat's acknowledging the gap but deferring it. For a P1 baseline isolation FR, this should be a P1 commitment, not a Design TBD.\n\n**Suggested fix:** L7 egress proxy (Envoy / Squid / sidecar with hostname-based allowlist + TLS SNI inspection) is P1 as part of baseline isolation. `NetworkPolicy` is the L4 backstop, not the primary control. Pick the implementation in this PRD, not in DESIGN.\n\nRelated: this is the same channel through which DNS exfiltration (Risks line 989) flows — both close together with an L7 proxy + restricted DNS resolver.","created_at":"2026-04-27T10:53:58Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T10:53:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146703291","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146703291"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146703291"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146703291/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":692,"original_line":692,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":692,"position":692,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146736777","pull_request_review_id":4180278121,"id":3146736777,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2SJ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-1] Silver discovery auto-grants SELECT on new matching scopes without re-approval**\n\nThe silver install gets `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for every scope discovery resolves to. UC-004 alt flow (line 898) says new matching connectors are picked up \"automatically on the next run\" — meaning the GRANT set expands without explicit tenant-admin approval. A malicious or compromised snapshot author can introduce a connector with a matching tag and silently expand which scopes silver can read.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Tenant admin must explicitly approve when discovery resolution adds new scopes to a silver install's GRANT set. Better: bind the resolved set into the snapshot at authoring time (see C-9 lockfile).","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:30Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146736777","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146736777"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146736777"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146736777/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":602,"original_line":602,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":602,"position":602,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146736903","pull_request_review_id":4180278269,"id":3146736903,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2UH","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-2] Trust-root registration UX undefined — backdoor into instance-signed tier**\n\n\"A key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root\" — but how is this registration secured? Out-of-band verification of the key fingerprint? Quorum / two-person approval? Audit-logged with reason?\n\nThe whole instance-signed tier rests on the right key getting registered. If the registration UX accepts a bare key upload over the admin API and the admin API is compromised (or a single admin account is phished), an attacker registers their own key, then signs malicious plugins as \"instance-signed\" with full capabilities (full DOM widget access, any silver engine, runc, broad egress). This is the most direct path to cluster compromise via signing — and the entire mechanism is deferred (Open Question line 1007).\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR — trust-root registration requires out-of-band fingerprint verification (shown in two channels), multi-party approval, full audit log with reason, ability to revoke a previously-trusted key, and a defined trust-root rotation procedure.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:31Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:31Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146736903","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146736903"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146736903"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146736903/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":665,"original_line":665,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":665,"position":665,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737023","pull_request_review_id":4180278403,"id":3146737023,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2V_","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A live snapshot exists for the tenant. The tenant admin has authored a new snapshot (either by editing the live one and saving as a new snapshot, by cloning a vendor or instance reference, or by composing from scratch).\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects \"deploy as shadow\" on the new snapshot\n+2. Plugin runtime instantiates the new snapshot as a *shadow*: provisions per-install ClickHouse users with scoped GRANTs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`), grants each shadow install `SELECT` on the corresponding live install's bronze (per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), creates the new bronze / silver scopes, schedules the shadow plugins' work\n+3. For each plugin in the shadow whose `plugin_id` was also present in the live snapshot, the runtime executes the plugin-author-shipped migration (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`) to populate or expose historical bronze — view, materialized view, full copy, or no migration at the author's choice\n+4. Both snapshots run concurrently. The live snapshot continues to feed widgets and analytics-api; the shadow snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own scopes\n+5. The admin UI exposes a side-by-side comparison view: row counts per silver table, schema diffs, per-column distributional statistics where shapes match (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+6. Adaptation is open-ended. The trial reminder timer (default 7 days, tenant-configurable) surfaces a non-blocking reminder if the shadow has not been promoted or rejected. Reminders escalate by frequency, never by action (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+7. The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT, and the previous (now non-live) snapshot is retained until explicitly retired\n+8. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the new live snapshot\n+\n+**Postconditions**: On promote — the new snapshot is live; the previous snapshot remains running but no longer feeds users; bronze of the previous snapshot is retained until explicit retirement (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`). On reject — the shadow snapshot is uninstalled; its bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects at step 1**: No state change. The new snapshot is saved but not deployed; admin can deploy or discard later.\n+- **A plugin in the shadow fails to install or migrate**: The runtime surfaces the failure; **the live snapshot is untouched**. Admin can retry, edit the snapshot, or discard the shadow.\n+- **Admin REJECTS during adaptation (step 6)**: Runtime uninstalls the shadow snapshot, drops its DB scopes, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT. Tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+- **Trial reminder expires without action**: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts. If the tenant prefers fewer notifications, the reminder cadence is configurable.\n+- **Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot**: This is not a distinct \"rollback\" operation. The admin starts a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history), and follows the same flow. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based, so the same silver plugin accepts any connector whose output shape satisfies its input contract.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+- **A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot**: Silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag. No silver-plugin change is required when a new compatible connector is added.\n+\n+### UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-tenant-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant admin wants a plugin combination not available as a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the snapshot editor in the admin UI\n+2. Admin starts from a fresh canvas, from a clone of the live snapshot, or from a clone of a vendor / instance reference snapshot\n+3. Admin adds, removes, or version-pins individual plugins (the catalog UI surfaces available versions and trust tiers per plugin)\n+4. Admin saves the composition as a new snapshot (immutable; subsequent edits produce a new snapshot, not a mutation)\n+5. Admin deploys the new snapshot as a shadow per UC-003\n+\n+**Postconditions**: A new tenant-owned snapshot is recorded in inventory. The tenant can deploy it now or later. Other snapshots in the tenant's history remain unchanged.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Composition contains a plugin combination flagged by static checks (e.g., two plugins sharing the same `output.tag` in incompatible ways)**: The editor surfaces the issue at save time; the admin can either resolve and resave, or save with a warning. There is no system veto — warnings are non-blocking, but visible.\n+- **Composition includes an unsigned plugin while instance policy disallows unsigned plugins (per `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`)**: Save succeeds, deploy is blocked at the instance level until policy is changed or the plugin is replaced.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to an OCI registry and added to a tenant snapshot through the admin UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the snapshot is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; adding a second connector that produces the expected shape to the same snapshot makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf, nor mutate a tenant's snapshot, through the admin API; force-retire of a known-bad plugin is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason and tenant notification\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two snapshots can coexist in one tenant indefinitely as fully independent data planes (independent bronze / silver scopes, independent per-install ClickHouse users, independent pods); promoting one does not delete the other\n+- [ ] Promoting a new snapshot retains the previous snapshot's bronze until the admin explicitly retires it\n+- [ ] A new snapshot installed as a shadow alongside a live snapshot processes the same connector inputs, exposes a side-by-side comparison view, and never feeds user-facing surfaces until promoted\n+- [ ] A trial reminder timer expires without admin action surfaces a reminder in the UI; no automatic promote, no automatic revert\n+- [ ] Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot is performed by initiating a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot — there is no distinct \"rollback\" API\n+- [ ] Each plugin's transform code (dbt project or equivalent) is self-contained in the plugin directory; attempting to use dbt `ref()` to reach across plugins fails at compile time; a downstream plugin discovers upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract with runtime-injected `sources.yml`\n+- [ ] A plugin install runs under a per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and to discovered upstream bronze for silver); attempting to read or write outside the scope is rejected at the database layer\n+- [ ] A shadow snapshot's installs receive `SELECT` GRANT on the live snapshot's matching bronze for the duration of the shadow only; the GRANT is revoked atomically on promote, reject, or retire\n+- [ ] A plugin install attempted with an image referenced by tag (no digest) is rejected at install time\n+- [ ] A plugin install whose image signature does not chain to a configured trust root is allowed in the unsigned tier with reduced capabilities (iframe widget render, dbt-only silver, gVisor / Kata runtime, curated-registry-only)\n+- [ ] A revoked image digest cannot start new pods even if the install was created before revocation\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can inspect the inventory of all installed plugins across all tenants with per-install health state and per-snapshot grouping (a flat list view satisfies this; a graph visualization is a `SHOULD` per `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` and is not release-gating)\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables are reachable only through the install's per-install user with scoped GRANTs; cross-tenant and cross-install reads are rejected at the database layer, not merely filtered by application code\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin images. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs. Mutable tags are not used — images are pinned by digest | p1 |\n+| Sigstore / cosign infrastructure | Image signing and verification, transparency log, revocation list distribution | p1 |\n+| Instance database (MariaDB) | Stores the per-tenant snapshot inventory, snapshot composition, install config, audit log | p1 |\n+| ClickHouse | Hosts per-snapshot bronze / silver scopes; per-install user RBAC (GRANT/REVOKE), quotas | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles snapshot inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, K8s `NetworkPolicy`, ServiceAccount, ResourceQuota), frontend widget registration | p1 |\n+| Kubernetes `NetworkPolicy` controller | Enforces egress allowlist for plugin pods (baseline isolation) | p1 |\n+| Container runtime classes | Standard `runc` plus `gVisor` or `Kata` available for unsigned-tier plugins; required for kernel isolation | p2 (only needed once unsigned-tier ships) |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for native widget render in vendor- / instance-signed tiers; iframe render in unsigned tier works without it | p2 (widget capability ships when this is available) |\n+| External secret manager | Provides plugin credentials in production; supported integrations include Vault, External Secrets Operator (bridging to AWS / GCP / Azure secret managers), SealedSecrets. Manual-entry fallback for evaluation deployments | p1 (manual-entry suffices for v1, external store is required for prod-grade installs) |\n+| Central catalog service | Hosts plugin and (optionally) reference-snapshot metadata; URL install works without it | p2 |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Snapshot-as-state model: tenant validates the new snapshot in shadow before promoting, on real data, against the old snapshot side-by-side. SemVer hygiene is helpful but no longer load-bearing; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Connector schema conformance enforced at write (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`); silver consumers additionally ship data-quality tests against declared input contracts (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`); plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin image is swapped under the same tag (supply-chain attack) | Malicious code lands on next pull | Images are pinned by digest, never by tag (`cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`); signature verification on every pod admission |\n+| Plugin signing key (vendor or instance) is compromised | All plugins signed by that key become suspect | Sigstore transparency log + revocation list checked on pod admission; per-version signatures so revoking one version does not revoke all; key rotation procedure documented and exercised quarterly |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-3] Vendor signing key has global blast radius; air-gap revocation deferred**\n\nThe vendor key signs every vendor-signed plugin across every customer instance. Compromise = global trust collapse. The mitigation here is \"rotation quarterly\" — but for an air-gapped customer with no Sigstore transparency-log connectivity, revocation distribution is unsolved (Open Question line 1007).\n\nThe vendor key is the highest-impact secret in the entire system. Its protection (HSM choice, ceremony, rotation procedure, revocation distribution to air-gapped customers) is deferred to a DESIGN doc with no v1 commitment. That deferral is reasonable for a PRD but the *operational consequences* of compromise should be sized here: every running vendor-signed plugin across every customer instance becomes suspect simultaneously, and the cascading re-sign + re-deploy across all installations is an operational event the PRD doesn't model.\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR — vendor signing key in HSM with documented rotation procedure; signed revocation bundles distributable via the same channel as plugin updates (works in air-gap); per-version signing so single-version revocation doesn'''t cascade to all versions; documented compromise-response runbook.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:33Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737023","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737023"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737023"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737023/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":980,"original_line":980,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":980,"position":980,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737156","pull_request_review_id":4180278538,"id":3146737156,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2YE","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-4] Manifest schema deferred but load-bearing for entire trust model**\n\nThe manifest declares: capabilities, egress allowlist, secrets, dependencies, image digest, signing claims. *Every* security FR depends on \"what the manifest says.\" Yet the manifest schema itself is in Open Questions (line 996) marked for DESIGN.\n\nIf manifest validation is incomplete (unknown fields silently accepted, type coercion, JSON-vs-YAML parser quirks, length limits absent), the trust model is bypassable through manifest crafting. A malicious author crafts a manifest with extra fields the parser ignores, declared egress that doesn'''t match what the runtime applies, capability claims that the validator misreads, etc.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Manifest schema must be P1, not DESIGN. Strict JSON Schema with `additionalProperties: false`, canonical form for signing (deterministic serialization), hard length limits on all string fields, no type coercion, explicit version field. The schema should be written *before* the security FRs that depend on it.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:34Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:34Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737156","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737156"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737156"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737156/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":272,"original_line":272,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":272,"position":272,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737303","pull_request_review_id":4180278675,"id":3146737303,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2aX","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-5] Manifest tampering vs signing scope not specified**\n\nThe image is signed (`@sha256:...` + cosign). But the manifest contains the egress allowlist, declared capabilities, declared secrets — i.e., the entire security-policy surface. The PRD doesn'''t state whether the manifest is *inside* the signed image (signed by extension) or a *separate* artifact (registered separately, mutable post-sign).\n\nIf separate: an attacker with write access to the catalog or instance inventory can swap the manifest after install. The image signature still verifies, but the runtime now applies a different (more permissive) egress allowlist or capability set. The trust model is end-to-end only if the manifest is part of the signed payload.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Manifest MUST be inside the signed image at a canonical path (e.g., `/insight/manifest.yaml`). The runtime MUST read it from there only and never from a parallel source. Catalog metadata (description, screenshots) is separate and can be re-fetched, but the security-relevant manifest is signed-or-rejected.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:35Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:35Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737303","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737303"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737303"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737303/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":648,"original_line":648,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":648,"position":648,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737439","pull_request_review_id":4180278791,"id":3146737439,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2cf","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-6] Reverse-DNS namespace squatting in unsigned tier**\n\nUnsigned tier is restricted to \"Insight-curated allowlist only\" for image registries (line 672). But there'''s no FR controlling reverse-DNS *names*. An attacker publishes `com.openai.gpt-connector` to a registry on the curated allowlist, ships it unsigned, and the marketplace UI displays it under that name. Tenant admin sees a familiar name and installs it.\n\nThere'''s no FR requiring registry-author binding (e.g., DNS-validated proof of reverse-DNS control, organization verification, or namespace registration with the central catalog). Maven Central, npm scopes, Go modules — all enforce some form of namespace-author binding. Missing here.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Reverse-DNS ownership proof required at catalog acceptance: DNS TXT record under the claimed domain, or GitHub org verification, or explicit registration with a central namespace registry. For unsigned tier, prefix display name with \"untrusted:\" or render in a visually distinct way so users can'''t mistake it for vendor-signed.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:37Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737439","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737439"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737439"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737439/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":254,"original_line":254,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":254,"position":254,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737560","pull_request_review_id":4180278919,"id":3146737560,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2eY","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-7] Tag-based discovery has no provenance check — built-in data poisoning**\n\nAny installed plugin can declare any output tag in its manifest. There'''s no link between tags and plugin identity, no signing of the tag namespace, no validation. A malicious connector publishes tag `#core_metrics`; the legitimate `silver-core-metrics` plugin auto-discovers it (UC-004 alt flow line 898); silver'''s `UNION ALL` now includes attacker-injected data. Data poisoning is a built-in attack surface, not a misuse case.\n\nThis compounds with C-1 (auto-grant on discovery expansion): not only does the malicious connector pollute silver'''s output, the silver install also gets a new SELECT GRANT on the malicious connector'''s scope.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Tags must be in the plugin'''s reverse-DNS namespace (`com.cyberfabric.git/merge_requests` not just `merge_requests`); validated at install time against the plugin'''s author identity; signed as part of the image manifest. A plugin cannot publish under a tag namespace it doesn'''t own.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:38Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:38Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737560","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737560"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737560"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737560/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":437,"original_line":437,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":437,"position":437,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737689","pull_request_review_id":4180279034,"id":3146737689,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2gZ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-8] Resolver is a TCB-level \"god process\" — tenant isolation rests on code, not infrastructure**\n\nThe component that maps discovery rules → real table names → injects `sources.yml` has god-knowledge of all ClickHouse schemas across all tenants. Manipulation of discovery rules (crafted regex like `.*` or `bronze_.*_install_.*`) could trick the resolver into emitting cross-tenant scope names that the runtime then GRANTs to the silver plugin. The resolver is a TCB component with no specified hardening, no input validation grammar, no output verification.\n\nThe deeper issue: tenant isolation in this design is enforced by the *correctness of resolver code*, not by infrastructure-level boundaries. A bug in regex handling, name collision, or output validation in a single component opens cross-tenant access. In a robust design, GRANTs would be **statically managed** (computed once at snapshot-authoring time, written to durable state, applied unchanged at runtime) — not generated on-the-fly per-execution by a privileged service.\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR — strict resolver input grammar (no raw regex; bounded glob with character allowlist); resolver output verified to be a subset of scopes the requesting install'''s tenant owns; resolver isolated component with audit logging. Better: relegate resolution to *snapshot-authoring time*, persist the resolved input set as part of the snapshot (see C-9), and use only that persisted set at execution.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:39Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737689","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737689"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737689"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737689/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":494,"original_line":494,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":494,"position":494,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737809","pull_request_review_id":4180279165,"id":3146737809,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2iR","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[CRITICAL — C-9] Snapshots are not deterministic — execution-time resolution contradicts the \"self-contained snapshot\" claim**\n\nThe PRD claims snapshots are \"immutable, named\" declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` and that \"two snapshots are fully independent data planes.\" But discovery resolution happens at execution time, not at snapshot-authoring time. The same snapshot artifact behaves differently depending on what data the resolver \"sees\" at the moment of each run.\n\nThree failures of the determinism claim:\n\n1. **Discovery expansion** (C-1): a new connector added to the snapshot post-authoring expands what silver discovers, expands silver'''s GRANTs, expands its UNION ALL inputs — without snapshot mutation.\n2. **Tags-for-execution is an antipattern**: tags are appropriate for *discovery* (find candidates) but inappropriate for *execution* (the actual contract a query runs against). Execution bindings should be deterministic — by ID, digest, or explicit reference — and captured at the moment of decision.\n3. **No lockfile**: every analogous system in software engineering — Cargo.lock, package-lock.json, go.sum, pip-compile output — pins not just versions but the *resolution graph*. The PRD captures plugin versions but lets the resolution graph float.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Snapshot artifact MUST include a resolved input lockfile: `{install_id: {bronze_scope, columns, version, source_install_id}}`, computed at snapshot-authoring time, frozen for the snapshot'''s lifetime. Discovery is for *editing* a snapshot; execution reads the lockfile only. Adding a new connector requires authoring a new snapshot. This single change closes C-1, C-7, and most of C-8'''s execution-time blast radius.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:41Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737809","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737809"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737809"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737809/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":294,"original_line":294,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":294,"position":294,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737926","pull_request_review_id":4180279301,"id":3146737926,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2kG","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A live snapshot exists for the tenant. The tenant admin has authored a new snapshot (either by editing the live one and saving as a new snapshot, by cloning a vendor or instance reference, or by composing from scratch).\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects \"deploy as shadow\" on the new snapshot\n+2. Plugin runtime instantiates the new snapshot as a *shadow*: provisions per-install ClickHouse users with scoped GRANTs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`), grants each shadow install `SELECT` on the corresponding live install's bronze (per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), creates the new bronze / silver scopes, schedules the shadow plugins' work\n+3. For each plugin in the shadow whose `plugin_id` was also present in the live snapshot, the runtime executes the plugin-author-shipped migration (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`) to populate or expose historical bronze — view, materialized view, full copy, or no migration at the author's choice\n+4. Both snapshots run concurrently. The live snapshot continues to feed widgets and analytics-api; the shadow snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own scopes\n+5. The admin UI exposes a side-by-side comparison view: row counts per silver table, schema diffs, per-column distributional statistics where shapes match (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+6. Adaptation is open-ended. The trial reminder timer (default 7 days, tenant-configurable) surfaces a non-blocking reminder if the shadow has not been promoted or rejected. Reminders escalate by frequency, never by action (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+7. The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT, and the previous (now non-live) snapshot is retained until explicitly retired\n+8. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the new live snapshot\n+\n+**Postconditions**: On promote — the new snapshot is live; the previous snapshot remains running but no longer feeds users; bronze of the previous snapshot is retained until explicit retirement (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`). On reject — the shadow snapshot is uninstalled; its bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects at step 1**: No state change. The new snapshot is saved but not deployed; admin can deploy or discard later.\n+- **A plugin in the shadow fails to install or migrate**: The runtime surfaces the failure; **the live snapshot is untouched**. Admin can retry, edit the snapshot, or discard the shadow.\n+- **Admin REJECTS during adaptation (step 6)**: Runtime uninstalls the shadow snapshot, drops its DB scopes, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT. Tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+- **Trial reminder expires without action**: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts. If the tenant prefers fewer notifications, the reminder cadence is configurable.\n+- **Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot**: This is not a distinct \"rollback\" operation. The admin starts a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history), and follows the same flow. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based, so the same silver plugin accepts any connector whose output shape satisfies its input contract.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+- **A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot**: Silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag. No silver-plugin change is required when a new compatible connector is added.\n+\n+### UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-tenant-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant admin wants a plugin combination not available as a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the snapshot editor in the admin UI\n+2. Admin starts from a fresh canvas, from a clone of the live snapshot, or from a clone of a vendor / instance reference snapshot\n+3. Admin adds, removes, or version-pins individual plugins (the catalog UI surfaces available versions and trust tiers per plugin)\n+4. Admin saves the composition as a new snapshot (immutable; subsequent edits produce a new snapshot, not a mutation)\n+5. Admin deploys the new snapshot as a shadow per UC-003\n+\n+**Postconditions**: A new tenant-owned snapshot is recorded in inventory. The tenant can deploy it now or later. Other snapshots in the tenant's history remain unchanged.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Composition contains a plugin combination flagged by static checks (e.g., two plugins sharing the same `output.tag` in incompatible ways)**: The editor surfaces the issue at save time; the admin can either resolve and resave, or save with a warning. There is no system veto — warnings are non-blocking, but visible.\n+- **Composition includes an unsigned plugin while instance policy disallows unsigned plugins (per `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`)**: Save succeeds, deploy is blocked at the instance level until policy is changed or the plugin is replaced.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to an OCI registry and added to a tenant snapshot through the admin UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the snapshot is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; adding a second connector that produces the expected shape to the same snapshot makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf, nor mutate a tenant's snapshot, through the admin API; force-retire of a known-bad plugin is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason and tenant notification\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two snapshots can coexist in one tenant indefinitely as fully independent data planes (independent bronze / silver scopes, independent per-install ClickHouse users, independent pods); promoting one does not delete the other\n+- [ ] Promoting a new snapshot retains the previous snapshot's bronze until the admin explicitly retires it\n+- [ ] A new snapshot installed as a shadow alongside a live snapshot processes the same connector inputs, exposes a side-by-side comparison view, and never feeds user-facing surfaces until promoted\n+- [ ] A trial reminder timer expires without admin action surfaces a reminder in the UI; no automatic promote, no automatic revert\n+- [ ] Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot is performed by initiating a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot — there is no distinct \"rollback\" API\n+- [ ] Each plugin's transform code (dbt project or equivalent) is self-contained in the plugin directory; attempting to use dbt `ref()` to reach across plugins fails at compile time; a downstream plugin discovers upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract with runtime-injected `sources.yml`\n+- [ ] A plugin install runs under a per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and to discovered upstream bronze for silver); attempting to read or write outside the scope is rejected at the database layer\n+- [ ] A shadow snapshot's installs receive `SELECT` GRANT on the live snapshot's matching bronze for the duration of the shadow only; the GRANT is revoked atomically on promote, reject, or retire\n+- [ ] A plugin install attempted with an image referenced by tag (no digest) is rejected at install time\n+- [ ] A plugin install whose image signature does not chain to a configured trust root is allowed in the unsigned tier with reduced capabilities (iframe widget render, dbt-only silver, gVisor / Kata runtime, curated-registry-only)\n+- [ ] A revoked image digest cannot start new pods even if the install was created before revocation\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can inspect the inventory of all installed plugins across all tenants with per-install health state and per-snapshot grouping (a flat list view satisfies this; a graph visualization is a `SHOULD` per `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` and is not release-gating)\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables are reachable only through the install's per-install user with scoped GRANTs; cross-tenant and cross-install reads are rejected at the database layer, not merely filtered by application code\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin images. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs. Mutable tags are not used — images are pinned by digest | p1 |\n+| Sigstore / cosign infrastructure | Image signing and verification, transparency log, revocation list distribution | p1 |\n+| Instance database (MariaDB) | Stores the per-tenant snapshot inventory, snapshot composition, install config, audit log | p1 |\n+| ClickHouse | Hosts per-snapshot bronze / silver scopes; per-install user RBAC (GRANT/REVOKE), quotas | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles snapshot inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, K8s `NetworkPolicy`, ServiceAccount, ResourceQuota), frontend widget registration | p1 |\n+| Kubernetes `NetworkPolicy` controller | Enforces egress allowlist for plugin pods (baseline isolation) | p1 |\n+| Container runtime classes | Standard `runc` plus `gVisor` or `Kata` available for unsigned-tier plugins; required for kernel isolation | p2 (only needed once unsigned-tier ships) |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for native widget render in vendor- / instance-signed tiers; iframe render in unsigned tier works without it | p2 (widget capability ships when this is available) |\n+| External secret manager | Provides plugin credentials in production; supported integrations include Vault, External Secrets Operator (bridging to AWS / GCP / Azure secret managers), SealedSecrets. Manual-entry fallback for evaluation deployments | p1 (manual-entry suffices for v1, external store is required for prod-grade installs) |\n+| Central catalog service | Hosts plugin and (optionally) reference-snapshot metadata; URL install works without it | p2 |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Snapshot-as-state model: tenant validates the new snapshot in shadow before promoting, on real data, against the old snapshot side-by-side. SemVer hygiene is helpful but no longer load-bearing; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Connector schema conformance enforced at write (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`); silver consumers additionally ship data-quality tests against declared input contracts (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`); plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin image is swapped under the same tag (supply-chain attack) | Malicious code lands on next pull | Images are pinned by digest, never by tag (`cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`); signature verification on every pod admission |\n+| Plugin signing key (vendor or instance) is compromised | All plugins signed by that key become suspect | Sigstore transparency log + revocation list checked on pod admission; per-version signatures so revoking one version does not revoke all; key rotation procedure documented and exercised quarterly |\n+| Plugin reverse-DNS naming collides across registries (`com.cyberfabric.gitea` on dockerhub vs ghcr.io) | Tenant could install the wrong artifact | Trust tier requires signature; instance admin's curated registry allowlist prevents arbitrary registries for vendor-signed and instance-signed tiers; for unsigned, Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Plugin runtime crash leaves an install in a half-applied state | Manual recovery required | Every install/upgrade plan is persisted before application; runtime is reconciliation-based, so restart resumes from persisted state |\n+| Plugin attempts cross-tenant data access through DB | Tenant data leakage | Per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to install's own bronze / silver and (for silver) discovered upstream bronze; cross-snapshot / cross-tenant reads are physically impossible at the DB layer, not just filtered by application code (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`) |\n+| Widget plugin escapes its sandbox and accesses user JWT or tenant data | Authentication / data leakage | Widget never queries the data store directly; for unsigned plugins the widget renders in a cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` and the user JWT is never exposed across the iframe boundary (`cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`); for signed plugins, host SDK proxy is the only data path |\n+| Plugin pod runs scanners, miners, reverse VPN, or other unintended workloads inside the cluster | Compute theft / lateral movement | NetworkPolicy limits egress to manifest-declared targets; ResourceQuota caps CPU/memory; default ServiceAccount has no K8s API access; for unsigned plugins, gVisor/Kata runtime adds kernel isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) |\n+| Third-party plugin becomes unmaintained and no one upgrades it past a breaking platform version | Tenants stuck, cannot upgrade platform | Tenant snapshots pin specific digest+signature, so an abandoned plugin keeps working at its pinned version; new platform versions document compatibility windows; abandoned plugins flagged in catalog, not auto-removed |\n+| Storage cost balloons if many shadow snapshots accumulate over months/years | Disk exhaustion, query slowdown | View-based migrations keep storage at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` (`cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`); retired-snapshot reporting (`cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`) surfaces stale snapshots to admin for cleanup; bronze retention is admin-controlled, not auto |\n+| Tenant admin loses track of which old snapshots are still alive | Unbounded resource consumption, audit confusion | Snapshot inventory exposes `live` / `shadow` / `retired` status per snapshot; retired-snapshot reporting nudges cleanup; dependency graph view groups installs by snapshot |\n+| Plugin runtime egress through manifest allowlist is bypassed by DNS exfiltration or covert channels | Data leakage | Egress proxy enforces allowlist at L7 where possible; baseline only — does not claim defense against sophisticated covert channels. Documented limitation: instance admins should use cluster-level egress monitoring for residual coverage |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-2] DNS exfiltration explicitly accepted**\n\n> \"Egress proxy enforces allowlist at L7 where possible; baseline only — does not claim defense against sophisticated covert channels. Documented limitation: instance admins should use cluster-level egress monitoring for residual coverage\"\n\nAcknowledged but accepted. A connector with legitimate DNS access can encode ~250 bytes per query in subdomains and stream tenant data through DNS to an attacker-controlled NS via CNAME chains, TXT records, or query-name encoding. No size limit, no rate limit, no requirement to use a cluster-internal DNS resolver only.\n\nFor a system that runs third-party code with database credentials, \"instance admin should monitor egress\" is not a control — it'''s an unverified assumption pushed onto every customer.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Plugin pods MUST use a cluster-internal DNS resolver that (a) logs queries, (b) rate-limits by entropy/length signal, (c) enforces TTL caps to prevent NXDOMAIN-burst exfil. NetworkPolicy MUST default-deny egress to UDP/53 except to that internal resolver.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:43Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:43Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737926","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737926"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146737926"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146737926/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":989,"original_line":989,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":989,"position":989,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738066","pull_request_review_id":4180279527,"id":3146738066,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2mS","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-3] Egress allowlist is plugin-self-declared with no validation**\n\nThe plugin'''s manifest declares its egress targets. Tenant admin reviews and approves at install (for unsigned tier per line 678; for signed tiers, accepted). Nothing validates that the declared targets are *needed* by the plugin'''s stated capability.\n\nA plugin author writes \"I'''m a GitLab connector, I need egress to `gitlab.com` **and** `evil.com` (for telemetry).\" Tenant admin approves the install — the egress to evil.com is now sanctioned by policy. For signed tiers the matrix says \"manifest allowlist accepted\" with no further check.\n\nThis stacks with H-1 (L4-only): even if the egress destination is legitimate, L4 enforcement means anyone hosted on the same IP range is reachable.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Catalog acceptance (for signed tiers) includes validation that declared egress matches the plugin'''s stated function — outliers flagged for review. For unsigned tier, the install UI surfaces *every* egress destination prominently, with friendly host names and attribution (\"api.gitlab.com — official GitLab API\" vs \"unrecognized: evil.com\").","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:44Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:44Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738066","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738066"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738066"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738066/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":692,"original_line":692,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":692,"position":692,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738197","pull_request_review_id":4180279663,"id":3146738197,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2oV","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-4] Pod Security Standard `restricted` not pinned**\n\nThe list of pod restrictions (read-only rootfs, no privileged, no hostPath, no host networking) is partial. Missing from this enumeration:\n\n- `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`\n- Drop ALL Linux capabilities (default container has CAP_CHOWN, CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, CAP_FSETID, CAP_FOWNER, CAP_MKNOD, CAP_NET_RAW, CAP_SETGID, CAP_SETUID, CAP_SETFCAP, CAP_SETPCAP, CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, CAP_SYS_CHROOT, CAP_KILL, CAP_AUDIT_WRITE — all should be dropped)\n- Non-root UID enforced (`runAsNonRoot: true`, explicit `runAsUser`)\n- seccomp profile (`RuntimeDefault` or stricter)\n- AppArmor / SELinux profile\n- No-new-privileges\n\nWithout explicit Pod Security Standard `restricted`, \"no privileged\" doesn'''t prevent CAP_SYS_ADMIN granted as a default capability via the container runtime'''s default set. CAP_NET_RAW alone enables raw socket attacks; CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE bypasses file ACLs.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Reference Kubernetes Pod Security Standard `restricted` explicitly in this FR and enumerate every restriction. The label `pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted` on plugin namespaces is the operational mechanism.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:46Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:46Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738197","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738197"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738197"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738197/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":689,"original_line":689,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":689,"position":689,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738341","pull_request_review_id":4180279821,"id":3146738341,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2ql","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-5] gVisor / Kata only for unsigned tier; trusted tiers run on shared kernel**\n\nThe trust model says \"we trust these tiers more\" but the runtime hardening goes the opposite way: vendor-signed and instance-signed run on `runc` (shared kernel), only unsigned gets gVisor / Kata kernel isolation.\n\nThe implication: a vendor-signing-key compromise (C-3) OR an instance-signed plugin with a 0-day = container escape directly to the shared kernel — full node access, all other pods on the node compromised. Meanwhile the *least-trusted* tier has the *strongest* isolation. This inverts the defense-in-depth posture.\n\nA 0-day in a vendor-signed plugin is not hypothetical: vendor plugins are first-party code with the most CH access, the broadest egress allowlist, the largest attack surface. They'''re also the most likely target for supply-chain attacks (more deployments = higher payoff).\n\n**Suggested fix:** Kernel isolation (gVisor or Kata) applies to **all** tiers. The perf delta is acceptable for the security posture. If perf is a real concern, narrow the exception to specific declared workloads (e.g., bulk INSERT-only connectors) with explicit risk acknowledgment, not by trust tier.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:47Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:47Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738341","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738341"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738341"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738341/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":677,"original_line":677,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":677,"position":677,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738537","pull_request_review_id":4180280061,"id":3146738537,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2tp","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-6] Secrets via env vars — visible in /proc, K8s API, dumps**\n\nEnv vars are visible in:\n- `/proc/<pid>/environ` — readable by any process in the same pod (init container, sidecar, the plugin'''s own subprocess, dependencies that fork, language runtimes that expose `os.environ`)\n- K8s API to anyone with `get pods` permission, including the plugin runtime (which is a privileged component)\n- Core dumps and crash tracebacks (Python `os.environ` always in the traceback)\n- Argo Workflows controller logs (see H-18) and centralized log aggregation\n\nFile-mounted secrets via `tmpfs` is the standard hardening pattern. The PRD picks the weakest pattern explicitly.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Secrets injected as `tmpfs` file mounts at a defined path (e.g., `/run/secrets/clickhouse/password`). Env vars carry only the path, not the value. Plugin SDK provides a helper to read the file. Closes H-6, mitigates H-18 (Argo logging).","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:49Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738537","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738537"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738537"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738537/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":599,"original_line":599,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":599,"position":599,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738680","pull_request_review_id":4180280246,"id":3146738680,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2v4","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-7] Plugin runtime hardening unspecified — most-privileged component undefended in spec**\n\nThe plugin runtime: pulls images, generates ClickHouse users (needs CH admin creds), creates K8s NetworkPolicies / ServiceAccounts / ResourceQuotas (needs K8s admin role), reads/writes secrets, mounts the inventory DB, owns the resolver. Compromise of the runtime = total instance pwn — every tenant'''s data, every tenant'''s plugin set, every secret.\n\nThe PRD has zero FRs about how the runtime itself is isolated, what its blast radius looks like, how its own secrets are managed, who can deploy it, what its K8s permissions are bounded to. The single most attack-valuable component in the system is undefended in the spec.\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR for plugin runtime hardening: separate K8s namespace, scoped ServiceAccount with least-privilege role (only the K8s objects it actually creates), separate CH admin user with DB-level GRANTs only (not server-level), secrets accessed via service-mesh-issued tokens, runtime self-monitoring with alerts on unusual K8s/CH operations. Same trust-tier signing applied to the runtime'''s own image.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:50Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:50Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738680","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738680"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738680"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738680/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":178,"original_line":178,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":178,"position":178,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738801","pull_request_review_id":4180280397,"id":3146738801,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2xx","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-8] Migration scripts execute SQL with cross-snapshot read; no allowlist of CH functions**\n\nPlugin authors ship arbitrary SQL that runs with `SELECT` GRANT on the prior snapshot'''s bronze. ClickHouse SQL is rich:\n- `system.functions`, `system.tables`, `system.columns`, `system.users`\n- Executable UDFs (if `allow_executable_user_defined_functions` enabled)\n- Table functions: `url()`, `file()`, `s3()`, `remote()`, `cluster()`, `mysql()`, `postgresql()`, `hdfs()`, `kafka()`\n- Settings that change query behavior: `allow_introspection_functions`\n\nWithout explicit DENY of these (which the per-install GRANT model needs to enforce *explicitly* via ClickHouse settings constraints, not just GRANTs), a \"migration\" can be RCE in the database server (`url()` to attacker-controlled endpoint, executable UDF), data exfil (`url()` POST), or cross-tenant probe (`remote()` to other shards).\n\n**Suggested fix:** Migration SQL parsed and validated against a statement allowlist before execution. The per-install ClickHouse user has explicit settings constraints denying all the above. Migration scripts are run by the runtime in a wrapper that pre-validates them, not directly by the plugin pod.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:52Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738801","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738801"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738801"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738801/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":477,"original_line":477,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":477,"position":477,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738924","pull_request_review_id":4180280532,"id":3146738924,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j2zs","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-9] Atomic snapshot promote unspecified across K8s + CH + DB**\n\n> \"atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot\"\n\nAtomic with respect to what? Single DB transaction is feasible for the inventory record. But \"switching live\" also means:\n\n1. Revoking cross-snapshot GRANTs on the shadow\n2. Granting whatever new GRANTs the new live needs\n3. Redirecting analytics-api to the new scopes\n4. Terminating old snapshot pods\n5. Starting new snapshot pods if they weren'''t already\n6. Updating audit log\n\nThese cross K8s + ClickHouse + admin-DB. There is no distributed transaction. The TOCTOU window is real:\n\n- An in-flight query in the shadow plugin against the old live'''s bronze can complete *after* the GRANT is revoked because ClickHouse doesn'''t terminate active queries on REVOKE\n- Pod terminations are not atomic with traffic redirect; analytics-api may briefly read from the old, briefly from the new, briefly fail with no data\n- If step 4 succeeds but step 3 hasn'''t propagated, analytics-api hits dead pods\n\n**Suggested fix:** Define promote as a state machine with explicit rollback at every step; tolerate partial failure with a documented recovery procedure; bound the cross-snapshot GRANT lifetime independent of the atomic flip (e.g., GRANT remains valid for K minutes after promote to drain in-flight queries, then revoked); analytics-api uses a versioned routing token validated at query time.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:53Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:53Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738924","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738924"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146738924"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146738924/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":341,"original_line":341,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":341,"position":341,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739078","pull_request_review_id":4180280747,"id":3146739078,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j22G","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-10] Force-retire by instance admin lacks safeguards**\n\nInstance admin can force-retire a plugin across all tenants. The FR says it'''s audit-logged with reason and notifies tenant admin. But:\n\n- Synchronous? Cooling-off window before action takes effect?\n- Two-person approval for cross-tenant retire?\n- Is the audit log replicated to tamper-evident storage?\n- Is there a recovery procedure if the action was wrong?\n\nA compromised instance-admin account can wipe every tenant'''s install with one call. There'''s no defense in depth — one credential, one verb, all tenants affected immediately.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Force-retire requires:\n1. Reason field (validated, non-empty, free text)\n2. Notification to all affected tenant admins BEFORE action with a delay window (e.g., 1 hour) unless emergency-flagged\n3. Emergency flag requires explicit \"emergency=true\" parameter and additional audit log entry\n4. Two-person approval for retiring more than N tenants in a single action\n5. Audit log replicated to append-only sink (S3 WORM / SIEM forwarder)\n6. Retire is reversible within 7 days (mark as retired, drop after grace period)","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:55Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:55Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739078","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739078"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739078"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739078/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":322,"original_line":322,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":322,"position":322,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739253","pull_request_review_id":4180280913,"id":3146739253,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j241","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-11] Audit log lives in inventory DB, not tamper-evident**\n\nThe audit log of authoring and promotion events lives in the same instance database that stores the inventory. If the database is compromised (SQL injection in the admin API, leaked DB creds, malicious DBA), the audit log is alterable — the very record that would prove the breach can be edited by the attacker.\n\nStandard pattern: append-only audit storage external to the primary DB, exported to SIEM in real time. Object storage with object lock (S3 WORM, GCS retention policy) is the cheap version; immutable distributed ledger for higher assurance.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Audit events streamed to an append-only sink (S3 WORM, GCS retention-locked bucket, or SIEM forwarder) in addition to the inventory DB. Inventory DB copy is for fast querying; the WORM copy is the legal record. Cross-validated periodically; divergence raises an incident.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:56Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:56Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739253","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739253"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739253"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739253/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":365,"original_line":365,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":365,"position":365,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739412","pull_request_review_id":4180281067,"id":3146739412,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j27U","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-12] `tenant_id` FR contradicts schema-per-install model**\n\nThis FR demands BOTH:\n- \"All plugin-produced data MUST carry the `tenant_id`\"\n- \"Cross-tenant reads ... MUST be rejected by the data store'''s enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code\"\n\nBut the rest of the design (per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`) enforces isolation via **schema-per-install**, not via `tenant_id` columns. With schema isolation, the `tenant_id` column is redundant — the install can'''t even see other tenants'''s schemas, GRANT-rejected.\n\nOpen Question line 1002 says the choice is DESIGN-level. But this FR pre-commits to *both* models:\n- ClickHouse has no native row-level security (RLS), so the `tenant_id`-based DB-layer enforcement isn'''t mechanically possible without application code or a custom proxy\n- Schema-per-install + per-install user IS mechanically enforced — but doesn'''t require `tenant_id` columns\n\nThe two halves of the FR contradict.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Pick one. If schema-per-install + per-install GRANT is the choice (consistent with the rest of v1.3), drop the `tenant_id`-column requirement (it'''s redundant) and reword the FR to talk about scope-level isolation. If `tenant_id` + RLS is the choice, justify against ClickHouse limitations and specify the proxy mechanism that enforces it.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:58Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739412","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739412"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739412"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739412/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":552,"original_line":552,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":552,"position":552,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739543","pull_request_review_id":4180281221,"id":3146739543,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j29X","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-13] Airbyte stdout protocol: no transport typing, no backpressure**\n\nThe Airbyte protocol = JSON-lines over stdout. Two real problems:\n\n**Security**: a compromised connector writes arbitrary bytes to stdout. The host parser is parsing untrusted JSON-lines from a process with no input validation contract. JSON parsers have had RCE-class bugs (deeply nested structures hitting stack limits, prototype pollution in JS-based parsers, billion-laughs analogues). Without a declared schema for the stream, the host has to be defensive against every possible malicious payload shape.\n\n**Reliability**: no backpressure mechanism. Stdout is a kernel pipe with a fixed buffer (~64KB on Linux). If the host reads slowly (silver behind, CH slow, network glitch), the kernel buffer fills, the connector blocks on `write()`. Depending on the connector'''s internal logic this can OOM the connector pod or corrupt the stream when the connector retries.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Connector → host channel uses a typed protocol (gRPC + protobuf) with declared schemas and built-in flow control. Raw stdout JSON-lines is acceptable only as transport *internal* to the Airbyte-wrapping shim — never as the contract surface to the Insight host. Define the host-facing protocol explicitly; \"any protocol the author wants\" is not a contract.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:00:59Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:00Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739543","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739543"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739543"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739543/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":415,"original_line":415,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":415,"position":415,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739740","pull_request_review_id":4180281450,"id":3146739740,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3Ac","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-14] No mTLS / handshake protocol between plugin pods and runtime API**\n\n> \"Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime'''s API, not directly against the K8s API.\"\n\nWhat API? Auth scheme? Plugin pods have a default ServiceAccount with no bindings, so K8s ServiceAccount tokens aren'''t the answer. IP-based allowlisting is spoofable from any compromised pod in the cluster network. Header-based \"I'''m install_id=X\" is trivially forgeable.\n\nWithout mTLS, any pod that can reach the runtime'''s network endpoint can call its API claiming to be a plugin. The runtime API is an attractive target (it spawns jobs, reads/writes inventory, manages GRANTs).\n\n**Suggested fix:** mTLS with per-install client certificates issued at install time (via cert-manager or SPIFFE/SPIRE) and rotated per snapshot lifetime; identity bound to the `install_id` via cert SAN; runtime API rejects requests without valid client cert; cert revocation on uninstall.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:01Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:01Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739740","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739740"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739740"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739740/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":694,"original_line":694,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":694,"position":694,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739943","pull_request_review_id":4180281655,"id":3146739943,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3Dn","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-15] Plugin Runtime Interface has no version negotiation / N-1 backward-compat**\n\n> \"Stability: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\"\n\nThe PRD versions snapshots, plugins, and capability contracts. It does NOT version the **Host API** — the surface the runtime exposes to plugins. When the platform upgrades, every plugin built against the prior version breaks.\n\nFor a system where plugins live for years (digest-pinned, abandoned-author-friendly per Risk line 986), this is a guaranteed breakage at every platform upgrade. There'''s no FR requiring N-1 or N-2 backward compatibility on the host side, no negotiation handshake at install time, no rejection-with-clear-error if a plugin is incompatible.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Host API versioned with semver; plugins declare their minimum compatible version in the manifest; runtime supports N-1 and ideally N-2 host versions concurrently; install-time rejection with clear error if incompatible; deprecation policy with two-minor-release window before removing a host API version.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:03Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:03Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739943","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739943"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146739943"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146739943/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":768,"original_line":768,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":768,"position":768,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740079","pull_request_review_id":4180281796,"id":3146740079,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3Fv","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-16] Widget postMessage origin checks + DOM isolation for native-render unspecified**\n\nFor *vendor-signed* and *instance-signed* widgets that render as \"native React component (full DOM)\" — they have access to the host page DOM, cookies, localStorage, the user'''s auth token (matrix says \"through host SDK proxy\" but the matrix doesn'''t require widget code to be otherwise DOM-isolated). A signed widget can read another widget'''s data, scrape the user'''s session cookie, inject into the parent DOM, modify global JS state.\n\nFor *unsigned* widgets in iframe — `postMessage` requires `targetOrigin` validation on both sides. The matrix says iframe + postMessage but doesn'''t specify required origin checks, message schema validation, or rate limiting on cross-frame messages. Misconfigured `targetOrigin: '''*'''` is the classic XSS-adjacent bug.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Widget runtime contract specifies:\n- postMessage schema (typed message protocol, version field, validated against schema)\n- Required `targetOrigin` (never `'''*'''`)\n- DOM-isolation primitives even native-render widgets must respect (Shadow DOM, scoped styles, data passed in as props, no `window.parent` access, no `document.cookie` access — enforced by the host SDK)\n- Rate limiting on cross-frame messages","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:04Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740079","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740079"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740079"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740079/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":675,"original_line":675,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":675,"position":675,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740233","pull_request_review_id":4180281940,"id":3146740233,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3IJ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-17] Widget data over-fetching — host doesn'''t project to declared columns**\n\n> \"The host application MUST resolve the data based on the dashboard'''s widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget MUST NOT query the data store directly. The host MUST act as a pass-through — it MUST NOT validate that the delivered data matches the widget'''s declared schema.\"\n\nThe widget declares its expected schema (column names, types, nullability). The host MUST NOT *validate* shape — but does the host *project* to only the columns the widget declared? If the host returns a SELECT * (or all columns the silver scope contains, or fields the dashboard config requested but the widget didn'''t declare), the widget receives data it didn'''t declare it needed. Sensitive columns pass through to the browser.\n\nA malicious or compromised widget can exfil that extra data via egress (matrix: native React = full DOM, can `fetch()` anywhere the browser CSP allows).\n\n**Suggested fix:** Host MUST project query results to the declared widget input schema; columns not in the declared schema are dropped before passing to the widget. Widget'''s own egress is independently restricted by browser CSP set by the host.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:06Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:06Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740233","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740233"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740233"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740233/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":520,"original_line":520,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":520,"position":520,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740407","pull_request_review_id":4180282097,"id":3146740407,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3K3","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-18] Argo Workflows logs leak env-injected secrets**\n\nArgo'''s pod-spec logging is well-documented: workflow parameters and pod env appear in:\n- Argo controller logs\n- Pod spec stored in etcd (visible to anyone with `get pods`)\n- Centralized log aggregation (Elasticsearch / Loki / Datadog) when controller logs are forwarded\n- Python crash tracebacks include `os.environ`\n\nFor a plugin pod with `CH_PASSWORD` injected via env (per this FR), the password lands in all of the above. The PRD picks env-var injection AND uses Argo as the orchestrator (line 958, 968) without flagging this interaction.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Move secrets to tmpfs file mounts (also fixes H-6); audit Argo log filters to redact env content; require log-forwarder sanitization layer; standard practice: mount secrets at `/run/secrets/...` and pass only the *path* through env.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:08Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:08Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740407","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740407"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740407"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740407/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":599,"original_line":599,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":599,"position":599,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740523","pull_request_review_id":4180282229,"id":3146740523,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3Mr","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-19] \"Any language\" silver = binary opacity AND undefined injection protocol for non-dbt**\n\nTwo compounding problems:\n\n1. **Audit opacity**: dbt SQL is auditable — parse the project, list every table touched, every function called. A Rust binary or compiled Python script is opaque to static analysis. After receiving a valid `sources.yml`, the binary can scan adjacent name ranges, probe for ClickHouse protocol-level bugs, fingerprint the schema for later attack — none detectable without runtime instrumentation.\n\n2. **Injection protocol undefined for non-dbt engines**: For dbt the runtime injects `sources.yml` — a standard, well-known mechanism. For Rust / Python / Go the PRD waves at \"env vars or config file\" without specifying the format, path, schema, encoding, or version-compat story. Every binary plugin author writes a custom parser for the magic config. A format change (YAML → JSON, key rename, structure flattening) breaks every binary plugin simultaneously and silently.\n\nAlso: \"dbt only\" for unsigned tier is mechanically unenforceable since dbt itself executes arbitrary SQL via macros, hooks (pre/post/on-run-start/on-run-end), and operation macros.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Define a versioned, schema-validated injection contract that applies to **all** engines — single canonical file at a known path with a schema version field, SDK helper for each supported language. For higher tiers, require runtime query telemetry (every CH query the plugin issues, logged). For unsigned tier, replace \"dbt only\" with a concrete enforceable restriction (e.g., a sidecar that intercepts CH connections and validates SQL against an allowlist before forwarding).","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:09Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:09Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740523","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740523"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740523"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740523/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":459,"original_line":459,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":459,"position":459,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740708","pull_request_review_id":4180282372,"id":3146740708,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3Pk","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-20] Per-install storage and write-volume limits not specified — terabytes-of-garbage attack unmitigated**\n\nThe per-install GRANT gives `INSERT` on the install'''s scope. ClickHouse imposes no inherent limit on cumulative INSERT volume — a plugin with valid GRANTs can INSERT until disk is exhausted. The only quota mention here is \"max queries per minute, max memory per query\" — both per-query, not cumulative. ResourceQuota on the K8s namespace caps pod CPU / memory / ephemeral storage but does not extend to CH-side storage.\n\nA malicious or buggy connector can:\n- INSERT terabytes of random strings into its own bronze scope\n- Generate billion-row outputs that fit the declared schema (so connector-schema-conformance doesn'''t catch them)\n- Hold long-running INSERT transactions blocking compaction\n- Saturate CH disk I/O, degrading every other tenant'''s dashboards\n\nClickHouse is **shared across tenants** (one cluster per instance, line 954) — this is a cross-tenant DoS vector. The trust-tier matrix doesn'''t address it; even vendor-signed plugins have no write-volume cap.\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR family for write-side limits:\n1. Per-install storage quota (max bytes per `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`)\n2. Per-install write-rate quota (max rows/bytes inserted per minute)\n3. Per-tenant storage cap aggregating all snapshot scopes\n4. Admin alerts on threshold (80%)\n5. Auto-disable on quota breach with admin-required resume\n6. Per-query CH settings: `max_execution_time`, `max_rows_to_read`, `max_bytes_to_read`, `max_result_rows`, `max_inserted_rows`","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:11Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740708","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740708"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740708"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740708/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":606,"original_line":606,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":606,"position":606,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740865","pull_request_review_id":4180282606,"id":3146740865,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3SB","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A live snapshot exists for the tenant. The tenant admin has authored a new snapshot (either by editing the live one and saving as a new snapshot, by cloning a vendor or instance reference, or by composing from scratch).\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects \"deploy as shadow\" on the new snapshot\n+2. Plugin runtime instantiates the new snapshot as a *shadow*: provisions per-install ClickHouse users with scoped GRANTs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`), grants each shadow install `SELECT` on the corresponding live install's bronze (per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), creates the new bronze / silver scopes, schedules the shadow plugins' work\n+3. For each plugin in the shadow whose `plugin_id` was also present in the live snapshot, the runtime executes the plugin-author-shipped migration (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`) to populate or expose historical bronze — view, materialized view, full copy, or no migration at the author's choice\n+4. Both snapshots run concurrently. The live snapshot continues to feed widgets and analytics-api; the shadow snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own scopes\n+5. The admin UI exposes a side-by-side comparison view: row counts per silver table, schema diffs, per-column distributional statistics where shapes match (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+6. Adaptation is open-ended. The trial reminder timer (default 7 days, tenant-configurable) surfaces a non-blocking reminder if the shadow has not been promoted or rejected. Reminders escalate by frequency, never by action (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+7. The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT, and the previous (now non-live) snapshot is retained until explicitly retired\n+8. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the new live snapshot\n+\n+**Postconditions**: On promote — the new snapshot is live; the previous snapshot remains running but no longer feeds users; bronze of the previous snapshot is retained until explicit retirement (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`). On reject — the shadow snapshot is uninstalled; its bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects at step 1**: No state change. The new snapshot is saved but not deployed; admin can deploy or discard later.\n+- **A plugin in the shadow fails to install or migrate**: The runtime surfaces the failure; **the live snapshot is untouched**. Admin can retry, edit the snapshot, or discard the shadow.\n+- **Admin REJECTS during adaptation (step 6)**: Runtime uninstalls the shadow snapshot, drops its DB scopes, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT. Tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+- **Trial reminder expires without action**: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts. If the tenant prefers fewer notifications, the reminder cadence is configurable.\n+- **Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot**: This is not a distinct \"rollback\" operation. The admin starts a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history), and follows the same flow. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based, so the same silver plugin accepts any connector whose output shape satisfies its input contract.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+- **A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot**: Silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag. No silver-plugin change is required when a new compatible connector is added.\n+\n+### UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-tenant-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant admin wants a plugin combination not available as a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the snapshot editor in the admin UI\n+2. Admin starts from a fresh canvas, from a clone of the live snapshot, or from a clone of a vendor / instance reference snapshot\n+3. Admin adds, removes, or version-pins individual plugins (the catalog UI surfaces available versions and trust tiers per plugin)\n+4. Admin saves the composition as a new snapshot (immutable; subsequent edits produce a new snapshot, not a mutation)\n+5. Admin deploys the new snapshot as a shadow per UC-003\n+\n+**Postconditions**: A new tenant-owned snapshot is recorded in inventory. The tenant can deploy it now or later. Other snapshots in the tenant's history remain unchanged.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Composition contains a plugin combination flagged by static checks (e.g., two plugins sharing the same `output.tag` in incompatible ways)**: The editor surfaces the issue at save time; the admin can either resolve and resave, or save with a warning. There is no system veto — warnings are non-blocking, but visible.\n+- **Composition includes an unsigned plugin while instance policy disallows unsigned plugins (per `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`)**: Save succeeds, deploy is blocked at the instance level until policy is changed or the plugin is replaced.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to an OCI registry and added to a tenant snapshot through the admin UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the snapshot is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; adding a second connector that produces the expected shape to the same snapshot makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf, nor mutate a tenant's snapshot, through the admin API; force-retire of a known-bad plugin is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason and tenant notification\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two snapshots can coexist in one tenant indefinitely as fully independent data planes (independent bronze / silver scopes, independent per-install ClickHouse users, independent pods); promoting one does not delete the other\n+- [ ] Promoting a new snapshot retains the previous snapshot's bronze until the admin explicitly retires it\n+- [ ] A new snapshot installed as a shadow alongside a live snapshot processes the same connector inputs, exposes a side-by-side comparison view, and never feeds user-facing surfaces until promoted\n+- [ ] A trial reminder timer expires without admin action surfaces a reminder in the UI; no automatic promote, no automatic revert\n+- [ ] Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot is performed by initiating a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot — there is no distinct \"rollback\" API\n+- [ ] Each plugin's transform code (dbt project or equivalent) is self-contained in the plugin directory; attempting to use dbt `ref()` to reach across plugins fails at compile time; a downstream plugin discovers upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract with runtime-injected `sources.yml`\n+- [ ] A plugin install runs under a per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and to discovered upstream bronze for silver); attempting to read or write outside the scope is rejected at the database layer\n+- [ ] A shadow snapshot's installs receive `SELECT` GRANT on the live snapshot's matching bronze for the duration of the shadow only; the GRANT is revoked atomically on promote, reject, or retire\n+- [ ] A plugin install attempted with an image referenced by tag (no digest) is rejected at install time\n+- [ ] A plugin install whose image signature does not chain to a configured trust root is allowed in the unsigned tier with reduced capabilities (iframe widget render, dbt-only silver, gVisor / Kata runtime, curated-registry-only)\n+- [ ] A revoked image digest cannot start new pods even if the install was created before revocation\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can inspect the inventory of all installed plugins across all tenants with per-install health state and per-snapshot grouping (a flat list view satisfies this; a graph visualization is a `SHOULD` per `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` and is not release-gating)\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables are reachable only through the install's per-install user with scoped GRANTs; cross-tenant and cross-install reads are rejected at the database layer, not merely filtered by application code\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin images. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs. Mutable tags are not used — images are pinned by digest | p1 |\n+| Sigstore / cosign infrastructure | Image signing and verification, transparency log, revocation list distribution | p1 |\n+| Instance database (MariaDB) | Stores the per-tenant snapshot inventory, snapshot composition, install config, audit log | p1 |\n+| ClickHouse | Hosts per-snapshot bronze / silver scopes; per-install user RBAC (GRANT/REVOKE), quotas | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles snapshot inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, K8s `NetworkPolicy`, ServiceAccount, ResourceQuota), frontend widget registration | p1 |\n+| Kubernetes `NetworkPolicy` controller | Enforces egress allowlist for plugin pods (baseline isolation) | p1 |\n+| Container runtime classes | Standard `runc` plus `gVisor` or `Kata` available for unsigned-tier plugins; required for kernel isolation | p2 (only needed once unsigned-tier ships) |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for native widget render in vendor- / instance-signed tiers; iframe render in unsigned tier works without it | p2 (widget capability ships when this is available) |\n+| External secret manager | Provides plugin credentials in production; supported integrations include Vault, External Secrets Operator (bridging to AWS / GCP / Azure secret managers), SealedSecrets. Manual-entry fallback for evaluation deployments | p1 (manual-entry suffices for v1, external store is required for prod-grade installs) |\n+| Central catalog service | Hosts plugin and (optionally) reference-snapshot metadata; URL install works without it | p2 |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Snapshot-as-state model: tenant validates the new snapshot in shadow before promoting, on real data, against the old snapshot side-by-side. SemVer hygiene is helpful but no longer load-bearing; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Connector schema conformance enforced at write (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`); silver consumers additionally ship data-quality tests against declared input contracts (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`); plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin image is swapped under the same tag (supply-chain attack) | Malicious code lands on next pull | Images are pinned by digest, never by tag (`cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`); signature verification on every pod admission |\n+| Plugin signing key (vendor or instance) is compromised | All plugins signed by that key become suspect | Sigstore transparency log + revocation list checked on pod admission; per-version signatures so revoking one version does not revoke all; key rotation procedure documented and exercised quarterly |\n+| Plugin reverse-DNS naming collides across registries (`com.cyberfabric.gitea` on dockerhub vs ghcr.io) | Tenant could install the wrong artifact | Trust tier requires signature; instance admin's curated registry allowlist prevents arbitrary registries for vendor-signed and instance-signed tiers; for unsigned, Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Plugin runtime crash leaves an install in a half-applied state | Manual recovery required | Every install/upgrade plan is persisted before application; runtime is reconciliation-based, so restart resumes from persisted state |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-21] Failure handling, retry, and partial-state recovery have no systematic FR family**\n\nThis Risk entry addresses *runtime* crash recovery (reconciliation-based) but says nothing about **plugin** crash, ClickHouse unavailability, network partitions, partial writes, abandoned migrations, or cascading failures across silvers.\n\nCommon failure modes the PRD doesn'''t address as FRs:\n\n- **CH unavailable**: no retry policy, backoff strategy, max-retry, circuit breaker\n- **CH connection timeout mid-INSERT**: no partial-write cleanup; rows may be persisted with no record\n- **CH disk full**: not addressed; INSERT fails mid-stream, state unclear\n- **Plugin crash mid-execution**: in-flight INSERTs, held GRANTs, scratch tables — what happens?\n- **Plugin in crash-loop**: no circuit-breaker after N consecutive failures; pod consumes resources until manual intervention\n- **Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT on a failed shadow**: when revoked? Held forever?\n- **Migration script fails mid-execution**: partial state in target scope? GRANT still active?\n- **Connector fails → silver stale → widget silently outdated**: no staleness signaling beyond \"data temporarily unavailable\"\n\nNo retry/backoff policy specified, no idempotency requirement (so retries are unsafe), no DLQ for poison batches, no transaction boundary specification, no time-to-detect/time-to-isolate NFR.\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR family for failure handling — retry policy in manifest with idempotency claim; circuit breaker after N failures; transactional semantics (silver writes to staging, atomic rename on success); GRANT lifecycle bounded by shadow-deploy state machine (failed shadow → GRANT revoked); migration cleanup contract (failed migration drops what it created); cascade isolation; staleness signaling on widget data; admin-alerting NFR.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:12Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:12Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740865","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740865"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146740865"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146740865/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":982,"original_line":982,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":982,"position":982,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146741051","pull_request_review_id":4180282769,"id":3146741051,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67j3U7","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[HIGH — H-22] TOCTOU between sources.yml resolver write and plugin SQL execute**\n\nThe resolver writes `sources.yml` at time T1, the plugin reads at T2 > T1, the plugin issues SQL at T3 > T2. Between T1 and T3:\n\n- A snapshot can be retired (operational, or via force-retire)\n- A connector can be upgraded as part of another tenant'''s shadow-deploy\n- A table can be dropped or renamed\n- Scope IDs can be reused if the cleanup logic isn'''t careful\n\nPlugin fails with `Table does not exist` (best case), or worse, the schema name resolves to a *different* install'''s data (worst case, if scope IDs are reused or shared name patterns collide).\n\nThe PRD has no atomicity guarantee for the metadata snapshot at transformation start, no version number on `sources.yml`, no validity check at execution time. Distinct from H-9 (atomic flip during promote) — this is the per-run race between the resolver and the plugin.\n\n**Suggested fix:** `sources.yml` carries a metadata-version token (snapshot revision + resolution timestamp + signed by the runtime). Runtime validates the token at SQL execution time (e.g., CH session setting that the plugin must include); if mismatched, the run aborts cleanly rather than reading stale resolution. Best: pre-compute the lockfile at snapshot-authoring time (see C-9), eliminating per-run resolution entirely.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:14Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:01:14Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146741051","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146741051"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146741051"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146741051/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":498,"original_line":498,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":498,"position":498,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146807911","pull_request_review_id":4180362342,"id":3146807911,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kHpn","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-1] Admin API authentication unspecified**\n\nThe catalog API and install API are listed as REST APIs but no FR says how callers are authenticated. Bearer JWT? mTLS? OIDC? Tenant-admin identity is the foundation of the entire authorization model — the snapshot-sovereignty FRs all assume \"the call is from this tenant'''s admin\" — and yet how that identity is verified is missing from the PRD.\n\nWithout an explicit auth FR, every customer deployment makes its own choice; some will pick weakly (basic auth, shared API keys) and the security posture varies by deployment.\n\n**Suggested fix:** P1 FR — admin API authentication via OIDC integration (instance-configurable IdP) with required scopes per operation; tokens carry `tenant_id` and `role` claims; validated against signed JWKS; rate-limited per token.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:25Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:25Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146807911","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146807911"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146807911"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146807911/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":744,"original_line":744,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":744,"position":744,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808088","pull_request_review_id":4180362505,"id":3146808088,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kHsY","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-2] No separation of duties on the tenant-admin role**\n\nA single tenant admin can: author a snapshot, deploy as shadow, promote to live, force-uninstall, retire. No four-eyes principle, no step-up auth for promote, no separation between \"compose\" and \"deploy\" roles. Compromise of one tenant-admin account = full plugin-surface control for that tenant — every install, every secret config, every shadow deploy.\n\nFor an enterprise tenant managing critical data flows, this lack of role separation is uncomfortable. The current design is effectively single-key-to-the-kingdom at tenant scope.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Optional but recommended (configurable per tenant): separate roles for \"snapshot author\" (can edit, save, deploy as shadow) and \"snapshot promoter\" (can promote shadow → live, reject, retire). Step-up auth (re-prompt for password / 2FA) on promote and force-retire actions.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:27Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:27Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808088","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808088"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808088"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808088/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":168,"original_line":168,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":168,"position":168,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808228","pull_request_review_id":4180362641,"id":3146808228,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kHuk","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-3] SBOM and SLSA build provenance not required**\n\nThe signing FR proves \"this digest was signed by this key\" but not \"this digest came from the source code I claim.\" A compromised build pipeline produces a properly-signed image with backdoors — the signature is valid, the image is malicious. Image scanning catches *known* CVEs in OS packages, not embedded malware in custom code.\n\nModern supply-chain hygiene requires:\n- **SLSA L2+ build provenance attestation**: ties the image to a specific, reproducible build pipeline\n- **CycloneDX (or SPDX) SBOM**: machine-readable bill of materials for vulnerability tracking and license compliance\n\nNeither is mentioned in the PRD.\n\n**Suggested fix:** SLSA L2+ provenance attestation required for vendor-signed and instance-signed tiers (verified at install time, same pipeline as cosign signature verification); CycloneDX SBOM published alongside the image and referenced by the manifest; SBOM diff-checked at upgrade for new high-severity vulnerabilities.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:28Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:28Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808228","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808228"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808228"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808228/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":656,"original_line":656,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":656,"position":656,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808382","pull_request_review_id":4180362769,"id":3146808382,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kHw-","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-4] Signature freshness / re-attestation not required**\n\nA signature once made is valid forever (until revoked). An old signed image with newly-discovered vulnerabilities remains \"trusted\" by signature alone. There'''s no requirement that signatures be refreshed periodically or re-attested with current scan results.\n\nIn practice, a vendor-signed plugin image from 18 months ago — with all the CVEs disclosed in the meantime — passes signature verification today and runs with full vendor-tier capabilities.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Signatures expire (e.g., 90 days). Re-attestation requires re-running scans against current threat intel. Revocation (mentioned in this section) is the lower bound; expiry forces a periodic re-affirmation that the image is still considered safe.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:30Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808382","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808382"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808382"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808382/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":652,"original_line":652,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":652,"position":652,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808561","pull_request_review_id":4180362931,"id":3146808561,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kHzx","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-5] Ingress and pod-to-pod NetworkPolicy missing**\n\nThe baseline isolation FR describes egress only. Plugin pods may listen on ports — widget microfrontend serving, health probes, sidecar communication. There'''s no requirement for a default-deny ingress policy or pod-to-pod restrictions.\n\nThe risk: two malicious plugins in the same tenant can communicate to split capabilities (\"widget A reads JWT through host SDK, ships it to widget B via pod-to-pod, widget B has wider egress allowlist\"). Without ingress NetworkPolicy, pod-to-pod traffic in the same namespace is open by default in Kubernetes.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Add to baseline isolation: default-deny ingress on plugin pods; per-pod ingress allowlist sourced from manifest (e.g., widget pod accepts ingress only from the host SDK service); no pod-to-pod traffic between distinct installs unless explicitly declared in both manifests.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:32Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:32Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808561","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808561"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808561"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808561/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":697,"original_line":697,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":697,"position":697,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808689","pull_request_review_id":4180363088,"id":3146808689,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kH1x","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-6] Encryption in transit not specified**\n\nClickHouse, plugin runtime API, Argo, secret manager — none of the inter-service comms have explicit TLS or mTLS requirements in the PRD. The \"scoped, per-install credential\" mentioned here is great — but if it'''s sent over plaintext to ClickHouse, the credential leaks to anyone on the cluster network.\n\nIn a typical K8s deployment without explicit TLS, traffic between pods is unencrypted. Service-mesh (Istio/Linkerd) auto-mTLS isn'''t universal.\n\n**Suggested fix:** TLS for all inter-service traffic; mTLS for plugin pod ↔ runtime API channels (covers H-14); CH connections use TLS with cert verification; secret manager API calls over TLS only. Listed as a baseline isolation requirement, not assumed.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:33Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808689","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808689"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808689"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808689/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":213,"original_line":213,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":213,"position":213,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808848","pull_request_review_id":4180363361,"id":3146808848,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kH4Q","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-7] Encryption at rest, CSPRNG quality not specified**\n\nPer-install passwords described as \"cryptographically random\" — but no spec on:\n- RNG source (crypto/rand vs `math/rand`? `/dev/urandom` vs `/dev/random`?)\n- Length (64 bits is brute-forceable; 256 bits is the modern minimum)\n- Entropy quality\n\nAlso missing from the PRD:\n- ClickHouse at-rest encryption mode (CH supports it but it'''s opt-in)\n- K8s Secret encryption-at-rest (etcd default is base64, not encrypted; KMS provider is opt-in)\n- Inventory DB encryption-at-rest\n\n**Suggested fix:** CSPRNG (Go `crypto/rand`, equivalent in other languages) with minimum 256-bit entropy for all generated secrets. ClickHouse and K8s secrets encrypted at rest with KMS-managed keys; KMS choice listed as a P1 dependency. Inventory DB encrypted at rest. Specified as baseline isolation requirements.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:35Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:35Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808848","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808848"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808848"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808848/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":599,"original_line":599,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":599,"position":599,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808996","pull_request_review_id":4180363522,"id":3146808996,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kH6k","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-8] Manual-entry secret rotation has no automation / expiry tracking**\n\nExternal secret manager paths (Vault, ESO, SealedSecrets) automate rotation. The manual-entry path here requires human re-entry — and the FR says secrets MUST be \"rotated atomically\" but for the manual path there'''s no:\n- Expiry tracking (when was this entered?)\n- Nag (this is N days old, please rotate)\n- Forced re-entry past a threshold\n- Audit of who last touched which manual secret\n\nLong-lived manually-entered secrets quietly age, and \"atomic rotation\" becomes \"the admin remembers to re-paste once a year.\"\n\n**Suggested fix:** Manual-entry secrets carry an `expires_at` field (configurable per-instance default); UI nag at threshold (e.g., 60 days); force re-entry past expiry (install pauses until re-supplied); audit log entry on every read/write.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:37Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808996","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808996"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146808996"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146808996/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":587,"original_line":587,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":587,"position":587,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809137","pull_request_review_id":4180363656,"id":3146809137,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kH8x","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-9] No anomaly detection / behavioral monitoring requirement**\n\nThis FR covers operational status (sync progress, errors, test failures). It does NOT require behavioral / anomaly monitoring of the plugin itself.\n\nA plugin issuing 10K queries/min against `system.tables`, generating 10GB of egress, scanning bronze scopes outside its discovery resolution, opening unusual connection patterns — none of this is required to be detected or surfaced. The model is \"preventive controls only, no detection.\" Detection is the second leg of defense-in-depth and the PRD has only the first.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Per-install metrics collected as part of status: query rate, query bytes read/written, egress volume, distinct destinations, secret reads, K8s API attempts (should always be zero per baseline isolation — non-zero is an alarm). Baseline anomaly detection on top with admin-visible alerts.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:38Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:38Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809137","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809137"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809137"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809137/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":616,"original_line":616,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":616,"position":616,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809263","pull_request_review_id":4180363803,"id":3146809263,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kH-v","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-10] K8s namespace per tenant implied but not required**\n\nThis FR says installs are tenant-scoped but doesn'''t specify that each tenant gets its own K8s namespace. Without per-tenant namespace isolation:\n\n- NetworkPolicies need explicit cross-namespace rules (or risk being too permissive within the shared namespace)\n- ResourceQuotas need to reference labels rather than namespace defaults — easier to misconfigure\n- ServiceAccount / Role bindings need cross-namespace selectors\n- A misconfigured plugin in tenant A can reach tenant B'''s pods at the K8s level (regardless of CH GRANTs)\n\n**Suggested fix:** Per-tenant K8s namespace as a P1 baseline isolation requirement. All NetworkPolicies, ResourceQuotas, ServiceAccounts, and Secrets scoped to that namespace; cross-namespace traffic explicitly denied at the CNI layer.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:40Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809263","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809263"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809263"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809263/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":286,"original_line":286,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":286,"position":286,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809375","pull_request_review_id":4180363936,"id":3146809375,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIAf","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-11] Catalog metadata trust independent from image signing**\n\nThe marketplace UI displays catalog metadata: description, screenshots, capability list, attribution. If the catalog service is compromised but signing is not, an attacker swaps metadata to deceive users — \"free GitLab connector\" (correctly signed, benign) re-described as \"Slack connector\" to phish credentials, or vice versa.\n\nImage signing protects the image. Catalog metadata is a separate trust surface that'''s not covered.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Catalog metadata signed by the same key that signs the image (so vendor-signed plugins have vendor-signed metadata), or fetched directly from the image'''s manifest at display time (so the marketplace UI always shows what the manifest declares, not what the catalog service stored). Tampering with the catalog service alone shouldn'''t change what users see about a plugin.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:41Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809375","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809375"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809375"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809375/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":262,"original_line":262,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":262,"position":262,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809498","pull_request_review_id":4180364122,"id":3146809498,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kICa","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-12] Revocation only blocks new pod admissions, not running pods**\n\n> \"A revoked digest MUST prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\"\n\nBut existing pods keep running until restart. For a fast-spreading compromise (key leaked, malicious version pushed, discovered hours later), this is too slow — every running instance keeps executing the malicious code until its next routine restart, which may be days for long-running connectors.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Revocation triggers immediate pod termination across the cluster — not lazy on next admission. The plugin runtime watches the revocation list and terminates running pods whose digest is revoked within N seconds (configurable, e.g., 60s). Tenant admins are notified that an install was forcibly stopped.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:43Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:43Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809498","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809498"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809498"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809498/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":650,"original_line":650,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":650,"position":650,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809658","pull_request_review_id":4180364282,"id":3146809658,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIE6","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-13] Connector-schema-conformance is MUST-with-SHOULD — non-actionable**\n\n> \"The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce ... at write time\"\n> \"The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\"\n\nBoth implementation paths are SHOULD. If the proxy is SHOULD and the DDL mirror is SHOULD, what'''s the MUST mechanism? The FR is non-actionable as written — a customer deployment that does neither is \"compliant\" with the SHOULD but violates the MUST.\n\nThis is a critical FR (it'''s the producer-side defense paired with `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`). It needs an actual concrete enforcement.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Pick one implementation and make it MUST: either (a) a schema-validating proxy that intercepts INSERTs from plugin pods to CH, OR (b) ClickHouse table DDL mirroring the manifest with strict column types. Either is mechanically enforceable; \"either or neither\" is not.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:44Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:44Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809658","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809658"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809658"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809658/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":707,"original_line":707,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":707,"position":707,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809786","pull_request_review_id":4180364398,"id":3146809786,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIG6","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-14] Image scan severity threshold deferred — inconsistent enforcement across instances**\n\nOpen Question line 1008: \"CVE severity threshold for hard-block vs warn\" is open. Until specified, every customer instance picks its own threshold — meaning a vendor-signed image scanning as MEDIUM CVEs may pass on lax instances and fail on strict ones. The same plugin, same digest, same signature, different acceptance.\n\nFor vendor-signed plugins this creates a support nightmare: \"the connector works at customer A but not at customer B\" with the difference being scan policy, not the plugin.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Default policy specified in the PRD (e.g., hard-block on HIGH+, warn on MEDIUM, ignore on LOW); customizable per instance with explicit deviation log. Consistency across customer instances becomes the default; explicit override is a documented choice.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:46Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:46Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809786","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809786"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809786"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809786/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":673,"original_line":673,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":673,"position":673,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809915","pull_request_review_id":4180364541,"id":3146809915,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kII7","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A live snapshot exists for the tenant. The tenant admin has authored a new snapshot (either by editing the live one and saving as a new snapshot, by cloning a vendor or instance reference, or by composing from scratch).\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects \"deploy as shadow\" on the new snapshot\n+2. Plugin runtime instantiates the new snapshot as a *shadow*: provisions per-install ClickHouse users with scoped GRANTs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`), grants each shadow install `SELECT` on the corresponding live install's bronze (per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), creates the new bronze / silver scopes, schedules the shadow plugins' work\n+3. For each plugin in the shadow whose `plugin_id` was also present in the live snapshot, the runtime executes the plugin-author-shipped migration (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`) to populate or expose historical bronze — view, materialized view, full copy, or no migration at the author's choice\n+4. Both snapshots run concurrently. The live snapshot continues to feed widgets and analytics-api; the shadow snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own scopes\n+5. The admin UI exposes a side-by-side comparison view: row counts per silver table, schema diffs, per-column distributional statistics where shapes match (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+6. Adaptation is open-ended. The trial reminder timer (default 7 days, tenant-configurable) surfaces a non-blocking reminder if the shadow has not been promoted or rejected. Reminders escalate by frequency, never by action (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+7. The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT, and the previous (now non-live) snapshot is retained until explicitly retired\n+8. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the new live snapshot\n+\n+**Postconditions**: On promote — the new snapshot is live; the previous snapshot remains running but no longer feeds users; bronze of the previous snapshot is retained until explicit retirement (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`). On reject — the shadow snapshot is uninstalled; its bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects at step 1**: No state change. The new snapshot is saved but not deployed; admin can deploy or discard later.\n+- **A plugin in the shadow fails to install or migrate**: The runtime surfaces the failure; **the live snapshot is untouched**. Admin can retry, edit the snapshot, or discard the shadow.\n+- **Admin REJECTS during adaptation (step 6)**: Runtime uninstalls the shadow snapshot, drops its DB scopes, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT. Tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+- **Trial reminder expires without action**: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts. If the tenant prefers fewer notifications, the reminder cadence is configurable.\n+- **Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot**: This is not a distinct \"rollback\" operation. The admin starts a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history), and follows the same flow. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based, so the same silver plugin accepts any connector whose output shape satisfies its input contract.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+- **A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot**: Silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag. No silver-plugin change is required when a new compatible connector is added.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-15] Tag fragility — typo causes silent data loss with no alert**\n\n> \"A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot: Silver discovery picks it up automatically...\"\n\nThe flip side: a typo in `output.tag` (e.g., `core_metric` vs `core_metrics`) makes silver discovery quietly miss the source. No compile error, no runtime alert. Dashboard shows \"no data\" or \"stale data\" without anyone knowing why.\n\nThis is the standard pitfall of stringly-typed implicit contracts. dbt'''s `ref()` catches this at compile time; tag-based discovery doesn'''t.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Tag schemas declared centrally and validated at snapshot-author time; explicit alert when a silver plugin'''s discovery resolves to fewer sources than the previous snapshot run (regression detection); CI tooling that warns on tag mismatch before deploy. Combined with C-7 (tag namespacing): tags within a plugin'''s reverse-DNS namespace are auto-completable in the editor, reducing typo surface.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:48Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:48Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809915","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809915"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146809915"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146809915/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":898,"original_line":898,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":898,"position":898,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810022","pull_request_review_id":4180364683,"id":3146810022,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIKm","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-16] Manual-entry secret leakage paths**\n\nThe \"MUST NOT be logged\" rule covers server-side logging. But manual-entered secrets leak through other paths the PRD doesn'''t address:\n\n- App request logs (if request body is logged at all, secret appears as POST data)\n- DB backups (if encryption-at-rest key is in the same backup, the secret is recoverable)\n- UI history / form autofill (browser stores submitted values)\n- Browser back-button (cached form data)\n- Screenshare during onboarding (\"can you walk me through entering the API token?\")\n- Browser dev-tools network tab (visible to anyone with access to the user'''s browser)\n- Crash dumps / error logs that include request context\n\n**Suggested fix:** Secret entry forms use `autocomplete=\"off\"`; never echo back the value in the UI; encrypted at the API boundary (HTTPS + payload-level encryption with the instance'''s public key, decrypted server-side with the private key); request body redacted in all logs at the API gateway; runbook for incident response includes \"secret leaked via screenshare/log.\"","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:49Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810022","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810022"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810022"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810022/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":589,"original_line":589,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":589,"position":589,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810154","pull_request_review_id":4180364807,"id":3146810154,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIMq","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-17] `sources.yml` injection mechanism unspecified — read-only rootfs vs runtime-written file**\n\nBaseline isolation requires read-only rootfs. But the runtime must write `sources.yml` into the plugin'''s project directory before each run (per the isolated-transform-project FR line 498). These two rules conflict unless explicitly reconciled:\n\n- If the project dir is in a writable volume → not signed, mutable post-image deployment, tamperable\n- If an init container writes it before main container starts → still an unsigned config file the plugin trusts as authoritative\n- If the runtime writes via API call → adds new attack surface (who validates the API caller is the runtime?)\n\nThe `sources.yml` content is **not** part of the signed image but determines which tables the plugin'''s SQL reads from. So the plugin'''s \"signed SQL\" + \"unsigned table resolution\" = effectively unsigned data flow.\n\n**Suggested fix:** `sources.yml` written via an init container into a `tmpfs` mount at a defined path; signed by a runtime-managed signing key (separate from image signing); plugin verifies signature before reading; plugin SDK provides a helper that validates and parses. Closes the read-only-rootfs vs runtime-injection contradiction.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:51Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:51Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810154","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810154"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810154"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810154/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":695,"original_line":695,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":695,"position":695,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810291","pull_request_review_id":4180364931,"id":3146810291,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIOz","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-18] Silver output schema not enforced at write — no parallel to connector-schema-conformance**\n\nConnectors get `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance` (line 707): runtime rejects rows that don'''t match the declared output. Silver gets no equivalent FR. A silver plugin'''s manifest declares output columns and types, but if the actual SQL writes different columns or types (extra columns, wrong types, NULLs where NOT NULL was declared), nothing rejects it.\n\nDownstream consumers (widgets, silver-of-silver, analytics-api) see drift with no enforcement point. The rationale for connector-schema-conformance (\"fail loudly at write rather than corrupt and require silver tests to catch the symptom\") applies symmetrically to silver — but silver'''s consumer is widgets / API, which don'''t ship dbt tests.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Add an FR mirroring connector-schema-conformance for silver output: runtime enforces declared silver output schema at write time; rows that don'''t conform rejected; surfaced as silver-transform failure to admin.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:52Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810291","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810291"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810291"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810291/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":449,"original_line":449,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":449,"position":449,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810399","pull_request_review_id":4180365052,"id":3146810399,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIQf","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-19] \"dbt only\" for unsigned tier mechanically unenforceable**\n\n\"Silver execution engine: dbt only\" for unsigned tier — but dbt is just a templating engine + SQL executor. It'''s not a sandbox.\n\n- dbt macros are Jinja2 with arbitrary expressions\n- dbt `pre-hook`, `post-hook`, `on-run-start`, `on-run-end` execute arbitrary SQL\n- dbt `operation` macros run ad-hoc SQL outside of model graph\n- A \"dbt-only\" plugin can issue `SELECT * FROM system.tables`, `INSERT INTO ...` to any granted scope, register UDFs (if granted), use `url()` table function (if granted) — all from inside legitimate dbt syntax\n\nThe restriction would have to be enforced via image scanning (\"is this a dbt project image?\" — defeated by including dbt in any image), manifest declaration trusted because signed (but unsigned plugins by definition aren'''t signed), or runtime AST inspection of the SQL dbt produces (not specified, very hard).\n\n**Suggested fix:** Either remove the \"dbt only\" claim, OR define a concrete enforcement: a sandbox sidecar that intercepts CH connections from the plugin pod, parses every SQL statement, validates against an allowlist (no `system.*`, no table functions, no UDF creation, no settings overrides), forwards only validated statements to CH. That'''s a real enforcement; \"dbt only\" by itself is just a label.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:54Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:54Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810399","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810399"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810399"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810399/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":676,"original_line":676,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":676,"position":676,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810526","pull_request_review_id":4180365200,"id":3146810526,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kISe","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-20] ClickHouse dangerous functions / table functions not denylisted**\n\nThe per-install GRANT model gives `INSERT/SELECT/ALTER` on the install'''s scope. ClickHouse exposes powerful primitives that, if not explicitly DENY-ed, are accessible to anyone with `SELECT` privilege:\n\n- Table functions: `url()`, `file()`, `s3()`, `remote()`, `cluster()`, `mysql()`, `postgresql()`, `hdfs()`, `kafka()` — all enable network egress or data import bypassing the plugin'''s declared egress allowlist\n- Executable UDFs (with `allow_executable_user_defined_functions`) — RCE in CH'''s context\n- Settings: `allow_introspection_functions`, `allow_ddl`, `format_csv_allow_*` — change query behavior\n- `system.*` tables beyond what the plugin needs (`system.users`, `system.grants`, `system.processes`, `system.metrics`)\n\nThese are exploit primitives. The PRD'''s GRANT enumeration doesn'''t address them.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Per-install user explicitly denied via ClickHouse `settings_profile`: `url()`, `file()`, `s3()`, `remote()`, `cluster()`, `mysql()`, `postgresql()`, `hdfs()`, `kafka()` table functions; `allow_introspection_functions = 0`; `allow_executable_user_defined_functions = 0`; `system.*` access limited to `system.tables` filtered to the install'''s own scope.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:55Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:55Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810526","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810526"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810526"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810526/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":691,"original_line":691,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":691,"position":691,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810664","pull_request_review_id":4180365336,"id":3146810664,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIUo","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-21] Resolver performance scaling — sources.yml generation is hot path on CH metadata**\n\nAt the stated NFR (200 installs total) this isn'''t a problem. At realistic growth (thousands of tenants × dozens of plugins each), the resolver generates `sources.yml` per run × per plugin × per tenant. Each generation:\n\n- Queries the central catalog\n- Probably queries ClickHouse `system.tables` (or equivalent metadata)\n- Evaluates discovery rules (regex/glob match against table inventory)\n- Writes the file to plugin pod context\n\nThis becomes a hot path on CH metadata for every Argo workflow start. Cold-start latency for transforms scales with active discovery work. The PRD has an NFR for install latency (10 min p95) but nothing for transform-start latency, so this can degrade silently.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Cache resolved `sources.yml` per (snapshot, install) tuple; invalidate only on snapshot mutation (which never happens in v1.3 since snapshots are immutable). Bound resolver QPS. Add NFR for transform-start latency. Best: pre-compute the resolution at snapshot-authoring time (per C-9 lockfile) — eliminates the hot path entirely.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:57Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:57Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810664","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810664"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810664"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810664/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":498,"original_line":498,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":498,"position":498,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810792","pull_request_review_id":4180365482,"id":3146810792,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIWo","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A live snapshot exists for the tenant. The tenant admin has authored a new snapshot (either by editing the live one and saving as a new snapshot, by cloning a vendor or instance reference, or by composing from scratch).\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects \"deploy as shadow\" on the new snapshot\n+2. Plugin runtime instantiates the new snapshot as a *shadow*: provisions per-install ClickHouse users with scoped GRANTs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`), grants each shadow install `SELECT` on the corresponding live install's bronze (per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), creates the new bronze / silver scopes, schedules the shadow plugins' work\n+3. For each plugin in the shadow whose `plugin_id` was also present in the live snapshot, the runtime executes the plugin-author-shipped migration (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`) to populate or expose historical bronze — view, materialized view, full copy, or no migration at the author's choice\n+4. Both snapshots run concurrently. The live snapshot continues to feed widgets and analytics-api; the shadow snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own scopes\n+5. The admin UI exposes a side-by-side comparison view: row counts per silver table, schema diffs, per-column distributional statistics where shapes match (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+6. Adaptation is open-ended. The trial reminder timer (default 7 days, tenant-configurable) surfaces a non-blocking reminder if the shadow has not been promoted or rejected. Reminders escalate by frequency, never by action (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+7. The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT, and the previous (now non-live) snapshot is retained until explicitly retired\n+8. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the new live snapshot\n+\n+**Postconditions**: On promote — the new snapshot is live; the previous snapshot remains running but no longer feeds users; bronze of the previous snapshot is retained until explicit retirement (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`). On reject — the shadow snapshot is uninstalled; its bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects at step 1**: No state change. The new snapshot is saved but not deployed; admin can deploy or discard later.\n+- **A plugin in the shadow fails to install or migrate**: The runtime surfaces the failure; **the live snapshot is untouched**. Admin can retry, edit the snapshot, or discard the shadow.\n+- **Admin REJECTS during adaptation (step 6)**: Runtime uninstalls the shadow snapshot, drops its DB scopes, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT. Tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+- **Trial reminder expires without action**: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts. If the tenant prefers fewer notifications, the reminder cadence is configurable.\n+- **Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot**: This is not a distinct \"rollback\" operation. The admin starts a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history), and follows the same flow. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based, so the same silver plugin accepts any connector whose output shape satisfies its input contract.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+- **A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot**: Silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag. No silver-plugin change is required when a new compatible connector is added.\n+\n+### UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-tenant-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant admin wants a plugin combination not available as a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the snapshot editor in the admin UI\n+2. Admin starts from a fresh canvas, from a clone of the live snapshot, or from a clone of a vendor / instance reference snapshot\n+3. Admin adds, removes, or version-pins individual plugins (the catalog UI surfaces available versions and trust tiers per plugin)\n+4. Admin saves the composition as a new snapshot (immutable; subsequent edits produce a new snapshot, not a mutation)\n+5. Admin deploys the new snapshot as a shadow per UC-003\n+\n+**Postconditions**: A new tenant-owned snapshot is recorded in inventory. The tenant can deploy it now or later. Other snapshots in the tenant's history remain unchanged.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Composition contains a plugin combination flagged by static checks (e.g., two plugins sharing the same `output.tag` in incompatible ways)**: The editor surfaces the issue at save time; the admin can either resolve and resave, or save with a warning. There is no system veto — warnings are non-blocking, but visible.\n+- **Composition includes an unsigned plugin while instance policy disallows unsigned plugins (per `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`)**: Save succeeds, deploy is blocked at the instance level until policy is changed or the plugin is replaced.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to an OCI registry and added to a tenant snapshot through the admin UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the snapshot is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; adding a second connector that produces the expected shape to the same snapshot makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf, nor mutate a tenant's snapshot, through the admin API; force-retire of a known-bad plugin is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason and tenant notification\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two snapshots can coexist in one tenant indefinitely as fully independent data planes (independent bronze / silver scopes, independent per-install ClickHouse users, independent pods); promoting one does not delete the other\n+- [ ] Promoting a new snapshot retains the previous snapshot's bronze until the admin explicitly retires it\n+- [ ] A new snapshot installed as a shadow alongside a live snapshot processes the same connector inputs, exposes a side-by-side comparison view, and never feeds user-facing surfaces until promoted\n+- [ ] A trial reminder timer expires without admin action surfaces a reminder in the UI; no automatic promote, no automatic revert\n+- [ ] Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot is performed by initiating a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot — there is no distinct \"rollback\" API\n+- [ ] Each plugin's transform code (dbt project or equivalent) is self-contained in the plugin directory; attempting to use dbt `ref()` to reach across plugins fails at compile time; a downstream plugin discovers upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract with runtime-injected `sources.yml`\n+- [ ] A plugin install runs under a per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and to discovered upstream bronze for silver); attempting to read or write outside the scope is rejected at the database layer\n+- [ ] A shadow snapshot's installs receive `SELECT` GRANT on the live snapshot's matching bronze for the duration of the shadow only; the GRANT is revoked atomically on promote, reject, or retire\n+- [ ] A plugin install attempted with an image referenced by tag (no digest) is rejected at install time\n+- [ ] A plugin install whose image signature does not chain to a configured trust root is allowed in the unsigned tier with reduced capabilities (iframe widget render, dbt-only silver, gVisor / Kata runtime, curated-registry-only)\n+- [ ] A revoked image digest cannot start new pods even if the install was created before revocation\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can inspect the inventory of all installed plugins across all tenants with per-install health state and per-snapshot grouping (a flat list view satisfies this; a graph visualization is a `SHOULD` per `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` and is not release-gating)\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables are reachable only through the install's per-install user with scoped GRANTs; cross-tenant and cross-install reads are rejected at the database layer, not merely filtered by application code\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin images. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs. Mutable tags are not used — images are pinned by digest | p1 |\n+| Sigstore / cosign infrastructure | Image signing and verification, transparency log, revocation list distribution | p1 |\n+| Instance database (MariaDB) | Stores the per-tenant snapshot inventory, snapshot composition, install config, audit log | p1 |\n+| ClickHouse | Hosts per-snapshot bronze / silver scopes; per-install user RBAC (GRANT/REVOKE), quotas | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles snapshot inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, K8s `NetworkPolicy`, ServiceAccount, ResourceQuota), frontend widget registration | p1 |\n+| Kubernetes `NetworkPolicy` controller | Enforces egress allowlist for plugin pods (baseline isolation) | p1 |\n+| Container runtime classes | Standard `runc` plus `gVisor` or `Kata` available for unsigned-tier plugins; required for kernel isolation | p2 (only needed once unsigned-tier ships) |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-22] Argo time-scheduled, not data-aware — race conditions for chained silvers**\n\nArgo schedules plugins by cron. If silver-B'''s input contract matches silver-A'''s output (silver-of-silver), B'''s run at 14:00 can fire before A'''s 14:00 run completes — B reads stale data. The PRD'''s `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project` (line 494) explicitly forbids cross-plugin DAG (\"each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation\").\n\nThe trade-off documented: \"cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution).\" But the PRD doesn'''t actually specify *what* in the scheduling layer ensures readiness. Pure cron doesn'''t. Result: dashboards show inconsistent data when silvers chain.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Silver MAY declare an upstream readiness predicate in its manifest (e.g., `inputs_updated_within: 5m` or `wait_for: [{tag: \"git_bronze\"}]`); runtime evaluates the predicate before scheduling; if not satisfied, the run is deferred up to a configurable budget. Keeps each plugin'''s transform isolated while making chained pipelines reliable.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:58Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:12:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810792","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810792"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810792"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810792/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":958,"original_line":958,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":958,"position":958,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810918","pull_request_review_id":4180365612,"id":3146810918,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIYm","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A plugin holding broad ClickHouse credentials can read any tenant's data, write into any tenant's scope, enumerate `system.tables`, and pivot via stored procedures regardless of any application-level policy. Per-install scoped GRANTs make those misuses *cryptographically impossible* at the database layer rather than enforced by application code. Closes the data-plane access concerns raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.8 Observability and Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Plugins Report Operational Status\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-status-reporting`\n+\n+Every installed plugin **MUST** report its operational status to the plugin runtime: connector sync progress and errors, silver transform run outcomes and data-test failures, widget render failures. The plugin system **MUST** make this status available to the instance admin through the admin UI.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without telemetry flowing back, \"the dashboard is wrong\" degenerates into a manual hunt across four systems. Centralizing status inside the plugin inventory gives admins a single pane of glass.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Dependency Graph Surfaced to Admins\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui`\n+\n+Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to view the dependency graph across all installed plugins with per-node health state (healthy, degraded, broken, upgrade available, in-shadow-trial). Tenant admins **SHOULD** see the subset of the graph relevant to their tenant, including any shadow-deploy trials currently in flight.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin ecosystems grow dense fast, and the system is explicitly non-auto-healing (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) — failures and conflicts must be investigated by a human. A visual graph is a scalable way to find \"what is broken and what does it block.\" Note: the side-by-side **comparison view** during a shadow-deploy trial is a separate `MUST` already covered by `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; this FR is the broader cross-plugin / cross-tenant graph, kept at `SHOULD` because a flat list view of installs with health badges is sufficient for v1 operability. A graph visualization is the right long-term shape but not release-gating.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### End-User Error Surface Is Minimal\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-end-user-error`\n+\n+Non-admin users **MUST NOT** be exposed to plugin-internal error detail. When plugin state prevents a dashboard or widget from rendering accurately, end users **MUST** see a generic \"data temporarily unavailable\" indicator; plugin-level diagnostics are surfaced only to admins.\n+\n+**Rationale**: End users cannot act on plugin stack traces. Showing them is both a security concern (leaks plugin internals) and a UX regression.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.9 Security and Trust Model\n+\n+#### Image Signing and Digest Pinning Are Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** reference its container image by **digest** (`@sha256:<...>`) — never by mutable tag. The plugin runtime **MUST** reject any install whose manifest pins the image by tag alone. Every install **MUST** also be accompanied by a cryptographic signature over that digest (Sigstore / cosign or equivalent). The plugin runtime **MUST** verify the signature against the configured trust roots (see `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`) at install time and at every pod admission, and **MUST** reject pods whose image digest does not match a verified signature.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** support a revocation list (Sigstore transparency log + locally-cached revocation entries from configured signing authorities). A revoked digest **MUST** prevent further pod admissions for that image, even if the install was created before revocation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Mutable tags are how supply-chain attacks slip in: republish under the same tag, the next pull picks up the malicious image. Pinning by digest forecloses this. Signature verification ensures the digest we install is the digest the author intended to ship. Revocation closes the window between \"we discovered the key was compromised\" and \"every running deployment stops trusting it.\" All three together are the minimum viable supply-chain defense for a system that runs third-party code with cluster-internal data access.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Trust Tiers Modulate Capabilities Above a Common Baseline\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** classify every plugin install into one of three trust tiers, based on whose key signed the image:\n+\n+- **Vendor-signed**: signed by Insight's official signing key, distributed in the instance's bundled trust roots.\n+- **Instance-signed**: signed by a key the instance admin has explicitly registered as an additional trust root for this instance (used for in-house plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org).\n+- **Unsigned**: any install whose signature does not chain to a configured trust root. Unsigned installs are still allowed (this is not a gate on installation), but they run in a more restricted capability profile.\n+\n+The capability profile **MUST** modulate the following dimensions per tier; baseline isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) applies regardless of tier:\n+\n+| Dimension | Vendor-signed | Instance-signed | Unsigned |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| Image registry | any registry on the instance allowlist | any registry on the instance allowlist | Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Image-scan policy | required (Trivy / Grype or equivalent) | required | required + size limit |\n+| Widget render mode | native React component (full DOM) | native | cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` attribute; communicates via `postMessage` only |\n+| Widget access to user JWT | through host SDK proxy | through host SDK proxy | **never** — JWT is never exposed cross-origin |\n+| Silver execution engine | any (dbt / Rust / Python / WASM) | any | dbt only |\n+| Container runtime class | runc | runc | gVisor or Kata (kernel isolation) |\n+| Egress proxy strictness | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist accepted | manifest allowlist + tenant-admin approve at install |\n+| Cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT (for migrations) | granted automatically | granted automatically | requires explicit tenant-admin approval per install |\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust modulates the *delta above baseline*, not whether the plugin can run at all. Two-tier \"trusted vs untrusted\" is too coarse for enterprise — internal plugins authored by the customer's own engineering org need higher capabilities than community plugins, but the customer cannot get the vendor's signing key. Three tiers map to three real authoring contexts (vendor / customer / community). The capability matrix is fixed in the PRD so it is auditable and not negotiable per-deployment; the alternative — \"instance admin chooses which capabilities each tier gets\" — yields too many configuration surfaces and incident vectors.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Baseline Isolation Applies to Every Plugin Regardless of Trust Tier\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`\n+\n+Independent of trust tier, every plugin install **MUST** run under the following baseline isolation. None of these may be relaxed by signing.\n+\n+- **ClickHouse access**: per-install user with capability-scoped GRANTs only (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`). The `default` user is forbidden.\n+- **Network egress**: a `NetworkPolicy` derived from the manifest's declared egress allowlist is applied to the plugin's pods. Outbound to anything not on the allowlist is dropped at the network layer. There is no \"trusted plugin can talk to anywhere\" mode — every egress destination is declared and visible to the admin.\n+- **Compute quotas**: CPU and memory `requests` / `limits` from the manifest, capped by per-tenant and per-instance defaults configured by the instance admin. A single plugin cannot DoS the cluster regardless of its provenance.\n+- **Kubernetes API access**: the default `ServiceAccount` for plugin pods has no role bindings — the plugin cannot list pods, read secrets it does not own, or interact with the K8s API in any way. Plugins that need to spawn jobs (e.g., to invoke Argo) do so through the plugin runtime's API, not directly against the K8s API.\n+- **Filesystem**: pod root filesystem is read-only; only declared volume mounts (typically a per-install `emptyDir` for transient data) are writable.\n+- **Per-install bronze / silver scopes**: each install writes only into its own scope (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`). Cross-scope writes are not possible because the per-install GRANTs do not cover them.\n+- **No host networking, no privileged containers, no `hostPath` mounts.**\n+\n+**Rationale**: Trust-tier modulation is meaningful only on top of a credible baseline. A \"trusted\" plugin that can't run a backdoor egress is still trustworthy because of the baseline; an \"untrusted\" plugin that *can* run a backdoor egress is dangerous regardless of tier. The baseline is the foundation; tiers are the delta.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Connector Schema Conformance Enforced at Write\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** enforce the connector's declared output schema (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`) at write time: rows that do not conform to the declared columns and types **MUST** be rejected and surfaced as a connector failure. The runtime **SHOULD** support this via a schema-validating proxy or a ClickHouse table schema that mirrors the manifest exactly.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The silver-side `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates` defends the consumer; this FR defends the producer. A connector that emits malformed rows by accident or malice should fail loudly at write rather than corrupt bronze and require the silver tests to catch the symptom downstream.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 NFR Inclusions\n+\n+#### Install-to-Functional Time\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-install-latency`\n+\n+From the moment a tenant admin approves an install through the admin UI to the moment the plugin is functional (first successful bronze write for connector; first successful transform for silver; widget available on the dashboard surface), the plugin system **MUST** complete the flow in under 10 minutes p95 for plugins up to 200 MB in image size on a standard instance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 ≤ 10 minutes, measured from install request to `status = ready`.\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Plugin Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-nfr-tenant-capacity`\n+\n+An instance **MUST** support at least 50 plugin installs per tenant and at least 200 plugin installs total across all tenants without degradation of catalog or resolver performance beyond documented thresholds.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 50 installs per tenant; 200 per instance; resolver completes in < 2 seconds p95 at this scale.\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+- **Multi-region distribution** (OPS): Plugin system is co-located with the Insight instance it extends; multi-region replication is an instance-level concern, not a plugin-level one.\n+- **Offline support** (UX): The admin UI that drives plugin install is an online tool. Air-gapped installation uses a mirrored OCI registry; offline authoring is out of scope.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Plugin Catalog API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-catalog-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (read-only for tenant admins; read/write for instance admins)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: Lists plugins available for install (merged view of central catalog + instance-local overrides + URL-installable check). Exposes plugin identity, available versions, description, declared capabilities, and compatibility range. The exact endpoint shape, pagination, and response payload are specified in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Additive fields non-breaking. Removing or renaming fields is a major version bump with a two-minor-release deprecation window.\n+\n+#### Plugin Install API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-install-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (tenant admin + instance admin)\n+\n+**Stability**: stable\n+\n+**Description**: CRUD on installed plugins: request install, approve a resolved plan, upgrade, downgrade, enable, disable, uninstall. Every mutation produces an auditable event; every install change is presented as a resolved plan first and only applied on explicit approval.\n+\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Same as catalog API.\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime Interface\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-interface-runtime`\n+\n+**Type**: Internal interface between the admin API and the plugin runtime\n+\n+**Stability**: unstable (v1); target stable by v2\n+\n+**Description**: Describes how a requested install/upgrade/uninstall is reconciled into runtime state — fetching the image, running the capability entrypoints, registering widgets with the frontend, surfacing status. Shape is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### OCI Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-oci`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external registry\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: OCI distribution spec v1.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Any registry that speaks OCI v1 works. Private registries require a pull credential configured at the instance level.\n+\n+#### Plugin Capability Contracts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-contract-capabilities`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by platform, required of plugins\n+\n+**Protocol/Format**: For each of `connector`, `silver`, `widget`, the set of declared inputs/outputs in the manifest is the contract. A plugin that does not honor its declared contract (emits wrong tables, demands unmet config, etc.) is considered broken and **MUST** be surfaced as such by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Compatibility**: Additive capability-contract evolution is allowed within a major version. Breaking changes require a major bump and explicit admin-approved migration.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-install-from-catalog`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant has at least one admin; the instance has the central catalog configured; the target plugin is in the catalog and not blocked at the instance level.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the plugin marketplace in the product UI and selects a plugin\n+2. UI displays the plugin's description, declared capabilities, compatibility range, and required config\n+3. Admin clicks Install; the admin API invokes the resolver\n+4. Resolver returns a resolved plan (this plugin + any dependencies with versions)\n+5. UI shows the plan and asks the admin to approve\n+6. Admin approves; plugin runtime pulls the image(s), runs any bronze migrations, configures the install, and activates it\n+7. Admin is presented with a configuration form generated from the plugin's config schema\n+8. Admin supplies configuration and secrets; the plugin begins operating within its declared capability\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The plugin is installed, enabled, configured, and reporting status to the inventory. No prior plugins were modified.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Resolver finds a conflict**: The resolver surfaces the specific conflict (plugin X requires version Y of plugin Z, but plugin Z is pinned at another version); admin rejects the plan or asks the owner of the conflicting plugin to upgrade. No state changes.\n+- **Image pull fails**: The install is marked failed; no state is partially applied. Admin retries or escalates to instance admin.\n+\n+### UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-publish-new-version`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The author has write access to an OCI registry and an existing plugin at version N.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Author bumps the version in the plugin manifest per SemVer rules\n+2. Author builds the image, runs local validation against the documented contract, and pushes it to the registry\n+3. If the plugin is in the central catalog, the author (or their team) updates the catalog entry with the new version\n+4. Existing tenant admins see the new version appear in the UI as \"upgrade available\"\n+\n+**Postconditions**: The new version is discoverable. Existing installs at the prior version continue to operate unchanged until their admins explicitly upgrade.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Breaking change**: Author bumps the major version. Tenant admins see the upgrade as \"breaking — major bump\" in the UI and must explicitly opt in; the resolver flags dependent plugins that would also need upgrading.\n+\n+### UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A live snapshot exists for the tenant. The tenant admin has authored a new snapshot (either by editing the live one and saving as a new snapshot, by cloning a vendor or instance reference, or by composing from scratch).\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin selects \"deploy as shadow\" on the new snapshot\n+2. Plugin runtime instantiates the new snapshot as a *shadow*: provisions per-install ClickHouse users with scoped GRANTs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`), grants each shadow install `SELECT` on the corresponding live install's bronze (per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`), creates the new bronze / silver scopes, schedules the shadow plugins' work\n+3. For each plugin in the shadow whose `plugin_id` was also present in the live snapshot, the runtime executes the plugin-author-shipped migration (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`) to populate or expose historical bronze — view, materialized view, full copy, or no migration at the author's choice\n+4. Both snapshots run concurrently. The live snapshot continues to feed widgets and analytics-api; the shadow snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own scopes\n+5. The admin UI exposes a side-by-side comparison view: row counts per silver table, schema diffs, per-column distributional statistics where shapes match (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+6. Adaptation is open-ended. The trial reminder timer (default 7 days, tenant-configurable) surfaces a non-blocking reminder if the shadow has not been promoted or rejected. Reminders escalate by frequency, never by action (`cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`)\n+7. The admin PROMOTES — the runtime atomically switches the *live* pointer to the new snapshot, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT, and the previous (now non-live) snapshot is retained until explicitly retired\n+8. Inventory is updated; the graph view reports green on the new live snapshot\n+\n+**Postconditions**: On promote — the new snapshot is live; the previous snapshot remains running but no longer feeds users; bronze of the previous snapshot is retained until explicit retirement (`cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`). On reject — the shadow snapshot is uninstalled; its bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it; the tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Admin rejects at step 1**: No state change. The new snapshot is saved but not deployed; admin can deploy or discard later.\n+- **A plugin in the shadow fails to install or migrate**: The runtime surfaces the failure; **the live snapshot is untouched**. Admin can retry, edit the snapshot, or discard the shadow.\n+- **Admin REJECTS during adaptation (step 6)**: Runtime uninstalls the shadow snapshot, drops its DB scopes, revokes the cross-snapshot GRANT. Tenant remains on the unchanged live snapshot.\n+- **Trial reminder expires without action**: UI raises a reminder; **no automatic promote, no automatic revert**. The shadow remains running until the admin acts. If the tenant prefers fewer notifications, the reminder cadence is configurable.\n+- **Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot**: This is not a distinct \"rollback\" operation. The admin starts a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history), and follows the same flow. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+### UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-silver-consumes-bronze`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: A connector plugin and a silver plugin are both installed in the same tenant; the connector's output bronze schema matches the silver plugin's declared input contract (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`). There is **no** plugin-level dependency declared between them — the match is schema-based, so the same silver plugin accepts any connector whose output shape satisfies its input contract.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Connector plugin completes a sync and writes updated bronze\n+2. Plugin runtime triggers the silver plugin, passing the declared bronze inputs\n+3. Silver plugin runs its data-quality tests against the bronze inputs (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`)\n+4. Tests pass; silver plugin runs its transform and writes silver outputs\n+5. Runtime records success; downstream consumers (widgets, analytics-api) observe refreshed silver data\n+\n+**Postconditions**: Silver data is current; bronze is unchanged; status is green.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Data-quality test fails**: Transform is not run; the failure is surfaced to the admin (instance and tenant) with a pointer to the failing test and the offending input. No silver state is overwritten with bad data.\n+- **Bronze schema changed unexpectedly**: Silver plugin's declared input contract no longer matches; runtime refuses the run and surfaces the contract mismatch to the admin, who must either upgrade the silver plugin or downgrade the connector.\n+- **A new connector with matching `output.tag` is added to the same snapshot**: Silver discovery picks it up automatically on the next run — silver is not aware of which specific connector plugin produced the bronze, only of the schema and tag. No silver-plugin change is required when a new compatible connector is added.\n+\n+### UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-usecase-tenant-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Preconditions**: The tenant admin wants a plugin combination not available as a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Admin opens the snapshot editor in the admin UI\n+2. Admin starts from a fresh canvas, from a clone of the live snapshot, or from a clone of a vendor / instance reference snapshot\n+3. Admin adds, removes, or version-pins individual plugins (the catalog UI surfaces available versions and trust tiers per plugin)\n+4. Admin saves the composition as a new snapshot (immutable; subsequent edits produce a new snapshot, not a mutation)\n+5. Admin deploys the new snapshot as a shadow per UC-003\n+\n+**Postconditions**: A new tenant-owned snapshot is recorded in inventory. The tenant can deploy it now or later. Other snapshots in the tenant's history remain unchanged.\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Composition contains a plugin combination flagged by static checks (e.g., two plugins sharing the same `output.tag` in incompatible ways)**: The editor surfaces the issue at save time; the admin can either resolve and resave, or save with a warning. There is no system veto — warnings are non-blocking, but visible.\n+- **Composition includes an unsigned plugin while instance policy disallows unsigned plugins (per `cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`)**: Save succeeds, deploy is blocked at the instance level until policy is changed or the plugin is replaced.\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] A plugin with `connector` capability can be published to an OCI registry and added to a tenant snapshot through the admin UI, end to end, with no code changes to the Insight product\n+- [ ] A silver plugin whose input discovery yields zero matching bronze tables in the snapshot is installable but surfaces a \"no sources discovered\" warning to the admin; adding a second connector that produces the expected shape to the same snapshot makes the silver plugin's transform run without modifying the silver plugin\n+- [ ] An instance admin cannot install or upgrade a plugin on a tenant's behalf, nor mutate a tenant's snapshot, through the admin API; force-retire of a known-bad plugin is allowed and is audit-logged with the reason and tenant notification\n+- [ ] A plugin that combines `connector` + `silver` + `widget` in a single manifest installs as one artifact and each capability becomes operational independently\n+- [ ] Two snapshots can coexist in one tenant indefinitely as fully independent data planes (independent bronze / silver scopes, independent per-install ClickHouse users, independent pods); promoting one does not delete the other\n+- [ ] Promoting a new snapshot retains the previous snapshot's bronze until the admin explicitly retires it\n+- [ ] A new snapshot installed as a shadow alongside a live snapshot processes the same connector inputs, exposes a side-by-side comparison view, and never feeds user-facing surfaces until promoted\n+- [ ] A trial reminder timer expires without admin action surfaces a reminder in the UI; no automatic promote, no automatic revert\n+- [ ] Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot is performed by initiating a new shadow-deploy targeting the prior snapshot — there is no distinct \"rollback\" API\n+- [ ] Each plugin's transform code (dbt project or equivalent) is self-contained in the plugin directory; attempting to use dbt `ref()` to reach across plugins fails at compile time; a downstream plugin discovers upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract with runtime-injected `sources.yml`\n+- [ ] A plugin install runs under a per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and to discovered upstream bronze for silver); attempting to read or write outside the scope is rejected at the database layer\n+- [ ] A shadow snapshot's installs receive `SELECT` GRANT on the live snapshot's matching bronze for the duration of the shadow only; the GRANT is revoked atomically on promote, reject, or retire\n+- [ ] A plugin install attempted with an image referenced by tag (no digest) is rejected at install time\n+- [ ] A plugin install whose image signature does not chain to a configured trust root is allowed in the unsigned tier with reduced capabilities (iframe widget render, dbt-only silver, gVisor / Kata runtime, curated-registry-only)\n+- [ ] A revoked image digest cannot start new pods even if the install was created before revocation\n+- [ ] A connector plugin using the Airbyte source protocol can be installed without the plugin author reimplementing destination logic\n+- [ ] A widget plugin loads in the host frontend at runtime without a product rebuild\n+- [ ] When a silver plugin's data-quality tests fail, the transform does not run and an admin-visible failure is recorded\n+- [ ] An instance admin can inspect the inventory of all installed plugins across all tenants with per-install health state and per-snapshot grouping (a flat list view satisfies this; a graph visualization is a `SHOULD` per `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` and is not release-gating)\n+- [ ] Attempting to install a plugin whose manifest is invalid (missing required fields, malformed schema) fails at the admin-API layer with a clear error, before any runtime action\n+- [ ] All plugin-produced tables are reachable only through the install's per-install user with scoped GRANTs; cross-tenant and cross-install reads are rejected at the database layer, not merely filtered by application code\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| OCI-compatible registry | Hosts plugin images. Must be reachable from the instance or mirrored for air-gapped installs. Mutable tags are not used — images are pinned by digest | p1 |\n+| Sigstore / cosign infrastructure | Image signing and verification, transparency log, revocation list distribution | p1 |\n+| Instance database (MariaDB) | Stores the per-tenant snapshot inventory, snapshot composition, install config, audit log | p1 |\n+| ClickHouse | Hosts per-snapshot bronze / silver scopes; per-install user RBAC (GRANT/REVOKE), quotas | p1 |\n+| Plugin runtime subsystem | Reconciles snapshot inventory into runtime state (Kubernetes workloads, K8s `NetworkPolicy`, ServiceAccount, ResourceQuota), frontend widget registration | p1 |\n+| Kubernetes `NetworkPolicy` controller | Enforces egress allowlist for plugin pods (baseline isolation) | p1 |\n+| Container runtime classes | Standard `runc` plus `gVisor` or `Kata` available for unsigned-tier plugins; required for kernel isolation | p2 (only needed once unsigned-tier ships) |\n+| Ingestion orchestration (Argo Workflows today) | Schedules connector syncs and silver transforms; called by the plugin runtime | p1 |\n+| Host frontend microfrontend loader | Required for native widget render in vendor- / instance-signed tiers; iframe render in unsigned tier works without it | p2 (widget capability ships when this is available) |\n+| External secret manager | Provides plugin credentials in production; supported integrations include Vault, External Secrets Operator (bridging to AWS / GCP / Azure secret managers), SealedSecrets. Manual-entry fallback for evaluation deployments | p1 (manual-entry suffices for v1, external store is required for prod-grade installs) |\n+| Central catalog service | Hosts plugin and (optionally) reference-snapshot metadata; URL install works without it | p2 |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- Insight instances run on Kubernetes; plugin containers are scheduled as Kubernetes workloads by the plugin runtime.\n+- Tenants are the atomic isolation boundary; a plugin install never spans tenants in v1.\n+- The product's existing microfrontend technology becomes available on the timeline expected for widget capability to ship at `p1`.\n+- The platform's existing Argo Workflows deployment is the orchestrator used to execute connector syncs and silver transforms; plugins do not bring their own scheduler.\n+- SemVer discipline is realistic to enforce on first-party and enterprise plugins; third-party plugins in the catalog may be checked by Insight before acceptance.\n+- Bronze data volumes remain within single-cluster capacity for v1; cross-cluster plugin coordination is not required.\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Plugin authors misuse SemVer, shipping breaking changes as minor versions | Silent downstream breakage across tenants | Snapshot-as-state model: tenant validates the new snapshot in shadow before promoting, on real data, against the old snapshot side-by-side. SemVer hygiene is helpful but no longer load-bearing; consumer plugins ship data-quality tests as the last line of defense |\n+| Connector plugins write garbage bronze — typo, API change, malicious plugin — and silver consumers can't tell | Silver / gold layers quietly corrupt | Connector schema conformance enforced at write (`cpt-plugin-fr-connector-schema-conformance`); silver consumers additionally ship data-quality tests against declared input contracts (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`); plugin system runs them pre-transform and halts on failure |\n+| OCI registry is the single point of failure for install and upgrade | Plugin system unavailable when registry is down | Air-gapped path supports mirroring; instance admins can cache plugin images locally; install is idempotent so retry is safe |\n+| Plugin image is swapped under the same tag (supply-chain attack) | Malicious code lands on next pull | Images are pinned by digest, never by tag (`cpt-plugin-fr-image-signing-required`); signature verification on every pod admission |\n+| Plugin signing key (vendor or instance) is compromised | All plugins signed by that key become suspect | Sigstore transparency log + revocation list checked on pod admission; per-version signatures so revoking one version does not revoke all; key rotation procedure documented and exercised quarterly |\n+| Plugin reverse-DNS naming collides across registries (`com.cyberfabric.gitea` on dockerhub vs ghcr.io) | Tenant could install the wrong artifact | Trust tier requires signature; instance admin's curated registry allowlist prevents arbitrary registries for vendor-signed and instance-signed tiers; for unsigned, Insight-curated allowlist only |\n+| Plugin runtime crash leaves an install in a half-applied state | Manual recovery required | Every install/upgrade plan is persisted before application; runtime is reconciliation-based, so restart resumes from persisted state |\n+| Plugin attempts cross-tenant data access through DB | Tenant data leakage | Per-install ClickHouse user with GRANTs scoped to install's own bronze / silver and (for silver) discovered upstream bronze; cross-snapshot / cross-tenant reads are physically impossible at the DB layer, not just filtered by application code (`cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`) |\n+| Widget plugin escapes its sandbox and accesses user JWT or tenant data | Authentication / data leakage | Widget never queries the data store directly; for unsigned plugins the widget renders in a cross-origin iframe with `sandbox` and the user JWT is never exposed across the iframe boundary (`cpt-plugin-fr-trust-tiers`); for signed plugins, host SDK proxy is the only data path |\n+| Plugin pod runs scanners, miners, reverse VPN, or other unintended workloads inside the cluster | Compute theft / lateral movement | NetworkPolicy limits egress to manifest-declared targets; ResourceQuota caps CPU/memory; default ServiceAccount has no K8s API access; for unsigned plugins, gVisor/Kata runtime adds kernel isolation (`cpt-plugin-fr-baseline-isolation`) |\n+| Third-party plugin becomes unmaintained and no one upgrades it past a breaking platform version | Tenants stuck, cannot upgrade platform | Tenant snapshots pin specific digest+signature, so an abandoned plugin keeps working at its pinned version; new platform versions document compatibility windows; abandoned plugins flagged in catalog, not auto-removed |\n+| Storage cost balloons if many shadow snapshots accumulate over months/years | Disk exhaustion, query slowdown | View-based migrations keep storage at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` (`cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`); retired-snapshot reporting (`cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`) surfaces stale snapshots to admin for cleanup; bronze retention is admin-controlled, not auto |\n+| Tenant admin loses track of which old snapshots are still alive | Unbounded resource consumption, audit confusion | Snapshot inventory exposes `live` / `shadow` / `retired` status per snapshot; retired-snapshot reporting nudges cleanup; dependency graph view groups installs by snapshot |\n+| Plugin runtime egress through manifest allowlist is bypassed by DNS exfiltration or covert channels | Data leakage | Egress proxy enforces allowlist at L7 where possible; baseline only — does not claim defense against sophisticated covert channels. Documented limitation: instance admins should use cluster-level egress monitoring for residual coverage |\n+| GDPR / compliance erasure conflicts with bronze-is-sacred | Regulatory exposure | Bronze-is-sacred is a plugin-system guarantee, not a compliance guarantee; GDPR erasure is handled by a platform-level data-lifecycle tool that can drop a tenant's snapshot scopes on lawful request — not part of this PRD |\n+\n+## 13. Open Questions\n+\n+| Question | Owner | Target Resolution |\n+|----------|-------|-------------------|\n+| Plugin manifest schema — exact YAML shape, required vs optional fields, how capabilities are expressed, extension points for future capability kinds | Backend tech lead + Plugin Author lead | DESIGN / first ADR |\n+| Snapshot manifest format — what does a snapshot literally look like on disk (`{plugin_id: digest, version}` list? full plugin manifest copies? something dbt-package-like?) and how is it stored / diffed in the instance database | Backend tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Snapshot inheritance and overlays — does a tenant snapshot reference a parent snapshot with overrides (\"vendor reference + 3 plugin overrides\"), or is every snapshot a flat list? Influences storage and UI complexity | Backend tech lead + Product design | DESIGN, before tenant-snapshot-editor UI ships |\n+| Vendor reference snapshots — do we ship them at all? If yes, what is the publishing process, the testing matrix, and the maintenance commitment? If no, document explicitly and rely on community / instance-admin reference snapshots only | Insight Product Team | Before v1 ship |\n+| Snapshot retirement policy — when does the system stop offering to keep an old snapshot alive (storage cap? per-tenant cap on number of concurrent snapshots? hard expiry?); the goal is to avoid unbounded accumulation while keeping tenant control | Insight Product Team + Backend tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Native Insight Connector Protocol — if and when we define a protocol beyond Airbyte wrapping; stdin/stdout JSONL vs gRPC vs OpenAPI endpoints; is the protocol necessary in v1 or is \"container writes to its declared scope\" enough | Ingestion tech lead | DESIGN / ADR, post-v1 if possible |\n+| Per-tenant isolation mechanism within a single snapshot — schema-per-install (snapshot+install in the schema name) appears to be the right model given the snapshot architecture, but the choice between `tenant_id` column + RLS vs schema-per-install for any tables that span installs (e.g., system audit) needs DESIGN | Data tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Installed-inventory data model — exact tables for `tenant_snapshot`, `snapshot_plugin_install`, `snapshot_status`, audit log shape, the live-pointer transactional semantics during promote | Backend tech lead | DESIGN |\n+| Integration testing strategy — how does a plugin author test their plugin against a real customer data shape when the customer's source system is behind a firewall Insight cannot reach; options include recorded/anonymized request traces shipped to the author, an opt-in customer-side trace-collection service operated by the Insight team, mock servers per connector type, or a combination | Plugin Author lead + Customer Success | DESIGN + customer-engagement policy; track separately from the data-model DESIGN |\n+| Plugin-authoring SDK / CLI — `insight-plugin init` / `validate` / `test` / `package` / `publish` (pipe-escaped to avoid table-render issues) — is it v1 (\"can't ship without this\") or v2 (\"docs + reference plugins first, CLI when patterns stabilize\"); the intent is the latter, but the threshold at which the CLI becomes a necessity should be articulated | Plugin Author lead | v2 planning; revisit after first three external plugins |","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-23] Plugin dev / CI is a black box without resolver infrastructure**\n\nThis Open Question is about the SDK / CLI but understates the problem: plugin authors ship a dbt project with `source('''git''', '''merge_requests''')`. The `sources.yml` resolving these is generated only at runtime by the **production** resolver. To unit-test or CI-test the plugin'''s SQL against representative data, the author needs:\n\n- Either a local copy of the resolver infrastructure (heavy)\n- Or a hand-written `sources.yml` mock — meaning local tests don'''t validate the integration with the resolver'''s actual behavior\n\nThe PRD doesn'''t address this in any FR. The \"publish to registry → tenant installs\" flow assumes the plugin works; first encounter with the actual resolver is in a customer'''s tenant. Effectively: CI/CD validates the dbt syntax; production validates everything else.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Ship a local `insight-plugin-resolver` CLI as part of the SDK (even at v1) that mirrors prod resolver behavior; document the resolver'''s contract precisely so authors can write golden-file tests; provide reference fixtures for common bronze shapes. Addresses the \"v2 SDK, v1 docs\" tension by acknowledging that the resolver'''s contract is the SDK'''s most important artifact.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:00Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:00Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810918","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810918"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146810918"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146810918/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":1005,"original_line":1005,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1005,"position":1005,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811027","pull_request_review_id":4180365741,"id":3146811027,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIaT","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.\n+\n+Secrets **MUST NOT** be logged, leaked into manifests, included in audit-log payloads beyond a redacted reference, or stored in the catalog. Secrets **MUST** be rotated atomically — a rotation produces a new version of the secret which the runtime injects into newly-spawned plugin pods on next sync.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise customers expect Vault / cloud-vendor managers; small deployments need a fallback that does not require that infrastructure. Both paths are common, and the split keeps each simple. Naming concrete integration points (rather than the vague \"external secret manager\") closes a clarification raised in review.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Per-Install ClickHouse User with Scoped GRANTs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be assigned a dedicated ClickHouse user, generated by the plugin runtime at install time with a cryptographically random password stored as a K8s `Secret` (or equivalent) and injected into the plugin pod via env. The plugin **MUST NOT** use the ClickHouse `default` user, an admin user, or the credentials of any other install. The plugin runtime **MUST** provision GRANTs scoped to the install's capability:\n+\n+- **Connector install**: `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON bronze_<snapshot>_<install>.*` (its own bronze scope only).\n+- **Silver install**: `SELECT ON bronze_<snapshot>_<discovered_input>.*` for each upstream bronze scope resolved through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`); plus `INSERT, SELECT, ALTER ON silver_<snapshot>_<install>.*` for its own silver scope.\n+- **Widget install**: no direct ClickHouse access. Widgets read data through the host application (`cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`).\n+- **Cross-snapshot SELECT** for the duration of a shadow snapshot's life, per `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`.\n+\n+The runtime **MUST** revoke all GRANTs and drop the user when the install is uninstalled (snapshot retired). The runtime **MUST** support per-user query and resource quotas (max queries per minute, max memory per query) configured at install time and capped by instance-admin-level defaults.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-24] ClickHouse DCL operations under per-install user model don'''t scale**\n\nThe per-install user model creates and tears down ClickHouse users + GRANTs frequently: at every install, every shadow deploy, every promote, every retire, plus cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT issuance and revocation per shadow.\n\nClickHouse is **not** optimized for high-frequency DCL (Data Control Language) operations:\n\n- `CREATE USER` / `GRANT` / `REVOKE` propagate through the cluster'''s RBAC backend (usually Keeper/ZooKeeper for `replicated` storage). Every change is a cluster-wide write.\n- `system.grants` and `system.users` are queried on every authorization check; row counts grow with the install graph.\n- Pod warm-up (start → ready-to-issue-SQL) includes a CH login round-trip; under DCL contention this grows non-linearly.\n\nAt 200 installs the load is modest. At thousands of tenants × dozens of installs × multiple shadows, the GRANT graph reaches tens of thousands of users and hundreds of thousands of grants. Operational lifecycle (promote/retire) starts to bottleneck on Keeper writes.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Add NFR on RBAC propagation latency under load. Pick the RBAC backend in DESIGN, sized for 10× projected install count. Explicit GC invariant: when a snapshot is retired, all its users and GRANTs MUST be dropped (no orphans). Consider role-based GRANTs (one role per capability shape, users assigned to roles) to flatten the GRANT count.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:01Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:01Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146811027","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811027"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146811027"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811027/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":606,"original_line":606,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":606,"position":606,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811159","pull_request_review_id":4180365872,"id":3146811159,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIcX","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: dbt's model is great for declarative SQL transforms but breaks down for algorithmic enrichment (identity resolution, ML scoring, custom aggregations). Forcing every silver plugin into dbt would either lock those cases out or contort them. Letting authors pick the engine keeps the capability broad.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+#### Silver Consumer Validates Its Inputs\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-consumer-validates`\n+\n+A silver plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests against its declared bronze inputs (dbt tests for dbt-based plugins; equivalent mechanism in the manifest for other engines). When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before running the transform and **MUST** report failures to the admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Connectors evolve independently of silver plugins. The only robust defense against \"connector sent us garbage today\" is for the silver consumer to declare its expectations and check them before transforming. Shifts responsibility for compatibility to the party best positioned to enforce it.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Ships Its Own Migrations (Optionally as Cross-Snapshot SQL Views)\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`\n+\n+A plugin **MAY** ship migration scripts that the runtime runs when a new snapshot containing this plugin is created and a previous snapshot containing the same plugin (under the same `plugin_id`, possibly at a different version) exists. Migrations **MAY** be expressed in any of the following forms; the choice is the plugin author's:\n+\n+- **`CREATE VIEW`** in the new install's scope, reading from the previous install's bronze through the cross-snapshot SELECT grant (`cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`). Zero storage cost; reads pay view-evaluation overhead. Suitable when historical data shape changes are minor renames / casts / computed columns.\n+- **`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW`**, populated as a background job. Storage proportional to historical data; reads cheap after materialization. Suitable when downstream silver / widgets need indexed or projection-optimized access.\n+- **`INSERT FROM SELECT`** (full physical migration). Run once during shadow installation; the cross-snapshot grant is no longer needed after completion. Storage proportional to historical data; full control of target shape.\n+- **No migration**: the plugin starts with an empty bronze scope; historical data does not carry over. Acceptable for connectors that re-fetch from the upstream source on schedule.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** make the prior snapshot's bronze scope name available to the migration script as a parameter and **MUST** revoke the cross-snapshot grant when the shadow snapshot is promoted, rejected, or retired.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin authors are the only party who knows whether a schema change is a rename, a derivation, a destructive transformation, or a from-scratch reconnect. Letting the author pick the migration form (and pay the storage cost they choose) keeps the system honest about the cost-vs-richness tradeoff. View-based migrations in particular keep the storage cost of a long-running shadow snapshot at `~1× + delta` instead of `2×` — the concern raised in PR review (storage explosion with multi-snapshot coexistence) becomes a function of plugin-author choice, not a system-imposed worst case.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Each Plugin Ships Its Own Isolated Transform Project\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`\n+\n+A plugin that performs transformations (connector normalization, silver transform, or any future transform capability) **MUST** ship its transformation code as a SELF-CONTAINED project scoped to its plugin directory — its own `dbt_project.yml`, its own `packages.yml`, its own macros and models, or the equivalents of another engine. Plugins **MUST NOT** use dbt `ref()` (or the equivalent cross-project reference mechanism of another engine) to reach into another plugin's models. The only supported cross-plugin data flow is via ClickHouse tables materialized by an upstream plugin and referenced by a downstream plugin as dbt `source()` (or equivalent) through the schema-based input contract (`cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`).\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** render source definitions (e.g., `sources.yml` for dbt plugins) with the concrete schema names resolved from the discovery rules at run time, injecting them into the plugin's transform project before each run. A plugin **MUST NOT** hardcode schema names that assume a specific upstream plugin install, and the plugin system **MUST NOT** construct a global transform DAG spanning plugins; each install runs its own transform end-to-end, on its own schedule, in isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A shared transform DAG (such as a single repo-wide dbt project) couples every plugin to every other plugin at compile time — one plugin's rename or type change compiles-fails a completely different plugin owned by a different author. Project-per-plugin isolation is what enables independent authorship, independent versioning, independent release cadence, independent runtime upgrade (including shadow-deploy), and the eventual path to third-party plugins. The cost is losing dbt's ability to order transforms across plugins — but cross-plugin ordering belongs at the system scheduling layer (cron + discovery-based source resolution), not inside the transform engine.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.5 Widget Capability\n+\n+#### Widget Ships as a Microfrontend Module\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-microfrontend`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `widget` capability **MUST** ship a microfrontend module that the Insight host frontend loads at runtime. The host frontend **MUST** fetch the module on demand — widgets are not bundled into the product build — and **MUST** render the module within the container-provided frame.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Runtime-loaded widgets are the only way to extend the dashboard surface without a frontend rebuild. Microfrontend is the contract the rest of the frontend is already moving toward; reusing it here means widget-capability plugins do not need a separate runtime.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Receives Data from the Host App\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-data-contract`\n+\n+A widget capability **MUST** declare its expected input data as a table schema (column names, types, nullability) and optional config (JSON schema). The host application **MUST** resolve the data based on the dashboard's widget-instance configuration, fetch it, and pass it to the widget through a stable interface. The widget **MUST NOT** query the data store directly. The host **MUST** act as a pass-through — it **MUST NOT** validate that the delivered data matches the widget's declared schema.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralizing data fetching in the host prevents widget plugins from leaking data between tenants, bypassing isolation, or holding long-lived DB credentials. Keeping the host a pure proxy (no semantic validation) avoids accidentally coupling the host to every widget's evolving schema; validation, when needed, belongs to the party that cares — the widget plugin itself (see `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`).\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Input Validation Is Opt-In via Plugin-Shipped Tests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-input-tests`\n+\n+A widget-capability plugin **MAY** ship data-quality tests (dbt or an equivalent mechanism declared in the manifest) against its declared input. When such tests are present, the plugin runtime **MUST** execute them before rendering the widget and **MUST** surface failures to the admin. When such tests are not present, the host renders whatever it fetched without checking it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Data-shape mismatches break widgets silently today. Letting widget authors declare and enforce their own expectations shifts responsibility to the party who feels the pain. Keeping tests opt-in avoids forcing every trivial widget to invent a test suite.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Widget Config Is Merged Across Scopes\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-widget-config-merge`\n+\n+Widget configuration **MUST** be layered across scopes with narrower scopes overriding broader ones through deep merge: instance default (from the silver plugin that ships the widget wiring) → tenant → dashboard → widget-instance. The layering **MUST** be deterministic and **MUST** surface the effective merged config to admins for diagnostics.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Real customers need per-tenant customization (branding, formatting) while keeping a sensible default. Deep merge lets admins change narrow slices of widget config without restating the whole thing at every layer.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-data-isolation`\n+\n+All plugin-produced data **MUST** carry the `tenant_id` of the tenant it was produced for and **MUST** be queryable only within that tenant's scope. Cross-tenant reads from any plugin-produced table **MUST** be rejected by the data store's enforcement layer, not merely filtered by application code.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Application-level filtering alone fails under bugs or malicious plugins. Enforcement at the data-store layer (row-level security policies or schema-per-tenant, as decided in DESIGN) is the only robust guarantee.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Plugin Versions Follow SemVer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-semver-contracts`\n+\n+Plugin authors **MUST** follow SemVer 2.0 — breaking changes to any declared capability contract (config schema, output tables, widget data contract) require a major-version bump. Additive changes **MAY** be minor versions; bug fixes **MAY** be patch versions. The plugin system's dependency resolver **MAY** use SemVer ranges in declared dependencies.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Predictable version semantics are the foundation on which dependency resolution, upgrade approval, and author-to-consumer trust all sit. Without SemVer discipline, the graph is noise.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+### 5.7 Configuration and Secrets\n+\n+#### Config Schema Drives the Install UI\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-config-schema-ui`\n+\n+Every plugin capability **MUST** declare its configuration requirements as a schema in its manifest. The admin UI **MUST** render a configuration form from that schema during install and configuration. The plugin system **MUST** validate submitted config against the schema before persisting it.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Matches the tenant-UI install flow (Airbyte-like) without requiring custom UI per plugin. Validation at admin-API time catches misconfiguration early.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Secrets Supplied Through External Store or Manual Input\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-secret-provisioning`\n+\n+Plugin capabilities that require secrets (API tokens, credentials) **MUST** declare them in the manifest. The plugin system **MUST** support providing them through:\n+\n+- **(a) An external secret manager**, configured at the instance level. Supported integrations include — at minimum — HashiCorp Vault, External Secrets Operator (which itself bridges to AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.), and SealedSecrets for GitOps-managed deployments. The exact set of supported integrations is a DESIGN decision; the contract is that the plugin manifest names a secret by reference, not by value.\n+- **(b) A manual entry path in the admin UI** for instances without an external store wired up. The submitted value is encrypted at rest in the instance's K8s `Secret` (or equivalent), scoped to the install.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-25] K8s Secret count grows with install count — etcd, API server, rotation scaling unaddressed**\n\nEvery plugin install needs at minimum one K8s Secret (per-install ClickHouse credentials) plus zero or more for plugin-declared secrets (API tokens, OAuth credentials). At PRD'''s NFR (200 installs) this is fine. At realistic growth (thousands of tenants × dozens of installs × multiple shadow snapshots) the count reaches 10K–100K+ secrets per cluster.\n\nK8s Secrets at this scale hit several cliffs:\n\n- **etcd**: default 8 GiB limit; long before that, watch/list operations on large Secret sets degrade API server latency\n- **Encryption at rest opt-in**: K8s stores Secrets base64-encoded plaintext in etcd unless EncryptionConfiguration with KMS is enabled; the manual-entry path here doesn'''t mention etcd encryption\n- **KMS rate limits**: AWS/GCP/Azure KMS providers throttle at high QPS; rotation across thousands of secrets simultaneously hits the limit\n- **RBAC graph multiplication**: per-install ServiceAccount + Role + RoleBinding for least-privilege Secret access ≈ 4× the cluster object count\n- **Backup/restore time**: etcd backup grows with Secret count; restore time grows linearly\n- **\"Atomic rotation\" claim**: at 10K secrets, atomicity is operationally fictional\n\nThe PRD says \"K8s Secret (or equivalent)\" — the \"or equivalent\" hides an architectural choice (native K8s vs External Secrets Operator vs CSI Secret Store driver) that materially changes scaling. PRD lists three options as supported but picks none.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Pick one production-grade backend (External Secrets Operator with Vault, or CSI driver) as the default for production deployments; manual K8s Secret path remains for evaluation only. Add NFRs: time-to-rotate at scale, max secret count before degradation, per-pod startup-latency contribution. Require etcd encryption-at-rest with KMS as a P1 dependency.","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:03Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:03Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146811159","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811159"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146811159"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811159/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":587,"original_line":587,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":587,"position":587,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811327","pull_request_review_id":4180365997,"id":3146811327,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s67kIe_","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1013 @@\n+# PRD — Plugin System\n+\n+<!-- toc -->\n+\n+- [Changelog](#changelog)\n+- [1. Overview](#1-overview)\n+  - [1.1 Purpose](#11-purpose)\n+  - [1.2 Background / Problem Statement](#12-background--problem-statement)\n+  - [1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)](#13-goals-business-outcomes)\n+  - [1.4 Glossary](#14-glossary)\n+- [2. Actors](#2-actors)\n+  - [2.1 Human Actors](#21-human-actors)\n+  - [2.2 System Actors](#22-system-actors)\n+- [3. Operational Concept & Environment](#3-operational-concept--environment)\n+  - [3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints](#31-module-specific-environment-constraints)\n+- [4. Scope](#4-scope)\n+  - [4.1 In Scope](#41-in-scope)\n+  - [4.2 Out of Scope](#42-out-of-scope)\n+- [5. Functional Requirements](#5-functional-requirements)\n+  - [5.1 Packaging and Distribution](#51-packaging-and-distribution)\n+  - [5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation](#52-snapshot-based-versioning-and-installation)\n+  - [5.3 Connector Capability](#53-connector-capability)\n+  - [5.4 Silver Capability](#54-silver-capability)\n+  - [5.5 Widget Capability](#55-widget-capability)\n+  - [5.6 Tenant Isolation and Versioning](#56-tenant-isolation-and-versioning)\n+  - [5.7 Configuration and Secrets](#57-configuration-and-secrets)\n+  - [5.8 Observability and Lifecycle](#58-observability-and-lifecycle)\n+  - [5.9 Security and Trust Model](#59-security-and-trust-model)\n+- [6. Non-Functional Requirements](#6-non-functional-requirements)\n+  - [6.1 NFR Inclusions](#61-nfr-inclusions)\n+  - [6.2 NFR Exclusions](#62-nfr-exclusions)\n+- [7. Public Library Interfaces](#7-public-library-interfaces)\n+  - [7.1 Public API Surface](#71-public-api-surface)\n+  - [7.2 External Integration Contracts](#72-external-integration-contracts)\n+- [8. Use Cases](#8-use-cases)\n+  - [UC-001 Tenant Admin Installs a Plugin from the Catalog](#uc-001-tenant-admin-installs-a-plugin-from-the-catalog)\n+  - [UC-002 Plugin Author Publishes a New Version](#uc-002-plugin-author-publishes-a-new-version)\n+  - [UC-003 Tenant Admin Promotes a New Snapshot](#uc-003-tenant-admin-promotes-a-new-snapshot)\n+  - [UC-004 Silver Plugin Consumes Bronze from a Connector Plugin](#uc-004-silver-plugin-consumes-bronze-from-a-connector-plugin)\n+  - [UC-005 Tenant Admin Authors a Custom Snapshot](#uc-005-tenant-admin-authors-a-custom-snapshot)\n+- [9. Acceptance Criteria](#9-acceptance-criteria)\n+- [10. Dependencies](#10-dependencies)\n+- [11. Assumptions](#11-assumptions)\n+- [12. Risks](#12-risks)\n+- [13. Open Questions](#13-open-questions)\n+\n+<!-- /toc -->\n+\n+## Changelog\n+\n+- **v1.3** (current): Major restructure addressing the second wave of PR #230 review comments (cyberantonz, ~24 comments). Six themes:\n+  1. **Snapshot model replaces per-plugin runtime resolution.** A *snapshot* is an immutable, named, tenant-curated set of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. Conflicts are resolved at snapshot authoring time, never at install. Two snapshots can coexist arbitrarily long as fully independent data planes (own DB scopes, own per-install credentials, own pods). Upgrade = create a new snapshot alongside the old, run both in parallel, the tenant admin promotes when satisfied. Closes the version-hell concern raised on the resolver text (no runtime conflict states reach the tenant) and the multi-version routing concern (each snapshot is its own data plane). Vendor-published snapshots are an *optional convenience*, not a governance gate — tenant authoring is the default.\n+  2. **Per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs.** Every plugin install gets a generated CH user; GRANTs are scoped to its own bronze / silver scope (and, for silver, to the discovered upstream bronze scopes). Default user is forbidden for plugins. Closes data-plane access concerns (plugin doing `SELECT * FROM another_tenant_db`, plugin writing with a forged `tenant_id`, plugin enumerating `system.tables` to find anything not its own).\n+  3. **Plugin-shipped migrations via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANTs and SQL views.** Plugin authors ship migration scripts; the runtime grants the new snapshot's CH user `SELECT` on the prior snapshot's bronze for the duration of the trial period. Migrations may be expressed as `CREATE VIEW`, `MATERIALIZED VIEW`, or full `INSERT FROM SELECT`. Storage cost during a trial is `~1× + delta`, not `2×`. Closes the storage explosion concern.\n+  4. **Trial period is a soft reminder, not a hard timer.** Adaptation is open-ended. The trial timer surfaces a reminder (\"you switched N days ago, retire the old snapshot?\") but does not auto-revert. Removes the contradiction CodeRabbit flagged between UC-003 and the FR.\n+  5. **New Section 5.9 — Security.** Three FRs: image-signing-required (cosign + digest pinning), trust-tiers (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix), baseline-isolation (per-install CH user, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, default ServiceAccount with no K8s API access — applies to ALL plugins regardless of trust tier). Closes supply-chain concerns (image swap, naming collision, mutable tags, abandoned-plugin tampering) and runtime-sandbox concerns (scanners/miners/VPN inside cluster, widget JWT theft).\n+  6. **Priority rebalance.** Previously every release-relevant FR was `p1`; cyberantonz noted \"when everything is p1, nothing is.\" Re-ranked across all sections — `p1` is now reserved for items without which v1 cannot ship safely (~12 FRs); functional features and UX polish are `p2`; future improvements are `p3`.\n+- **v1.2**: Four changes addressing PR #230 review comments.\n+  1. **Rollback is replaced by shadow-deploy.** The plugin system never mutates a live install in place. An upgrade installs the new version alongside the old one, both run for a tenant-configurable trial period against the same inputs, the admin inspects a comparison view, and either PROMOTES the shadow stack (old one is uninstalled; bronze retained) or REJECTS it (shadow stack is uninstalled; tenant returns to the unchanged live stack). Expired trials default to REJECT. There is no distinct rollback operation. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`; UC-003 rewritten to this model (addresses `cyberantonz` review comment).\n+  2. **Each plugin ships its own isolated transform project.** Plugins MUST NOT use dbt `ref()` across plugin boundaries; downstream plugins discover upstream outputs only through the schema-based input contract, and the runtime injects `sources.yml` at run time. No global dbt DAG spans plugins. New FR `cpt-plugin-fr-isolated-transform-project`. Aligns transformation mechanics with the independent-authorship goal already stated for packaging and versioning.\n+  3. **Dependency-graph UI elevated to p1/MUST.** Given non-auto-healing behavior (no legacy mode, no silent rollback) and the shadow-deploy workflow, tenant and instance admins cannot operate the plugin system without a graph view of live + shadow stacks and per-node health. `cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui` now MUST and p1, matching the acceptance criterion that already assumed it.\n+  4. **UC-004 preconditions fixed.** v1.1 refactored silver to schema-based input contracts but missed the UC-004 preconditions paragraph; it still said \"silver declares a dependency on the connector plugin.\" Rewritten to match `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`.\n+- **v1.1**: Clarified four points after product-team review.\n+  1. Connectors are responsible not only for fetching data but also for normalizing it into their declared output schema — bronze is what the connector declares, not the vendor's raw payload.\n+  2. Silver plugins are decoupled from their suppliers by design: a silver plugin declares the input schema it expects plus discovery rules (tags, patterns, or install-time parameters) for locating source tables, and does NOT declare plugin-level dependencies on connectors. Contract matching is schema-based; a new connector that produces the expected shape feeds existing silver without either side knowing about the other. `UNION ALL` across discovered sources is the recommended pattern but not a requirement.\n+  3. The platform does not validate widget input data on the wire — the host app is a data proxy. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent); the plugin runtime executes them before render.\n+  4. Tenant upgrade sovereignty is explicit: the instance admin can set policy (catalog allowlists, block lists, force-uninstall a broken plugin) but **cannot** install or upgrade plugins for a tenant. Upgrade decisions stay with the tenant admin so that a bad upgrade is caught by the party responsible for the data.\n+- **v1.0**: Initial PRD. Established a unified plugin model with composable capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`), tenant-scoped installation, explicit admin-driven dependency resolution, and bronze-as-sacred data policy. Deferred manifest schema, native-protocol details, and authoring SDK to DESIGN/ADR and v2.\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+The Plugin System is the extension mechanism of the Insight platform. It enables first-party teams, enterprise customers, and third-party authors to add new data sources, transformations, and visualizations to a running Insight instance without rebuilding the product and without coordinating a release cycle with the vendor.\n+\n+Every plugin is a self-contained artifact published to an OCI registry. A tenant admin installs it through the product UI, the instance runtime resolves its dependencies, and its behavior is bounded by well-defined capability contracts: `connector` (fetches from a source system and writes out a declared, normalized schema — its bronze), `silver` (discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract and produces cross-source silver tables), and `widget` (renders part of the dashboard UI). A single plugin may declare any combination of these three capabilities.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+Insight today ships with a fixed set of connectors, silver dbt models, and frontend widgets, all embedded in the product repository. Extending the system for a new source — GitLab, Jira, a bespoke internal system — requires a backend PR, frontend PR, dbt model updates, and a full release. A customer that wants to onboard a data source the vendor has not yet built cannot do so; an enterprise customer with internal tooling has no path to express its own metrics without forking the product.\n+\n+Two additional pressures make the current model untenable. First, the ingestion substrate is transitioning from Airbyte to a native orchestration that gives the platform more control, and this transition should not require every connector to re-ship as a hand-written service. Second, the dashboarding surface is moving to a microfrontend model; the set of available widgets must expand at runtime, not at build time.\n+\n+A plugin system decouples the vendor's release cadence from the customer's need to extend the product. It treats every new source, transform, and visualization as an installable unit with a declared contract, making the product extensible in the same way Airbyte makes source-system ingestion extensible, VS Code makes editor features extensible, or dbt packages make transformations extensible — but unified around a single artifact type so the contract across extension points is consistent.\n+\n+**Target Users**:\n+\n+- Tenant admins who need to onboard a data source or add a custom metric without vendor involvement\n+- Insight instance admins who set which plugins a tenant may install and manage the central catalog mirror\n+- Plugin authors (first-party, enterprise, third-party) who build and publish connectors, silver transformations, or widgets\n+- Downstream components (analytics-api, frontend, admin UI) that need a uniform way to discover and interact with installed extensions\n+\n+**Key Problems Solved**:\n+\n+- Customers can extend Insight with their own sources, transforms, and widgets without forking the product\n+- Vendor release cadence is decoupled from customer extension requests\n+- First-party and third-party extensions are subject to the same contract, so quality and support stories do not bifurcate\n+- The transition away from Airbyte does not require re-shipping every connector as product code\n+- Widgets extend the dashboarding surface at runtime rather than requiring a frontend rebuild\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+**Success Criteria**:\n+\n+- A tenant admin can install a first-party connector plugin end-to-end through the product UI in under 10 minutes (baseline: impossible today, requires vendor; target: self-service)\n+- A plugin author can ship a new version of an existing plugin to a customer without a product release (baseline: not supported; target: publish-to-registry + customer `install` is the full path)\n+- At least 3 distinct plugin authors (first-party, 1 enterprise customer, 1 third-party reference) have published working plugins against this contract before v1 is frozen (baseline: 0; target: 3)\n+- 100% of currently bundled ingestion connectors are expressible as plugins on this contract (baseline: all hard-coded in the repo; target: all refactored into the plugin model by end of rollout)\n+- Zero vendor releases required to add, upgrade, or remove a tenant's plugin set (baseline: required today; target: 0)\n+\n+**Capabilities**:\n+\n+- Publish a plugin to any OCI-compatible registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, a customer-owned registry), pinned by digest and signed (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- Compose a plugin from one or more capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — in a single artifact\n+- Author and operate **tenant-owned snapshots** — declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and upgrade by promoting a shadow snapshot validated alongside the live one on real data\n+- Carry the full plugin set, plugin-shipped migrations, and per-install ClickHouse credentials independently per snapshot, so two snapshots run as fully isolated data planes\n+- Discover available plugins through any OCI registry; central catalog is optional convenience\n+- Coexist with the existing Airbyte-based ingestion substrate during the transition period\n+- Modulate plugin capabilities by trust tier (vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned) above a fixed isolation baseline that applies to every plugin\n+- Report per-plugin health, per-snapshot status, and dependency-graph state to instance and tenant admins\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Plugin | A versioned, self-contained extension packaged as an OCI artifact with a declarative manifest. A plugin declares one or more capabilities (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`) and its own dependencies on other plugins and on the platform. |\n+| Plugin ID | A globally unique reverse-DNS identifier for a plugin (for example, `com.cyberfabric.git-gitlab`). Namespace-per-author disambiguates plugins that would otherwise collide by short name. |\n+| Plugin version | A SemVer string attached to each published plugin build. Version comparison, range expressions, and breaking-change semantics follow SemVer 2.0. |\n+| Capability | One of three declared behaviors a plugin may contribute: `connector` (emits bronze data from a source system), `silver` (transforms bronze into unified silver tables), `widget` (renders a UI element). One plugin may declare any combination. |\n+| Manifest | The machine-readable declaration inside a plugin that describes its identity, capabilities, dependencies, input/output data contracts, config schema, and required secrets. Exact schema is defined in DESIGN. |\n+| Central catalog | The Insight-operated registry of curated plugins, serving metadata (identity, versions, compatibility, description) that the instance UI uses to populate the marketplace. A plugin can also be installed by direct URL without appearing in the catalog. |\n+| Plugin install | A record in an Insight instance's database that says \"tenant T has plugin P at version X installed and enabled (or disabled).\" The installed image + manifest is cached locally; its reconciliation into runtime resources is performed by the plugin runtime. |\n+| Plugin runtime | The subsystem inside the Insight instance that reconciles installed plugins into runtime state — fetches images, runs migrations, schedules connectors, applies silver transforms, registers widgets, reports health. |\n+| Connector capability | A plugin capability that fetches data from an external system, normalizes it into its declared output schema, and writes it to the connector's per-install bronze scope. Normalization is the connector's responsibility — a downstream silver plugin expects the connector to have already shaped the data, not raw vendor payloads. The execution protocol may be Airbyte, a future native Insight protocol, or any other protocol the author wants, as long as the running container produces the declared bronze output. |\n+| Silver capability | A plugin capability that discovers bronze tables matching a declared input contract (schema + discovery rules — tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) and produces silver tables with a unified cross-source schema. Silver plugins are decoupled from specific connectors by design: they match on the input contract, not on a connector's plugin identity. The recommended default is a `UNION ALL` across discovered sources followed by incremental consolidation, but plugins MAY skip that step and emit derived tables directly. Execution engine is author's choice — dbt is the reference. |\n+| Widget capability | A plugin capability that provides a runtime-loaded frontend component (microfrontend) for the dashboard surface. A widget declares the data shape it expects; the host application fetches data by reference and passes it to the widget through a stable interface **without validating its shape or content**. Widgets that want input validation ship their own data tests (dbt or equivalent) and the plugin runtime executes them before rendering. |\n+| Bronze data | The data a connector plugin writes to its per-install scope in the connector's declared output schema (not the vendor's raw payload — the connector is responsible for normalizing into its declared shape). Treated as sacred — never deleted or transformed in place by the plugin system; retained so silver and gold layers can always be rebuilt by re-running downstream plugins. |\n+| Snapshot | A tenant-owned, immutable, named declaration of a plugin set: `{plugin_id: version, ...}`. The unit of versioning at tenant scope. A tenant runs one *live* snapshot; *shadow* snapshots may coexist for adaptation. Each snapshot has its own DB scopes (`bronze_<snapshot>_<install>`, `silver_<snapshot>_<install>`), per-install credentials, and pods — two snapshots are fully independent data planes. Snapshots may inherit from a vendor- or instance-published reference snapshot; the unit of authorship is the tenant. |\n+| Trial period | The window during which a freshly-installed shadow snapshot runs alongside the live snapshot. Open-ended; the trial timer surfaces a reminder for the tenant admin to either promote or retire the shadow, but does not auto-promote or auto-revert. |\n+| Trust tier | The provenance level of a plugin: *vendor-signed* (Insight's signature), *instance-signed* (a signature from a key the instance admin registered as trusted), or *unsigned*. Trust tier modulates capabilities ON TOP of baseline isolation, which applies regardless of tier. |\n+| Dependency resolution | The process of determining, for a requested install or upgrade, what other plugin versions must coexist. In v1.3 this happens at *snapshot authoring time* (the snapshot's curator picks a self-consistent set), not at runtime install. The runtime simply applies the snapshot as-given. |\n+| Plugin capability contract | The set of declared inputs (tables, config, secrets) and outputs (tables, widget interface) a capability exposes. Consumers (other plugins, the host app) rely on this contract; authors may add non-breaking fields within a major version. |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Author\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`\n+\n+**Role**: Builds, packages, and publishes plugins. May be a first-party Insight engineer shipping a bundled plugin, an enterprise customer's internal team building a plugin for their tenant, or a third-party publishing to the marketplace. Owns the plugin's manifest, image, tests, and backward compatibility commitments across versions.\n+\n+**Needs**: A clear, stable contract for each capability; a reference plugin to copy; a published manifest schema; a path to test against a local Insight instance before publish; the ability to iterate without vendor gatekeeping for non-catalog plugins.\n+\n+#### Instance Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Operates an Insight instance. Holds an **oversight** role over the plugin surface — not a gatekeeping one. Instance admins **can** (when they choose to) set catalog allowlists/blocklists, force-uninstall a broken or unsafe plugin, and restrict which plugins their tenants may install; this is optional, not required, and the default instance policy allows tenants to self-serve. Instance admins **cannot** install, upgrade, or reconfigure plugins on a tenant's behalf — every change that touches tenant data is initiated by the tenant admin so that the party responsible for the data is the one who approves each change. Owns the instance's overall plugin inventory visibility and the external secret store configuration.\n+\n+**Needs**: Visibility into every installed plugin across tenants with health and version; the ability to block or force-uninstall a specific plugin when safety requires; a dependency graph view showing conflicts and reachability; an audit log of who installed or upgraded what.\n+\n+#### Tenant Admin\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Curates and operates the tenant's plugin surface. Authors *snapshots* — tenant-owned declarations of which plugins at which versions run for the tenant — and decides when to upgrade by initiating a shadow-deploy of a new snapshot. Configures each plugin install (credentials, workspace IDs, sync schedules) through the product UI. Optionally inherits from a vendor-published or instance-published reference snapshot to bootstrap.\n+\n+**Needs**: A marketplace UI listing installable plugins with description, trust tier, and compatibility; a snapshot editor (or \"save as snapshot\" from a current state); a side-by-side comparison view between the live and shadow snapshot during adaptation; a configuration form generated from each plugin's declared config schema; per-plugin status (last sync, error count); an uninstall path that does not orphan bronze data without warning.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Plugin Runtime\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+**Role**: Reconciles the installed-plugin inventory into running state inside the instance. Pulls plugin images from the configured OCI registry, runs the plugin's migrations, schedules connector syncs, applies silver transforms, registers widgets with the frontend, and writes health telemetry back to the inventory.\n+\n+#### Admin API\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+**Role**: Exposes CRUD on plugin installs, invokes the dependency resolver, talks to the OCI registry to fetch manifests for resolution, and serves the marketplace UI and admin dashboards.\n+\n+#### Central Catalog\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+**Role**: Insight-operated service that maintains curated plugin metadata (identity, versions, descriptions, compatibility ranges). Read-only from the instance's perspective. Instance admins mirror or filter it into their local marketplace; tenant admins do not query it directly.\n+\n+#### OCI Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Any OCI-compliant container registry (Docker Hub, ghcr.io, customer-owned). Stores plugin images and manifests. Plugin runtime pulls from here during install and upgrade. Air-gapped instances are expected to mirror images into a customer-owned registry.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+- Every plugin install must be able to reach the OCI registry it was installed from, OR the instance must mirror the image into a reachable registry during install. Air-gapped deployments require the latter.\n+- Plugin containers run in the same Kubernetes cluster as the Insight instance; cluster resource quotas apply per install.\n+- Plugin capabilities that write to the shared data store (`connector`, `silver`) require the platform to provide them with a scoped, per-install credential — they do not use the tenant's own DB credentials.\n+- Widget capability requires the host frontend to support the microfrontend loader that is being adopted for the product; until that lands, widget capability ships as `p2` (see FRs below).\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Plugin artifact model — identity, versioning, manifest, OCI packaging by digest, reverse-DNS naming, SemVer semantics, image signing (cosign / Sigstore)\n+- The three capabilities — `connector`, `silver`, `widget` — and the contract each exposes to consumers\n+- Single-artifact multi-capability plugins (one plugin may mix `connector` + `silver` + `widget` contributions)\n+- Distribution — any OCI-compatible registry; central Insight catalog as optional convenience\n+- Snapshot model — tenant-curated, immutable, named declarations of `{plugin_id: version, ...}`; live + shadow snapshots running in parallel as independent data planes; promote / reject lifecycle; plugin-shipped migrations between snapshots via cross-snapshot SELECT GRANT\n+- Trust tier model — vendor-signed / instance-signed / unsigned, with capability matrix\n+- Baseline isolation — per-install ClickHouse user with scoped GRANTs, NetworkPolicy egress allowlist, ResourceQuota, no K8s API access — applies to every plugin regardless of trust tier\n+- Per-install configuration via a plugin-declared config schema; UI renders form from it\n+- Per-install secret storage via an external secret manager plus a manual-entry fallback\n+- Observability — per-plugin health, sync status, dependency-graph state surfaced to instance admin\n+- Transition-period coexistence with Airbyte — the connector capability can use the Airbyte protocol for connectors that already implement it and reuse the Airbyte destination to write bronze\n+- Bronze data preservation across plugin upgrades and version switches (bronze is sacred; silver/widget configs are re-derivable)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Plugin authoring SDK / CLI (`insight-plugin init|validate|test|package|publish`) — deferred to v2; v1 ships docs + reference plugins\n+- Automatic dependency resolution or auto-upgrade — every resolution is explicit and admin-approved\n+- \"Legacy\" compatibility mode — a plugin cannot be upgraded if its transitive dependencies cannot also be upgraded; the admin decides whether to proceed or stay on the old version\n+- Plugin sandboxing beyond standard Kubernetes container isolation (network policy, resource quota, serviceaccount scoping) — stronger sandboxing (gVisor, WASM) is a follow-up PRD\n+- Rollback of silver and widget data — these layers are re-derivable from bronze; plugin system does not snapshot them. Connectors MAY ship migrations for bronze-schema changes but are not required to\n+- Cross-tenant plugin sharing — each tenant install is independent; future federation is not in v1\n+- Exact manifest schema, exact database schema for the installed-plugin inventory, and the API/protocol shape for native-Insight (non-Airbyte) connectors — these are DESIGN-level and tracked in Open Questions\n+- Soft-delete / GDPR erasure tooling for plugin-produced data — inherited from platform-wide data lifecycle (separate PRD)\n+- Billing, metering, or marketplace payment flow — out of scope for v1 plugin system; any commercial model is a later program\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Packaging and Distribution\n+\n+#### Plugins Ship as OCI Artifacts\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-oci-artifact`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be published as an OCI artifact (container image plus manifest metadata) to an OCI-compatible registry. The plugin system **MUST** accept plugins from Docker Hub, ghcr.io, and any registry that speaks the OCI distribution spec, including customer-owned private registries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Uniform packaging across first-party, enterprise, and third-party plugins; leverages an already-deployed ecosystem; supports air-gapped installations through registry mirroring.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-oci-registry`\n+\n+#### Reverse-DNS Naming and SemVer Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-naming-versioning`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** be identified by a reverse-DNS identifier (`com.acme.git-gitlab`) that is globally unique across authors. Every published build **MUST** carry a SemVer 2.0 version. The plugin system **MUST** reject installs whose ID collides with an already-installed plugin from a different author.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Reverse-DNS avoids short-name collisions between independent authors (two different `git` plugins). SemVer gives dependency resolution a principled ordering and a clear breaking-change signal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+#### Central Catalog and URL Install Coexist\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-catalog-and-url`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** support two install sources: a curated central catalog (metadata served by the Insight-operated catalog service) and ad-hoc URL install (tenant admin supplies an OCI reference for a plugin that is not in the catalog). Instance admins **SHOULD** be able to restrict their tenants to catalog-only plugins via an instance-level policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Central catalog enables discovery, curation, and compatibility testing for the common case. URL install enables customer-authored plugins that are never intended to be published publicly without requiring a catalog gatekeeping workflow.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-central-catalog`\n+\n+#### Declarative Manifest Per Plugin\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-manifest`\n+\n+Every plugin **MUST** ship a machine-readable manifest that declares at minimum: reverse-DNS identity, version, human-readable name, description, author/license, supported platform version range, the capabilities it contributes (`connector`, `silver`, `widget`, or any composition), for each capability its data contract (inputs, outputs), config schema, required secrets, and the plugin's dependencies on other plugins. The manifest schema itself is defined in DESIGN.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The manifest is the contract that dependency resolution, UI form generation, catalog display, and health reporting all depend on. Without a declared manifest, the platform cannot install a plugin safely.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`\n+\n+### 5.2 Snapshot-Based Versioning and Installation\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Installs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-scoped-installs`\n+\n+Every plugin install **MUST** be scoped to a single tenant. Two tenants in the same instance running the same plugin **MUST** get independent installs — independent config, independent bronze scope, independent credentials, independent schedule. Instance admins **MAY** restrict the set of plugins available to tenants through allowlists or trust-tier policies (see Section 5.9), but **MUST NOT** install or activate plugins on a tenant's behalf.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants are the atomic billing and isolation boundary; shared installs break tenant isolation and complicate uninstall.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Is the Unit of Tenant Plugin State\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-as-state`\n+\n+A tenant's plugin state **MUST** be expressed as a SNAPSHOT — an immutable, named, tenant-owned declaration of `{plugin_id: version, ...}` plus the tenant config and secrets that pin each install. A tenant **MUST** have at most one *live* snapshot at any time (the one that feeds user-facing surfaces) and **MAY** have any number of *shadow* snapshots running alongside (for adaptation, comparison, or experimentation).\n+\n+Each snapshot **MUST** carry its own DB scope namespace. Per-install scopes are keyed `bronze_<snapshot>_<install>` / `silver_<snapshot>_<install>` so two snapshots are fully independent data planes — no shared tables, no merging, no cross-snapshot interference at runtime. Per-install ClickHouse credentials follow the same scoping (see `cpt-plugin-fr-per-install-db-user`).\n+\n+A snapshot **MUST** be immutable once published: editing a snapshot means publishing a new snapshot. Tenants iterate by authoring successive snapshots, never by mutating an existing one.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Lifting the unit of versioning from plugin to snapshot eliminates the runtime resolver's worst case — dependency-hell at install time. The snapshot author resolves all conflicts once, at authoring time; the runtime simply applies the snapshot. The same primitive supports \"two versions running in parallel\" (the previous *multi-version* requirement), upgrade, rollback, and per-tenant customization, with one mental model instead of four.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Authorship Is Tenant-First, Vendor-Optional\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-authoring`\n+\n+The default and primary author of a tenant's snapshots is the *tenant admin*. The plugin system **MUST** support tenant authoring as a first-class flow — authoring a snapshot is the same kind of operation as authoring any other tenant configuration, not a privileged system-admin operation.\n+\n+The plugin system **MAY** ALSO support reference snapshots published by other parties (Insight as the vendor; instance admins for an organization-wide internal stack; third parties for community templates). Reference snapshots **MUST NOT** be privileged — they are starting points the tenant admin can clone, modify, or ignore. They are *convenience*, not *governance*. A tenant **MAY** run for the entire lifetime of the instance without ever using a vendor or instance reference snapshot.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Forcing snapshots through a curator gate would replicate the problem the plugin system was built to solve — vendor coupling. Tenant-first authoring keeps the tenant in control of its own composition. Vendor reference snapshots remain valuable as starting points and as documented \"this combination is known to work\" recommendations, but never as the only path.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Tenant Upgrade Sovereignty\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-tenant-sovereignty`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** allow an instance admin to install, upgrade, reconfigure, enable, or otherwise advance a tenant's snapshot on the tenant's behalf. Instance admins retain oversight powers — trust-tier policy (Section 5.9), catalog allowlists / blocklists, force-retirement of a known-malicious or vulnerable plugin from across all tenants — but every additive change to a tenant's snapshot **MUST** be initiated and approved by a tenant admin. Emergency force-retire by an instance admin **MUST** be recorded in the audit log with the reason and **MUST** notify the tenant admin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: If a plugin upgrade corrupts tenant data, the party who approved the upgrade must be the one responsible for the data. The instance admin's job is to protect the instance (block dangerous plugins, contain outages), not to push changes into tenants who have not asked for them.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-instance-admin`\n+\n+#### Shadow-Deploy Snapshot Upgrade with Soft Trial Reminder\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-snapshot-shadow-deploy`\n+\n+When a tenant admin upgrades the live snapshot to a new snapshot, the plugin runtime **MUST** install the new snapshot as a *shadow* alongside the live snapshot rather than replacing it in place. Both snapshots **MUST** run concurrently:\n+\n+- The *live* snapshot continues to feed user-facing surfaces (widgets, analytics-api). No user observes the upgrade until the tenant admin explicitly *promotes* the shadow.\n+- The *shadow* snapshot processes the same connector inputs into its own per-snapshot scopes, on its own schedules, with its own per-install credentials. Each install in the shadow is an independent install — different `install_id` from any same-plugin install in the live snapshot.\n+\n+The plugin runtime **MUST** expose a side-by-side comparison view in the admin UI: row counts, schema diffs, and — where the live and shadow tables match in shape — per-column distributional statistics, so the tenant admin can inspect the change on real data before promoting.\n+\n+The adaptation period is **open-ended**. The system **MUST NOT** auto-promote OR auto-reject when a configurable *trial reminder timer* (default 7 days; tenant-configurable) expires; instead the UI **MUST** raise a non-blocking reminder (\"you switched N days ago, ready to retire the old snapshot?\"). Subsequent reminders escalate by frequency rather than by action — no plugin or snapshot ever silently promotes itself or silently disappears. The tenant admin always retains the choice and the timing.\n+\n+When the tenant admin PROMOTES the shadow snapshot, the runtime **MUST** atomically switch the *live* pointer to the new snapshot. The previous (now non-live) snapshot **MAY** continue running for as long as the tenant admin wants, or **MAY** be retired explicitly. Bronze data is retained until explicit retirement (per `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`).\n+\n+When the tenant admin REJECTS the shadow snapshot, the runtime uninstalls it; shadow bronze is discarded since no downstream consumer relied on it.\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST NOT** expose a distinct \"rollback\" operation. Reverting a previously-promoted snapshot to its predecessor is accomplished by initiating a new shadow-deploy whose target is the older snapshot (still in the tenant's snapshot history): the same shadow / compare / promote loop applies. There is no asymmetry between forward and backward transitions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation matches how real teams validate changes — over weeks of business cycles, not on a 7-day timer. The reminder UX prevents tenants from forgetting an old snapshot indefinitely (which would consume storage and operational attention) without removing tenant control. The \"no auto-action\" rule is the price of `tenant-sovereignty`. Lifting the operation to *snapshot* level (rather than per-plugin shadow) eliminates the question \"if two plugin versions coexist, which feeds analytics-api?\" — only the *live* snapshot does, by definition.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Cross-Snapshot SELECT GRANT for Migration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-cross-snapshot-grant`\n+\n+When a shadow snapshot is created, the plugin runtime **MUST** grant each install in the shadow snapshot `SELECT` privilege on the corresponding install's bronze scope in the live snapshot, **for the duration of the shadow snapshot's existence and only that duration**. The grant **MUST** be revoked when the shadow snapshot is promoted (after the live pointer moves), rejected, or retired. The grant **MUST NOT** include `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, or `ALTER` on the live snapshot's scope — read-only.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Plugin-author-shipped migrations need to read historical bronze from the previous snapshot to populate the new snapshot's scope. Granting time-bounded read access is the minimum viable mechanism: the plugin can author migrations as views or as full-copy SQL (per `cpt-plugin-fr-migration-via-views`), but it is the *runtime* that scopes the grant in time, so an abandoned shadow does not leave a permanent cross-scope hole. Revocation on promotion / rejection / retirement keeps the security surface minimal.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Inventory in Instance Database\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-inventory-storage`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** persist the per-tenant snapshot inventory (snapshot ids, plugin set, status — `live`, `shadow`, `retired` — created-at, who-by, the audit log of authoring and promotion events) in the instance database. The inventory **MUST** survive restarts, `helm upgrade` of the Insight umbrella, and reconciliation by the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The inventory is the source of truth about a tenant's extension surface. Storing it in the instance DB (not in Kubernetes CRDs) keeps it co-located with tenant data, enables transactional admin-API operations, and simplifies backup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-admin-api`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Retired Snapshot Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-retired-snapshot-reporting`\n+\n+The plugin system **SHOULD** identify snapshots that have been non-live for longer than a configurable threshold (default 30 days) and **SHOULD** surface them to the tenant admin as candidates for retirement. The system **MUST NOT** retire them automatically.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Open-ended adaptation is intentional, but old snapshots that nobody is comparing against waste storage and cognitive load. Surfacing them keeps the tenant in control of cleanup.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.3 Connector Capability\n+\n+#### Connector Normalizes Source Data to a Declared Output Schema\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-output-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `connector` capability **MUST** declare its output as a schema (tables + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** guarantee that every row it writes to bronze conforms to that declared schema. The connector **MUST** perform whatever reshaping is required to normalize raw source payloads into the declared shape (pivoting, renaming, type coercion, de-nesting, timestamp normalization, etc.) — it is the connector's responsibility, not the downstream silver plugin's, to absorb the vendor's data quirks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from vendor idiosyncrasies is the architectural intent. If every silver plugin had to parse every connector's raw output, adding a new connector would require changing every silver plugin that consumes it. Pushing normalization into the connector means silver plugins match on shape alone and a new source becomes a drop-in.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Writes to a Per-Install Bronze Scope\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-bronze-scope`\n+\n+A connector capability **MUST** write into a per-install bronze scope in the data store, isolated from every other connector install (including other installs of the same plugin for the same tenant at different versions). Tables, schemas, and any other data-store namespace the connector uses **MUST** be derivable from the `(tenant_id, plugin_id, plugin_version, install_id)` tuple so scopes do not collide.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the durable record of what the connector produced after normalization. Isolating scopes by install means uninstalls are safe (delete the scope), migrations between versions are safe (write to a new scope, validate, then switch), and multi-version coexistence works.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Bronze Data Is Preserved Across Upgrades\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-bronze-preserved`\n+\n+The plugin system **MUST** preserve a connector install's bronze data across plugin upgrades unless the admin explicitly requests its deletion. Connector plugins **MAY** ship forward-only migrations for bronze schema evolution; silver and widget layers are always re-derivable and **MUST NOT** require any such migration.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Bronze is the only layer not reconstructible from other layers. Losing it means losing history. Keeping it sacred is the single most important data-durability commitment of the plugin system.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Connector Execution Protocol Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-connector-protocol-flexibility`\n+\n+A connector-capability plugin **MAY** be executed using any protocol its author chooses, as long as the running container produces bronze tables in its declared per-install scope. The plugin system **MUST** support the Airbyte source protocol (so that existing Airbyte connectors can be wrapped as Insight plugins with minimal rework) and **MUST** be extensible to other protocols — for example, Singer tap, or a future native Insight connector protocol — as they are adopted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Requires zero migration cost for the existing Airbyte connector fleet and avoids locking Insight into a single execution contract for the next decade. The platform's job is to give the connector a scope to write to and collect its output; how it produces that output is a plugin-internal concern.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Airbyte Connectors Reuse the Airbyte Destination for Bronze\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-airbyte-destination-reuse`\n+\n+For connector plugins that use the Airbyte source protocol, the plugin system **MUST** support using the existing Airbyte ClickHouse destination to write bronze, rather than reimplementing the destination inside the plugin runtime.\n+\n+**Rationale**: The Airbyte destination is battle-tested for the types and quirks of ClickHouse ingestion. Reimplementing it for v1 is busywork and a regression risk.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+### 5.4 Silver Capability\n+\n+#### Silver Declares an Input Contract, Not Supplier Plugins\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-input-contract`\n+\n+A plugin that declares the `silver` capability **MUST** declare its inputs as a contract consisting of (a) the expected schema (tables, columns, types) of each input and (b) discovery rules (tags, name patterns, or install-time parameters) by which the plugin runtime locates source tables in the data store. The silver plugin **MUST NOT** declare plugin-level dependencies on specific connector plugins for its data sources — compatibility is schema-based, not plugin-identity-based.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decoupling silver from specific connectors is the point of the capability boundary. A tenant that adds a third connector (say `git-bitbucket`) producing the expected shape should feed the existing `silver-git` plugin without `silver-git` being aware of it. A connector that upgrades to a new major version without changing the output schema should not trigger a silver-plugin upgrade. Schema-level contracts give this flexibility; plugin-ID dependencies would not.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Silver Produces Declared Output Tables\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`\n+\n+A silver-capability plugin **MUST** declare its output silver tables (schema + columns + types) in its manifest and **MUST** produce them in a per-install silver scope. The recommended default processing pattern is `UNION ALL` across all discovered input sources with incremental consolidation, but the plugin **MAY** skip that step and emit pre-aggregated or derived tables directly; the choice is internal to the plugin.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A silver plugin is in essence a function `(discovered bronze) → silver*`. Declaring the output explicitly is what allows widgets and other downstream consumers to rely on it. Leaving the internal pipeline shape to the plugin author lets complex cases (algorithmic enrichment, cross-entity joins, ML scoring) fit the same contract as simple union-based silvers.\n+\n+**Actors**: `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-author`, `cpt-plugin-actor-plugin-runtime`\n+\n+#### Transformation Engine Is Author's Choice\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-engine-flexibility`\n+\n+Silver capability **MUST NOT** constrain the plugin to a specific transformation engine. dbt is the reference engine for the first-party silver plugins; authors **MAY** use Rust, Python, or any other language, provided the container reads the declared bronze inputs and writes the declared silver outputs.","path":"docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","original_commit_id":"e2f1cd71f36ccf48fb191b0ecaf70f2a17043ae4","user":{"login":"cyberantonz","id":270971350,"node_id":"U_kgDOECax1g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/270971350?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberantonz","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cyberantonz/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**[MEDIUM — M-26] Zombie scratch tables from \"any language\" plugins not GC'''d**\n\nRust / Python plugins with `INSERT/SELECT/ALTER` GRANT can `CREATE TABLE` for scratch / temp / staging purposes within their scope. The plugin system tracks only the *declared outputs* (per `cpt-plugin-fr-silver-transform`). Scratch tables are invisible to the runtime.\n\nOn uninstall / snapshot retirement, if the per-install scope IS its own database namespace, `DROP DATABASE` cleans up everything (good). If the scope is shared with other tables in the same database, cleanup may selectively drop only declared tables and leak undeclared ones (zombie tables that nobody knows about, growing forever).\n\n(This depends on whether schema-per-install is the chosen isolation mechanism — see H-12 for the contradiction with the `tenant_id` FR.)\n\n**Suggested fix:** Per-install scope MUST be its own database (not just a table-name prefix) so `DROP DATABASE` cleans up everything; OR a declare-or-drop policy: any table not in declared outputs is dropped at end-of-run. Ties to H-12 (pick the isolation mechanism cleanly).","created_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:05Z","updated_at":"2026-04-27T11:13:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146811327","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811327"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/230#discussion_r3146811327"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/230"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/3146811327/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":459,"original_line":459,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":459,"position":459,"subject_type":"line"}],"source_reviews":[{"id":4169449285,"node_id":"PRR_kwDORRWv8s74hL9F","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn `@docs/domain/plugins/specs/PRD.md`:\n- Line 691: The acceptance criterion currently makes the dependency-graph UI\nmandatory (checked box) but the feature requirement cpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui\nis defined as p2/SHOULD; resolve the conflict by either (A) changing the\nacceptance item \"- [ ] An instance admin can view the dependency graph...\" to\nremain optional/SHOULD (remove implication of release gating) or (B) elevating\ncpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui to p1/MUST so the acceptance becomes a hard\nrequirement; update the wording and the priority/strength value for\ncpt-plugin-fr-dep-graph-ui to match whichever option you choose so both the\nacceptance criterion and the FR are aligned.\n- Line 739: The table row containing the CLI example \"insight-plugin\ninit|validate|test|package|publish\" is breaking the Markdown table because the\npipe characters are unescaped; update that cell by escaping each pipe (e.g.,\nreplace | with \\|) or by using an HTML entity (&#124;) or wrapping the entire\nCLI string in an inline <code> tag so the pipes are treated as literal\ncharacters; target the table row sta